globalThis.ExamBank={"meta": {"title": "DP-800-BATERIA-4", "pages": 234, "questions": 101, "passingScore": 700, "minutes": 120, "totalPoints": 177, "scoring": "Un punto por subapartado en drag-and-drop, desplegables y triple Yes/No; un punto por pregunta de elección (conjunto exacto en múltiple). Nota = puntos obtenidos / puntos posibles × 1000.", "sourceSha256": "65a3e17ca6e82bb1e5dd7cac0c291cbfca84abd7e5ab5691bf1c741651a029b8", "types": {"binary": 8, "drag": 18, "dropdown": 16, "multiple": 4, "single": 45, "yesno": 10}}, "questions": [{"id": 1, "pages": [2, 10], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p003-001", "p003-002", "p004-003", "p006-004"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "SELECT f.FeedbackId, f.VehicleId, {{0}},\n EDIT_DISTANCE_SIMILARITY(JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text'), @KnownIssueDescription) AS SimilarityScore\nFROM dbo.CustomerFeedback f\nWHERE {{1}}\nORDER BY {{2}} DESC;", "pools": [["CONTAINS(FeedbackJson, @Keyword)", "EDIT_DISTANCE(JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.details.comment'), @Keyword) < 3", "EDIT_DISTANCE(JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text'), @Keyword) < 3", "JSON_QUERY(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text'), @KnownIssueDescription) AS FeedbackText", "JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text') AS FeedbackText", "SimilarityScore"], ["CONTAINS(FeedbackJson, @Keyword)", "EDIT_DISTANCE(JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.details.comment'), @Keyword) < 3", "EDIT_DISTANCE(JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text'), @Keyword) < 3", "JSON_QUERY(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text'), @KnownIssueDescription) AS FeedbackText", "JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text') AS FeedbackText", "SimilarityScore"], ["CONTAINS(FeedbackJson, @Keyword)", "EDIT_DISTANCE(JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.details.comment'), @Keyword) < 3", "EDIT_DISTANCE(JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text'), @Keyword) < 3", "JSON_QUERY(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text'), @KnownIssueDescription) AS FeedbackText", "JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text') AS FeedbackText", "SimilarityScore"]], "correct": [4, 0, 5], "image": 4, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Case Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment -\nAzure Environment -\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupportTickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); FleetId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(256))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(2000)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusLastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n  \"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill.\",\n  \"category\": \"Battery\",\n  \"metadata\": {\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\", \"device\": \"Android\", \"language\": \"en-GB\"}\n}"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso is deploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the\nfollowing:\nAI workloads -\nVector search -\nModernized API access -\nRetrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines\nSometimes the ingestion pipeline fails due to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads.\nThe engineers at Contoso report that the following dashboard query runs slowly."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "SELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "You review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports.\nSecurity Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following security requirements:\nRestrict the support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email\naddresses and phone numbers.\nEnforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which they are assigned. The\nanalysts can be assigned to multiple fleets.\nDatabase Performance and Requirements\nContoso identifies the following telemetry requirements:\nTelemetry data must be stored in a partitioned table.\nTelemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention operations. latitude,\nlongitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change.\nAvoid recursive updates.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nContoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following requirements:\nEmbeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables.\nEmbeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\nChunking must preserve semantic coherence.\nHybrid search must combine the following:\nVector similarity -\nKeyword filtering or boosting -\nDevelopment Requirements -\nThe development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot and will\nretrieve live metadata from the databases.\nContoso identifies the following requirements for querying data in the FeedbackJson column of the\nCustomerFeedback table:\nExtract the customer feedback text from the JSON document.\nFilter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword.\nCalculate a fuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description.\nOrder the results by similarity score, with the highest score first.\nYou need to meet the development requirements for the FeedbackJson column.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL query? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer\narea.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p008-005.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ01 T01\nCorrect Answers:\nBox 1 (SELECT clause): JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text') AS FeedbackText\nBox 2 (WHERE clause): CONTAINS(FeedbackJson, @Keyword)\nBox 3 (ORDER BY clause): SimilarityScore\nExplanation:\nBox 1 — Extracting the Feedback Text:\nEl requisito es extraer el texto del feedback del cliente desde el documento JSON — un único valor de texto\nescalar. JSON_VALUE está diseñado exactamente para eso: extrae un valor escalar de una cadena JSON\nen una ruta determinada. JSON_QUERY, en cambio, extrae un objeto o un array (no un escalar), y solo\nacepta dos argumentos (expression, path) — la opción que ofrece un tercer argumento\n(@KnownIssueDescription) no es una sintaxis válida para JSON_QUERY en absoluto, lo cual la descarta\ntanto por motivos semánticos como sintácticos.\nBox 2 — Filtering by Keyword:\nEl case study establece explícitamente que la columna FeedbackJson ya tiene un índice de texto completo\n(full-text index), y el requisito es filtrar las filas donde el texto JSON contiene una palabra clave — una\nbúsqueda de contención literal, no una coincidencia aproximada/difusa. CONTAINS es el predicado de Full-\nText Search creado específicamente para aprovechar un índice de texto completo en este tipo de\nbúsqueda por palabra clave, y es mucho más eficiente que las opciones con EDIT_DISTANCE, las cuales\nmiden la distancia de edición a nivel de carácter (una comparación de errores tipográficos difusa) en lugar\nde contención literal de una palabra clave — y una de esas dos opciones de EDIT_DISTANCE además\napunta a la ruta JSON incorrecta ($.details.comment en vez de $.text), lo cual la hace doblemente\nincorrecta.\nBox 3 — Ordering by Similarity Score:\nEl requisito es ordenar los resultados por puntuación de similitud, con la más alta primero. SimilarityScore\nes el alias ya definido en la lista del SELECT para EDIT_DISTANCE_SIMILARITY(...), y dado que esa\nfunción devuelve un valor de 0 a 100 donde 100 = mayor similitud, ordenar por SimilarityScore DESC (el\nDESC ya viene fijo en la plantilla) coloca correctamente la mayor similitud primero..\nReferences:\nBox 1 — JSON_VALUE(f.FeedbackJson, '$.text') AS FeedbackText\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/json-value-transact-sql\n\"Use the JSON_VALUE syntax to extract a scalar value from a JSON string... To extract an object or an\narray from a JSON string instead of a scalar value, see JSON_QUERY.\"\nBox 2 — CONTAINS(FeedbackJson, @Keyword)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/contains-transact-sql AND https://learn.microsoft.com/en-\nus/sql/relational-databases/search/full-text-search\nBox 3 — (ORDER BY clause) → SimilarityScore\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/edit-distance-similarity-transact-sql — \"Calculates a\nsimilarity value ranging from 0 (indicating no match) to 100 (indicating full match).\""}], "points": 3}, {"id": 2, "pages": [10, 12], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p010-006"], "type": "drag", "template": "CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE dbo.usp_CreateOrder\n @CustomerId int, @Amount decimal(10,2), @OrderId int OUTPUT\nAS\nBEGIN\n SET NOCOUNT ON;\n BEGIN TRY\n  BEGIN TRANSACTION;\n  INSERT INTO dbo.Orders(CustomerId, Amount, CreatedAt)\n  VALUES (@CustomerId, @Amount, SYSUTCDATETIME());\n  {{0}};\n  COMMIT TRANSACTION;\n END TRY\n BEGIN CATCH\n  {{1}};\n  THROW;\n END CATCH\nEND", "pools": [["BEGIN CATCH", "IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION", "RAISERROR('CreateOrder failed', 16, 1)", "ROLLBACK TRANSACTION", "SET @OrderId = SCOPE_IDENTITY()", "THROW"], ["BEGIN CATCH", "IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION", "RAISERROR('CreateOrder failed', 16, 1)", "ROLLBACK TRANSACTION", "SET @OrderId = SCOPE_IDENTITY()", "THROW"]], "correct": [4, 1], "image": 6, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named dbo.Orders.\nYou have an application that calls a stored procedure named dbo.usp_CreateOrder to insert rows into dbo.\nOrders.\nWhen an insert fails, the application receives inconsistent error details.\nYou need to implement error handling to ensure that any failures inside the procedure abort the transaction\nand return a consistent error to the caller.\nHow should you complete the stored procedure? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct\ntargets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar\nbetween panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p011-007.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ02 T01\nBloque TRY (antes de COMMIT TRANSACTION): SET @OrderId = SCOPE_IDENTITY()\nBloque CATCH (antes de THROW): IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION\nEste orden garantiza que el OUTPUT se asigne dentro de la transacción exitosa, y que cualquier fallo\nrevierta la transacción de forma segura antes de propagar el error original y consistente al llamador\nmediante THROW.\n---\nJustificación Técnica y Arquitectura\nEl patrón de manejo de errores transaccional recomendado por Microsoft para procedimientos\nalmacenados en Azure SQL Database:\nBEGIN TRY\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\nINSERT INTO dbo.Orders(CustomerId, Amount, CreatedAt)\nVALUES (@CustomerId, @Amount, SYSUTCDATETIME());\nSET @OrderId = SCOPE_IDENTITY();\nCOMMIT TRANSACTION;\nEND TRY\nBEGIN CATCH\nIF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;\nTHROW;\nEND CATCH\n- SCOPE_IDENTITY() debe ejecutarse inmediatamente después del INSERT y ANTES del COMMIT, ya\nque captura el último valor de identidad generado dentro del scope actual del procedimiento (a diferencia\nde @@IDENTITY o IDENT_CURRENT).\n- IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION en el bloque CATCH es el patrón oficial porque\nvalida si la transacción sigue abierta antes de intentar el rollback, evitando el error 3903 en transacciones\nya marcadas como \"doomed\" (XACT_STATE() = -1).\n- THROW (sin parámetros) relanza la excepción original preservando número de error, severidad, estado y\nmensaje exacto, cumpliendo el requisito de \"error consistente al llamador\".\n---\nDesglose de Opciones Incorrectas\nOpción BEGIN CATCH:\nYa es parte fija de la plantilla del área de respuesta (etiqueta estructural), no es un valor variable para los\nhuecos.\nOpción RAISERROR('CreateOrder failed', 16, 1):\nGenera un mensaje genérico, perdiendo el número de error, la línea y el contexto original de la excepción.\nContradice el requisito de error consistente.\nOpción ROLLBACK TRANSACTION (sin el IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0):\nUn ROLLBACK incondicional es inseguro: si la transacción ya fue revertida automáticamente (doomed\ntransaction), lanza el error 3903 y enmascara el error real.\nOpción THROW (usada en el hueco del bloque TRY):\nNo aplica dentro de TRY sin una excepción activa capturada. THROW sin parámetros solo es válido dentro\nde un bloque CATCH.\nOpción SET @OrderId = SCOPE_IDENTITY() (usada en el hueco del CATCH):\nEs funcionalmente inútil en el bloque CATCH, ya que en ese punto la inserción falló y no existe un valor de\nidentidad válido que asignar.\n---\nReferencias Oficiales Microsoft Learn\n- TRY...CATCH (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/try-catch-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\n- Uncommittable transactions y XACT_STATE:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/try-catch-transact-sql?view=sql-server-\nver17#uncommittable-transactions-and-xact_state\n- THROW (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/throw-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\n- Transaction locking and row versioning guide:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/sql-server-transaction-locking-and-row-versioning-\nguide?view=sql-server-ver17#transaction-basics\nAdvertencia sobre bancos de preguntas: algunas versiones de este reactivo en ExamTopics votan por\nROLLBACK TRANSACTION sin validar @@TRANCOUNT. La documentación oficial confirma que ese\npatrón es incorrecto en transacciones \"doomed\", por lo que la respuesta correcta es la que incluye IF\n@@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION."}], "points": 2}, {"id": 3, "pages": [12, 13], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Your team is developing an Azure SQL dataset solution from a locally cloned GitHub repository by using\nMicrosoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot Chat.\nYou need to disable the GitHub Copilot repository-level instructions for yourself without affecting other\nusers.\nWhat should you do?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "From Visual Studio Code, modify your GitHub Copilot Chat user settings."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Add a -debug flag when you start the GitHub Copilot Chat extension."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Delete .github/copilot-instructions.md."}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ03 T01\nCorrect Answer:\nFrom Visual Studio Code, modify your GitHub Copilot Chat user settings.\nExplanation:\nSÍ es la opción A, porque en la documentación menciona específicamente que se pueden modificar desde\nel menú de opciones de Visual Studio Code.\nNO es la opción B, porque la documentación no menciona ningún flag de arranque como mecanismo para\ncontrolar el uso de instrucciones personalizadas.\nNO es la opción C, porque \".github/copilot-instructions.md\" es un archivo compartido. Eliminarlo no solo\nafectaría al usuario actual.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/visualstudio/ide/copilot-chat-context?view=visualstudio (sección “Use a .github/\ncopilot-instructions.md file”)"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 4, "pages": [13, 16], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains the following SQL graph tables:\nA NODE table named dbo.Person -\nAn EDGE table named dbo.Knows -\nEach row in dbo.Person contains the following columns:\nPersonID (int)\nDisplayName (nvarchar(100))\nYou need to use a MATCH operator and exactly two directed Knows relationships to return the PersonID\nand DisplayName of people that are reachable from the person identified by an input parameter named\n@StartPersonId.\nWhich Transact-SQL query should you use?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "SELECT p2.PersonId, @StartPersonId\nFROM dbo.Person AS p1, dbo.Knows AS k1, dbo.Person AS p2, dbo.Knows AS k2, dbo.Person AS p3\nWHERE p1.DisplayName = p2.DisplayName\nAND MATCH(p1-(k1)->p2-(k2)->p3);"}, {"id": "B", "text": "SELECT p3.PersonId, p3.DisplayName\nFROM dbo.Person AS p1\nJOIN dbo.Knows AS k1 ON 1 = 1\nJOIN dbo.Person AS p2 ON 1 = 1\nJOIN dbo.Knows AS k2 ON 1 = 1\nJOIN dbo.Person AS p3 ON 1 = 1\nWHERE p1.PersonId = @StartPersonId\nAND MATCH(p3<-(k2)-p2<-(k1)-p1);"}, {"id": "C", "text": "SELECT p3.PersonId, p3.DisplayName\nFROM dbo.Person AS p1, dbo.Knows AS k1, dbo.Person AS p2, dbo.Knows AS k2, dbo.Person AS p3\nWHERE p1.PersonId = @StartPersonId\nAND MATCH(p1-(k1)->p2)\nAND MATCH(p2-(k2)->p3);"}, {"id": "D", "text": "SELECT p3.PersonId, p3.DisplayName\nFROM dbo.Person AS p1, dbo.Knows AS k1, dbo.Person AS p2, dbo.Knows AS k2, dbo.Person AS p3\nWHERE p1.PersonId = @StartPersonId\nAND MATCH(p1-(k1)->p2-(k2)->p3);"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ04 T01\nCorrect Answer:\nSELECT p3.PersonId, p3.DisplayName\nFROM dbo.Person AS p1, dbo.Knows AS k1, dbo.Person AS p2,\ndbo.Knows AS k2, dbo.Person AS p3\nWHERE p1.PersonId = @StartPersonId\nAND MATCH(p1-(k1)->p2-(k2)->p3);\nExplanation:\nLa consulta correcta utiliza un único MATCH() para representar un recorrido de exactamente dos relaciones\ndirigidas Knows:\np1 --k1--> p2 --k2--> p3\np1 representa la persona inicial y se limita mediante:\np1.PersonId = @StartPersonId\nLa primera relación:\np1-(k1)->p2\nindica que la persona inicial conoce a p2.\nLa segunda relación:\np2-(k2)->p3\nindica que p2 conoce a p3.\nPor tanto, p3 representa las personas situadas exactamente a dos saltos de la persona indicada mediante\n@StartPersonId. Finalmente:\nSELECT p3.PersonId, p3.DisplayName\ndevuelve precisamente las dos columnas solicitadas.\nMicrosoft muestra prácticamente este mismo patrón para encontrar un \"friend of a friend\":\nPerson1 -(friend)-> Person2 -(friend2)-> Person3\nes decir, dos relaciones dirigidas consecutivas dentro del mismo MATCH().\nPor qué no las otras opciones:\nSELECT p2.PersonId, @StartPersonId\nFROM dbo.Person AS p1, dbo.Knows AS k1, dbo.Person AS p2, dbo.Knows AS k2, dbo.Person AS p3\nWHERE p1.DisplayName = p2.DisplayName\nAND MATCH(p1-(k1)->p2-(k2)->p3);\nAunque contiene el patrón de dos saltos, no utiliza @StartPersonId para localizar a p1. En su lugar\ncompara p1.DisplayName = p2.DisplayName. Además, devuelve p2.PersonId y @StartPersonId, no p3.\nPersonId y p3.DisplayName. Por tanto, no satisface el resultado solicitado.\nSELECT p3.PersonId, p3.DisplayName\nFROM dbo.Person AS p1\nJOIN dbo.Knows AS k1 ON 1 = 1\nJOIN dbo.Person AS p2 ON 1 = 1\nJOIN dbo.Knows AS k2 ON 1 = 1\nJOIN dbo.Person AS p3 ON 1 = 1\nWHERE p1.PersonId = @StartPersonId\nAND MATCH(p3<-(k2)-p2<-(k1)-p1);\nEl patrón gráfico escrito al revés, MATCH(p3<-(k2)-p2<-(k1)-p1) no es el problema; Microsoft indica que un\npatrón dirigido puede escribirse desde cualquiera de los extremos. Por ejemplo, Person1-(friend)->Person2\nes equivalente a Person2<-(friend)-Person1.\nEl problema es que esta opción introduce las tablas mediante:\nJOIN ... ON 1 = 1\ny después usa esos aliases en MATCH. SQL Server documenta específicamente el error 13920: los\nidentificadores usados en MATCH no pueden estar asociados a JOIN o APPLY.\nSELECT p3.PersonId, p3.DisplayName\nFROM dbo.Person AS p1, dbo.Knows AS k1, dbo.Person AS p2, dbo.Knows AS k2, dbo.Person AS p3\nWHERE p1.PersonId = @StartPersonId\nAND MATCH(p1-(k1)->p2)\nAND MATCH(p2-(k2)->p3);\nDescribe correctamente las dos relaciones, pero utiliza dos operadores MATCH() independientes:\nMATCH(p1-(k1)->p2)\nAND MATCH(p2-(k2)->p3)\nEl requisito pide utilizar un operador MATCH() con exactamente dos relaciones dirigidas. La sintaxis\napropiada es expresar todo el camino de dos saltos dentro de un único patrón:\nMATCH(p1-(k1)->p2-(k2)->p3)\nMicrosoft utiliza exactamente esta construcción para los recorridos multi-hop.\nPor ello, la consulta correcta es la que realiza:\n@StartPersonId → p1 → k1 → p2 → k2 → p3\n↓\nPersonId + DisplayName\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/queries/match-sql-graph?view=sql-server-ver17\nDocumenta la sintaxis de MATCH, la dirección de las aristas y el patrón para recorrer varios saltos. Se\naplica a SQL Server, Azure SQL Database, Azure SQL Managed Instance y SQL Database in Microsoft\nFabric.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/graphs/sql-graph-overview?view=sql-server-ver17\nExplica las tablas NODE, EDGE y cómo MATCH permite realizar navegación multi-hop mediante SQL\nGraph. También documenta el soporte de SQL Graph en SQL Database de Microsoft Fabric.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/errors-events/database-engine-events-and-\nerrors-13000-to-13999?view=sql-server-ver17\nEl error 13920 confirma que JOIN y APPLY no se admiten para identificadores utilizados dentro de una\ncláusula MATCH."}], "points": 1}, {"id": 5, "pages": [16, 16], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains a column named Payload. Payload stores\ncustomer data in JSON documents that have the following format.\ntext\n{\n\"date\": \"2020-01-25\",\n\"customer_email\": \"user@contoso.com\",\n...\n}\nData analysis shows that some customers have subaddressing in their email address, for example, user1\n+promo@contoso.com.\nYou need to return a normalized email value that removes the subaddressing, for example, user1\n+promo@contoso.com must be normalized to user1@contoso.com.\nWhich Transact-SQL expression should you use?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "REGEXP_REPLACE(JSON_VALUE(Payload, ‘$.customer_email’), ‘\\+.*$’, ‘’)"}, {"id": "B", "text": "REGEXP_SUBSTR(JSON_VALUE(Payload, ‘$.customer_email’), ‘^[^+]+@.*$=’)"}, {"id": "C", "text": "REGEXP_REPLACE(JSON_VALUE(Payload, ‘$.customer_email’), ‘\\+.*@’, ‘@’)"}, {"id": "D", "text": "REGEXP_REPLACE(JSON_VALUE(Payload, ‘$.customer_email’), ‘\\+.*’, ‘’)"}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ05 T01\nCorrect Answer : REGEXP_REPLACE(JSON_VALUE(Payload, '$.customer_email'), '+.*@', '@')\nExplanation:La opción correcta es REGEXP_REPLACE(JSON_VALUE(Payload, '$.customer_email'), '+.\n*@', '@') porque busca desde el signo + hasta el @ (incluido) y lo sustituye por un único @. De esta forma\nse elimina solo el subaddressing y se mantiene intacta la parte local del email junto con el dominio,\nobteniendo exactamente user1@contoso.com.\nREGEXP_REPLACE(JSON_VALUE(Payload, '$.customer_email'), '\\+.*$', '') es incorrecta: el patrón '\\+.*$'\nelimina desde el + hasta el final de la cadena, por lo que se pierde el dominio y queda únicamente user1.\nREGEXP_SUBSTR(JSON_VALUE(Payload, '.customer_email'), '^[^+]+@.*') es incorrecta: el patrón '^[^+]\n+@.*$' exige que no exista ningún + antes del @. Cuando hay subaddressing el patrón no hace match y la\nfunción devuelve NULL.\nREGEXP_REPLACE(JSON_VALUE(Payload, '$.customer_email'), '+.*', '') es incorrecta: el patrón '\\+.*'\nelimina desde el + en adelante (incluyendo el dominio), dejando solo user1.\nReferences:\nREGEXP_REPLACE (Transact-SQL) – Microsoft Learn (SQL database in Microsoft Fabric): https://learn.\nmicrosoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/regexp-replace-transact-sql\nREGEXP_SUBSTR (Transact-SQL) – Microsoft Learn: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/\nregexp-substr-transact-sql\nJSON_VALUE (Transact-SQL) – Microsoft Learn: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/json-\nvalue-transact-sql\nRegular Expressions Functions (Transact-SQL) – Microsoft Learn: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-\nsql/functions/regular-expressions-functions-transact-sql"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 6, "pages": [16, 19], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p017-008"], "type": "drag", "template": "CREATE PROCEDURE dbo.usp_SearchOrders\n @StartDate date\nAS\nBEGIN\n SET NOCOUNT ON;\n DECLARE @EndDate date;\n SET @EndDate = {{0}};\n SELECT o.CreateDate, o.OrderId, o.ShipDate\n FROM dbo.Orders AS o\n WHERE o.CreateDate >= {{1}}\n AND o.CreateDate < {{2}};\nEND;\nGO", "pools": [["@EndDate", "@StartDate", "CONVERT(char(10), CreateDate, 121)", "CONVERT(date, @StartDate)", "DATEADD(day, 1, @StartDate)", "GETDATE()"], ["@EndDate", "@StartDate", "CONVERT(char(10), CreateDate, 121)", "CONVERT(date, @StartDate)", "DATEADD(day, 1, @StartDate)", "GETDATE()"], ["@EndDate", "@StartDate", "CONVERT(char(10), CreateDate, 121)", "CONVERT(date, @StartDate)", "DATEADD(day, 1, @StartDate)", "GETDATE()"]], "correct": [4, 1, 0], "image": 8, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that containS a table named dbo_Orders. db0_Orders containS a col\numn named CreateDate that Stores Order\ncreation dates.\nYou need to create a procedure that filters Orders by CreateDate for a single calendar day. The solution\nmust be SARGabIe.\nHOW Should complete the Transact-SQL cade? TO answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct\ntargets. Each value may be used once,\nmore than once, or not at all _ You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to View content.\nNOTE: Each correct se' ection is worlh one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p017-009.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ06 T01\n1. Analisis del Escenario\nSe necesita un procedimiento almacenado que filtre la tabla dbo.Orders por la columna CreateDate para un\nunico dia calendario.\nLa solucion debe ser SARGable (Search ARGument Able), es decir, permitir el uso eficiente de indices sin\naplicar funciones sobre la columna.\nDominio evaluado: Diseno y desarrollo de soluciones de base de datos (T-SQL avanzado y optimizacion de\nconsultas).\n2. Solucion Correcta\nParametro de entrada: @StartDate date\nCalculo del limite superior: SET @EndDate = DATEADD(day, 1, @StartDate)\nFiltro SARGable:\nWHERE o.CreateDate >= @StartDate\nAND o.CreateDate < @EndDate\nCodigo completo correcto:\nCREATE PROCEDURE dbo.usp_SearchOrders\n@StartDate date\nAS\nBEGIN\nSET NOCOUNT ON;\nDECLARE @EndDate date;\nSET @EndDate = DATEADD(day, 1, @StartDate);\nSELECT o.CreateDate,\no.OrderId,\no.ShipDate\nFROM dbo.Orders AS o\nWHERE o.CreateDate >= @StartDate\nAND o.CreateDate < @EndDate;\nEND;\n3. Justificacion Tecnica\nPara que una prediccion sea SARGable, nunca se debe aplicar una funcion sobre la columna (CreateDate).\nAl declarar el parametro como date y calcular el dia siguiente con DATEADD(day, 1, @StartDate), se\nobtiene un rango semi-abierto:\n= @StartDate → incluye desde las 00:00:00 del dia solicitado\n< @EndDate → excluye el dia siguiente\nEste patron permite al optimizador de Azure SQL realizar un Index Seek (si existe un indice en CreateDate)\nen lugar de un Index Scan, garantizando rendimiento predecible incluso con grandes volumenes de datos.\n4. Descarte de Alternativas\nCONVERT(char(10), CreateDate, 121) o CONVERT(date, CreateDate) → Aplican una funcion sobre la\ncolumna → el predicado deja de ser SARGable y fuerza un scan.\nGETDATE() → No tiene relacion con el filtrado de un dia concreto proporcionado por el usuario.\nComparaciones con igualdad (=) o rangos cerrados incorrectos → No cubren correctamente todo el dia o\ngeneran inclusiones/exclusiones erroneas de horas.\nCualquier expresion que envuelva CreateDate en funciones (CAST, CONVERT, DATEADD sobre la\ncolumna, etc.) invalida el uso del indice.\n5. Referencias Oficiales\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-transact-sql – Explica por que las funciones sobre\ncolumnas impiden el uso de indices.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/dateadd-transact-sql – Documentacion oficial de la\nfuncion utilizada para calcular el dia siguiente de forma segura.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/performance-guidance – Mejores practicas de\nMicrosoft para mantener predicados SARGable en Azure SQL."}], "points": 3}, {"id": 7, "pages": [19, 20], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p019-010"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database.\nYou need to create a scalar user-defined function (UDF) that returns the number of whole years between an\ninput parameter named @OrderDate and the current date/time as a single positive integer. The function\nmust be created in Azure SQL Database.\nYou write the following code."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "01 CREATE FUNCTION dbo.ufnYearsSinceOrder (@OrderDate datetime2)\n02 RETURNS int\n03 AS\n04 BEGIN\n05\n06 END"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "What should you insert at line 05?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "RETURN DATEDIFF(year, GETDATE(), @OrderDate);"}, {"id": "B", "text": "DATEDIFF(month, @orderdate, GETDATE()) / 12"}, {"id": "C", "text": "DATEPART(year, GETDATE()) - DATEPART(year, @orderdate)"}, {"id": "D", "text": "RETURN DATEDIFF(year, @OrderDate, GETDATE());"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ07 T01\nCorrect Answer:\nRETURN DATEDIFF(year, @OrderDate, GETDATE());\nExplanation:\nLa opción correcta es RETURN DATEDIFF(year, @OrderDate, GETDATE());, colocando @OrderDate\ncomo startdate y GETDATE() como enddate, precedida de la sentencia RETURN. Según la documentación\noficial de Transact-SQL, DATEDIFF devuelve el número de límites de la unidad de fecha (en este caso,\naños) cruzados entre startdate y enddate como un valor entero con signo. Al poner la fecha de pedido\nprimero y la fecha/hora actual después, se obtiene un entero positivo que representa el número de años\ncompletos transcurridos. Además, la sintaxis de una función escalar definida por el usuario (scalar UDF) en\nAzure SQL Database exige, dentro del bloque BEGIN…END, una sentencia RETURN que devuelva la\nexpresión escalar del tipo declarado (int).\nLa opción RETURN DATEDIFF(year, GETDATE(), @OrderDate); invierte el orden de los argumentos de\nDATEDIFF (GETDATE() como startdate y @OrderDate como enddate). Esto produce un valor negativo, lo\ncual no cumple el requisito de devolver un único entero positivo.\nLa opción DATEDIFF(month, @orderdate, GETDATE()) / 12 carece de la sentencia RETURN obligatoria\nen el cuerpo de una función escalar multi-sentencia. Además, dividir la diferencia en meses entre 12 no\ngarantiza el cálculo exacto de años completos (no tiene en cuenta el día del mes ni los años bisiestos de\nforma precisa).\nLa opción DATEPART(year, GETDATE()) - DATEPART(year, @orderdate) también omite la sentencia\nRETURN requerida. Además, restar únicamente las partes de año (DATEPART) no calcula los años\ncompletos: no verifica si la fecha de aniversario ya ha pasado en el año actual, por lo que puede devolver\nun valor incorrecto.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/datediff-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-function-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 8, "pages": [20, 21], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database.\nYou deploy Data API builder (DAB) to Azure Container Apps by using the mcr.microsoft.com/azure-\ndatabases/data-api-builder:latest image.\nYou have the following Container Apps secrets:\nMSSQL_CONNECTION_STRING that maps to the SQL connection string\nDAB_CONFIG_BASE64 that maps to the DAB configuration\nYou need to initialize the DAB configuration to read the SQL connection string.\nWhich command should you run?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "dab init --database-type mssql --connection-string “secretref:DAB_CONFIG_BASE64” --host-mode\nProduction --config dab-config.json"}, {"id": "B", "text": "dab init --database-type mssql --connection-string “@env(‘MSSQL_CONNECTION_STRING’)” --host-\nmode Production --config dab-config.json"}, {"id": "C", "text": "dab init --database-type mssql --connection-string “secretref:mssql-connection-string” --host-mode\nProduction --config dab-config.json"}, {"id": "D", "text": "dab init --database-type mssql --connection-string “@env(‘DAB_CONFIG_BASE64’)” --host-mode\nProduction --config dab-config.json"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ08 T01\nCorrect Answer:\ndab init --database-type mssql --connection-string “@env(‘MSSQL_CONNECTION_STRING’)” --host-\nmode Production --config dab-config.json\nExplanation:\nPor qué es correcta\nDAB usa la sintaxis @env('VARIABLE_NAME') para resolver, en tiempo de ejecución, el valor de una\nvariable de entorno del proceso host. Cuando despliegas DAB en Azure Container Apps, los \"secrets\" que\ndefines en Container Apps se exponen al contenedor como variables de entorno (normalmente\nmapeándolos explícitamente en la definición del container app). Por eso, para que DAB lea la cadena de\nconexión desde el secret MSSQL_CONNECTION_STRING, hay que referenciarlo con @env\n('MSSQL_CONNECTION_STRING') en el parámetro --connection-string. Esto es exactamente lo que\nmuestra la documentación oficial de DAB, incluida la página del comando init: \"Database connection\nstring. Supports @env().\" con el ejemplo dab init --database-type mssql --connection-string \"@env\n('MSSQL_CONNECTION_STRING')\", generando un dab-config.json con ese mismo valor literal.\nTambién en la guía de despliegue a Azure App Service se sigue el mismo patrón: se recomienda usar la\nfunción @env() para referenciar la variable de entorno de la cadena de conexión, de modo que las\ncredenciales no queden almacenadas en el archivo de configuración.\nPor qué las otras opciones son incorrectas\nA. --connection-string \"secretref:DAB_CONFIG_BASE64\" — Incorrecta por dos motivos: secretref: no es\nuna sintaxis reconocida por DAB (es sintaxis de Container Apps YAML/ARM para mapear un secret a una\nvariable de entorno o volumen, no algo que DAB interprete dentro de dab-config.json), y además\napunta al secret equivocado (DAB_CONFIG_BASE64, que es la configuración completa en base64, no la\ncadena de conexión).\nC. --connection-string \"secretref:mssql-connection-string\" — Mismo problema de sintaxis que A: secretref:\nno es una función soportada por DAB para resolver valores dentro del JSON de configuración.\nD. --connection-string \"@env('DAB_CONFIG_BASE64')\" — Usa correctamente la sintaxis @env(), pero\nreferencia la variable equivocada. DAB_CONFIG_BASE64 contiene la configuración completa de DAB\ncodificada en base64, no la cadena de conexión SQL, por lo que el resultado sería una connection\nstring inválida.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-init#%60--connection-string%60\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/concept/config/env-function\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/deployment/azure-app-service#build-the-configuration-\nfile\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/concept/config/akv-function"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 9, "pages": [21, 22], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains a nvarchar (max) column named MessageText.\nAn ID is always contained within the first paragraph of MessageText.\nYou need to write a Transact-SQL query that uses REGEXP_SUBSTR to extract the ID from MessageText.\nWhat should you include in the query?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Apply STRING_ESCAPE(MessageText, ‘json’) before calling REGEXP_SUBSTR."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Cast MessageText to nvarchar (4000) before calling REGEXP_SUBSTR."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Add a COLLATE Latin1_General_CS_AS clause to MessageText before calling REGEXP_SUBSTR."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Run TRY_CONVERT(varchar(max), MessageText) before calling REGEXP_SUBSTR."}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ09 T01\nRespuesta Correcta:\nCast MessageText to nvarchar (4000) before calling REGEXP_SUBSTR.\nExplicacion:\nLa documentación oficial de Microsoft Learn especifica que, dentro de la familia de funciones REGEXP_*\nde Transact-SQL, el soporte para tipos LOB (varchar(max) y nvarchar(max), hasta 2 MB) está limitado\núnicamente a REGEXP_LIKE, REGEXP_COUNT y REGEXP_INSTR. La función REGEXP_SUBSTR no\nadmite tipos LOB como parámetro string_expression.\nComo MessageText está definida como nvarchar(max), pasarla directamente a REGEXP_SUBSTR\nprovocaría un error de tipo de dato no admitido. La solución consiste en convertir la columna a un tipo no-\nLOB compatible, como nvarchar(4000), antes de invocar la función. Dado que el enunciado indica que el ID\nsiempre se encuentra en el primer párrafo del texto, truncar a 4000 caracteres no supone pérdida de la\ninformación necesaria.\nPor que el resto son incorrectas?:\nA. STRING_ESCAPE(MessageText, 'json') sirve para escapar caracteres especiales en cadenas JSON, no\npara resolver un problema de tipo de dato con funciones de expresión regular; no tiene relación con la\nrestricción de REGEXP_SUBSTR sobre LOB.\nC. Añadir una cláusula COLLATE Latin1_General_CS_AS solo modifica las reglas de ordenación/\ncomparación (sensibilidad a mayúsculas/acentos) de la cadena, pero no cambia su tipo de dato subyacente\nni elimina la restricción de tamaño LOB.\nD. TRY_CONVERT(varchar(max), MessageText) sigue produciendo un tipo (max), es decir, sigue siendo\nun tipo LOB, por lo que el problema de compatibilidad con REGEXP_SUBSTR persistiría; además,\nconvertir de nvarchar a varchar puede provocar pérdida de caracteres Unicode.\nReferencias:\nMicrosoft Learn – REGEXP_SUBSTR (Transact-SQL): \"The REGEXP_LIKE, REGEXP_COUNT, and\nREGEXP_INSTR functions support LOB types (varchar(max) and nvarchar(max)) up to 2 MB for the\nstring_expression parameter\" (nota que excluye explícitamente a REGEXP_SUBSTR de esta lista de\ncompatibilidad LOB).\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/regexp-substr-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 10, "pages": [22, 22], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains database-level Data Definition Language (DDL) triggers,\nincluding a trigger named ddl_Audit.\nYou need to prevent ddl_Audit from firing during the next deployment. The trigger object must remain in\nplace.\nWhich Transact-SQL statement should you use?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "ALTER TRIGGER"}, {"id": "B", "text": "ALTER DATABASE"}, {"id": "C", "text": "ALTER SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION"}, {"id": "D", "text": "DISABLE TRIGGER"}, {"id": "E", "text": "ALTER DATABASE AUDIT SPECIFICATION"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ10 T01\nCorrecta Answer:\nDISABLE TRIGGER\nExplanation:\nLa pregunta requiere impedir que el trigger DDL de nivel de base de datos ddl_Audit se dispare durante el\npróximo despliegue, sin eliminar el objeto.\nSegún la documentación oficial de Microsoft Learn:\nDISABLE TRIGGER deshabilita un trigger (incluyendo DDL de alcance DATABASE). El trigger sigue\nexistiendo como objeto en la base de datos, pero no se ejecuta cuando ocurren las instrucciones Transact-\nSQL para las que fue programado. Se puede volver a habilitar más tarde con ENABLE TRIGGER.\nEjemplo de sintaxis aplicable: DISABLE TRIGGER ddl_Audit ON DATABASE;\nPor qué las demas son incorrectas?:\nA. ALTER TRIGGER: modifica la definición del trigger y, según la documentación, habilita el trigger.\nB. ALTER DATABASE: se usa para cambiar opciones de base de datos, no para deshabilitar un trigger\nconcreto.\nC. ALTER SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION y E. ALTER DATABASE AUDIT SPECIFICATION:\npertenecen al sistema de SQL Server Audit, no a la gestión de triggers DDL.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/statements/disable-trigger-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/relational-databases/triggers/implement-ddl-triggers?view=sql-server-\nver17#disabling-and-dropping-ddl-triggers"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 11, "pages": [22, 24], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Your development team uses GitHub Copilot Chat in Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS) to\ngenerate and run Transact-SQL queries against an Azure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains tables\nthat store sensitive customer data.\nYou need to ensure that Transact-SQL queries that run from GitHub Copilot Chat in SSMS are restricted by\nthe same permissions as the developer's login.\nWhat prevents GitHub Copilot Chat from running queries accessing data beyond the developer's access?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "GitHub Copilot Chat runs queries in a read-only sandbox that is isolated from production database\npermissions."}, {"id": "B", "text": "GitHub Copilot Chat runs queries by using the developer’s database identity and permissions."}, {"id": "C", "text": "GitHub Copilot Chat filters query results on the client side to remove rows the developer is unauthorized\nto see."}, {"id": "D", "text": "GitHub Copilot Chat uses different row-level security (RLS) policies than the developer."}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ11-T01\nThe correct answer is: B\nGitHub Copilot Chat runs queries by using the developer's database identity and permissions.\nExplanation\nExecution Context\nQueries generated by GitHub Copilot in SSMS are executed under the context of the user's login and\npermissions, unless a custom database user or SQL login is specified in the front matter of the database's\nCONSTITUTION.md.\nLayered Security\n- Copilot Application Layer: Although this system blocks write operations, it is not a security boundary.\nAccess control must be enforced at the database layer using least-privilege principles.\n- Real Security Layer: The true security boundary is SQL Server. The database engine validates each\npermission according to configured authorization rules.\nAlternative Configuration with CONSTITUTION.md\nFor greater control, administrators can configure the execution context to ensure that queries generated\nby Copilot run under a dedicated account with lower privileges instead of the user's own permissions.\nThis is specified in CONSTITUTION.md using agentExecuteAsUser, but the default behavior is to use the\nconnected developer's permissions.\nWhy This Distinction Is Important\nAnswer B is correct because it identifies the fundamental mechanism: GitHub Copilot inherits the user's\npermissions. There is no magical filtering or sandbox isolation. It all comes down to SQL Server enforcing\nits security rules consistently, regardless of whether the query comes from a user typing directly or from\nCopilot.\nWhy the other options are incorrect:\n- A (Isolated sandbox): Incorrect. GitHub Copilot does not run in an isolated sandbox. Queries are executed\nagainst the real database using the developer's context.\n- C (Client-side filtering): Incorrect. Security is not implemented on the client side. The security boundary is\nthe enforcement of SQL Server permissions, not Copilot's classification.\n- D (Different RLS): Incorrect. GitHub Copilot uses the same RLS policies as the user. It does not apply\ndifferent policies.\nReferences:\n- Microsoft Learn - Execution context for GitHub Copilot in SQL Server Management Studio\nURL: https://learn.microsoft.com/ssms/github-copilot/execution-context\nTopic: Default execution model and context security\n- Microsoft Learn - Use the GitHub Copilot Chat experience in SQL Server Management Studio\nURL: https://learn.microsoft.com/ssms/github-copilot/chat\nTopic: How Copilot executes queries under user context\n- Microsoft Learn - Best practices for GitHub Copilot in SQL Server Management Studio\nURL: https://learn.microsoft.com/ssms/github-copilot/best-practices\nTopic: Least privilege principle and permission security\n- DP-800 Study Guide - Design and implement SQL solutions by using AI-assisted tools\nTopic: Interpretation of security impact when using AI-assisted tools"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 12, "pages": [24, 25], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named AdventureWorksDB that contains a table named dbo.Employee.\nYou have a C# Azure Functions app that uses an HTTP-triggered function with an Azure SQL input binding\nto query dbo.Employee.\nYou are adding a second function that will react to row changes in dbo.Employee and write structured logs.\nYou need to configure AdventureWorksDB and the app to meet the following requirements:\nChanges to dbo.Employee must trigger the new function within five seconds.\nEach invocation must process no more than 100 changes.\nWhich two database configurations should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Create an AFTER trigger on dbo.Employee for Data Manipulation Language (DML)."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Set Sql_Trigger_MaxBatchSize to 100."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Enable change tracking on the dbo.Employee table."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Enable change tracking at the database level."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Set Sql_Trigger_PollingIntervalMs to 5000."}, {"id": "F", "text": "Enable change data capture (CDC) for dbo.Employee table changes."}], "correct": ["C", "D"], "type": "multiple", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "CD\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ12-T01\n---ES---\nRespuesta\nC. Enable change tracking on the dbo.Employee table.\nD. Enable change tracking at the database level.\nExplicación\nEl Azure SQL trigger de Azure Functions requiere **Change Tracking**.\nLas dos configuraciones de base de datos necesarias son:\n- Habilitar Change Tracking a nivel de base de datos.\n- Habilitar Change Tracking en la tabla dbo.Employee.\nSon las únicas opciones de la lista que son configuraciones de base de datos.\nPor qué las demás no son correctas\n- A: Un trigger AFTER no se usa con el Azure SQL trigger binding.\n- B y E: Son ajustes de la aplicación (`Sql_Trigger_MaxBatchSize` y `Sql_Trigger_PollingIntervalMs`),\nno de la base de datos.\n- F: CDC no es el mecanismo que utiliza el Azure SQL trigger.\nReferencias\nMicrosoft Learn: Azure SQL trigger for Functions – Set up change tracking (required)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger#set-up-change-\ntracking-required\nSQL Server: About Change Tracking\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/track-changes/about-change-tracking-sql-server\n---EN---\nAnswer\nC. Enable change tracking on the dbo.Employee table.\nD. Enable change tracking at the database level.\nExplanation\nThe Azure SQL trigger for Azure Functions requires **Change Tracking**.\nThe two required database configurations are:\n- Enable Change Tracking at the database level.\n- Enable Change Tracking on the dbo.Employee table.\nThese are the only database-level options in the list.\nWhy the others are incorrect\n- A: An AFTER trigger is not used by the Azure SQL trigger binding.\n- B and E: These are application settings (`Sql_Trigger_MaxBatchSize` and\n`Sql_Trigger_PollingIntervalMs`), not database configurations.\n- F: CDC is not the mechanism used by the Azure SQL trigger.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Learn: Azure SQL trigger for Functions – Set up change tracking (required)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger#set-up-change-\ntracking-required\nSQL Server: About Change Tracking\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/track-changes/about-change-tracking-sql-server"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 13, "pages": [25, 27], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p026-011"], "type": "drag", "template": "SELECT MessageID,\n {{0}} MessageRaw, '\\d{3}[\\-\\s]*\\d{3}[\\-\\s]*\\d{4}' ) AS RawNumber,\n {{1}} MessageRaw, '\\d{3}[\\-\\s]*\\d{3}[\\-\\s]*\\d{4}' ), '\\D', '' ) AS DigitsOnly,\n CASE WHEN {{2}} MessageRaw, '\\d{3}[\\-\\s]*\\d{3}[\\-\\s]*\\d{4}' ) = 1\n THEN 'Valid' ELSE 'Missing' END AS PhoneStatus\nFROM dbo.CustomerMessages;", "pools": [["REGEXP_COUNT(", "REGEXP_INSTR(", "REGEXP_LIKE(", "REGEXP_REPLACE( REGEXP_SUBSTR(", "REGEXP_SUBSTR(", "STRING_SIMILARITY("], ["REGEXP_COUNT(", "REGEXP_INSTR(", "REGEXP_LIKE(", "REGEXP_REPLACE( REGEXP_SUBSTR(", "REGEXP_SUBSTR(", "STRING_SIMILARITY("], ["REGEXP_COUNT(", "REGEXP_INSTR(", "REGEXP_LIKE(", "REGEXP_REPLACE( REGEXP_SUBSTR(", "REGEXP_SUBSTR(", "STRING_SIMILARITY("]], "correct": [4, 3, 2], "image": 11, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a Microsoft SQL Server 2025 database that contains a table named dbo.CustomerMessages.\ndbo.CustomerMessages contains two columns named MessageID (int) and MessageRaw (nvarchar(max)).\nMessageRaw can contain a phone number in multiple formats, and some rows do NOT contain a phone\nnumber.\nYou need to write a single SELECT query that meets the following requirements:\nThe query must return MessageID, RawNumber, DigitsOnly, and PhoneStatus.\nRawNumber must contain the first substring that matches a phone-number pattern, or NULL if no match\nexists.\nDigitsOnly must remove all non-digit characters from RawNumber, or return NULL.\nPhoneStatus must return valid when a phone number exists in MessageRaw, otherwise return Missing.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL query? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct\ntargets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar\nbetween panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p026-012.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ13 T01\nCorrect Answer:\nPrimer espacio (RawNumber): REGEXP_SUBSTR(\nSegundo espacio (DigitsOnly): REGEXP_REPLACE( REGEXP_SUBSTR(\nTercer espacio (CASE / PhoneStatus): REGEXP_LIKE(\nExplanation:\nREGEXP_SUBSTR extrae la primera subcadena que coincide con el patrón de número de teléfono de\nMessageRaw (devuelve NULL si no hay coincidencia).\nPara obtener DigitsOnly se anida REGEXP_REPLACE sobre el resultado de REGEXP_SUBSTR y se\neliminan todos los caracteres que no sean dígitos.\nREGEXP_LIKE comprueba si el patrón existe en MessageRaw. Como en T-SQL el valor booleano true se\nevalúa como 1, la comparación = 1 funciona correctamente y permite devolver 'Valid' o 'Missing'.\nEsta combinación cumple los requisitos del enunciado en una sola consulta SELECT.\nEnglish translation of the Explanation:\nREGEXP_SUBSTR extracts the first matching phone-number substring from MessageRaw (returns NULL if\nnone exists).\nTo obtain DigitsOnly, REGEXP_REPLACE is nested on the result of REGEXP_SUBSTR and all non-digit\ncharacters are removed.\nREGEXP_LIKE checks whether the pattern exists in MessageRaw. Because in T-SQL the boolean value\ntrue evaluates to 1, the comparison = 1 works correctly and allows returning 'Valid' or 'Missing'.\nThis combination meets the requirements of the question in a single SELECT statement.\nReference:\nREGEXP_SUBSTR (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/functions/regexp-substr-\ntransact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nREGEXP_REPLACE (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/functions/regexp-\nreplace-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nREGEXP_LIKE (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/functions/regexp-like-\ntransact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nRegular expressions functions (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/functions/\nregular-expressions-functions-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 14, "pages": [27, 28], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p027-014"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named Rooms. Rooms was created by using the\nfollowing Transact-SQL statement."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE Rooms\n(\n RoomID int PRIMARY KEY,\n Owner nvarchar(100),\n Capacity int\n);"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "You discover that some records in the Rooms table contain NULL values for the Owner field.\nYou need to ensure that all future records have a value for the Owner field.\nWhat should you add?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "a foreign key"}, {"id": "B", "text": "a check constraint"}, {"id": "C", "text": "a nonclustered index"}, {"id": "D", "text": "a unique constraint"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ14 T01\nCorrect Answer:\nB. a check constraint\nExplanation:\nUna restricción CHECK permite limitar los valores admitidos en una columna mediante una condición\nlógica. En este caso, la condición Owner IS NOT NULL impide que los nuevos registros de la tabla Rooms\ntengan un valor NULL en Owner.\nComo ya existen registros con valores NULL, la restricción puede añadirse utilizando WITH NOCHECK\npara omitir la comprobación de los datos existentes, manteniendo la validación para las nuevas\ninserciones.\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/alter-table-table-constraint-transact-sql?view=sql-\nserver-ver17#remarks"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 15, "pages": [28, 29], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p028-015"], "type": "drag", "template": "ALTER TABLE WebSite.Logs\n ADD severity {{0}};\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_severity\n ON WebSite.Logs(severity)\n {{1}};\nGO", "pools": [["AS JSON_QUERY([log], '$.severity')", "AS JSON_VALUE([log], '$.severity') PERSISTED", "INCLUDE (log)", "INCLUDE (LogId, LogDateTime, [log])"], ["AS JSON_QUERY([log], '$.severity')", "AS JSON_VALUE([log], '$.severity') PERSISTED", "INCLUDE (log)", "INCLUDE (LogId, LogDateTime, [log])"]], "correct": [1, 3], "image": 15, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains a table named WebSite.Logs. WebSite.Logs\nstores application telemetry data. WebSite.Logs contains a nvarchar (max) column named log that stores\nJSON documents.\nYou have a daily report that filters by the $.severity JSON property and returns LogId, LogDateTime, and\nlog. The report frequently causes full table scans.\nYou need to modify WebSite.Logs to support efficient filtering by $.severity and avoid key lookups for the\ncolumns returned by the report.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL code to avoid full table scans? To answer, drag the appropriate\nvalues to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to\ndrag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p029-016.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ15 T01\nCORRECT ANSWER:\nALTER TABLE WebSite.Logs\nADD severity AS JSON_VALUE([log], '$.severity') PERSISTED;\nGO\nCREATE INDEX ix_severity\nON WebSite.Logs(severity)\nINCLUDE (LogId, LogDateTime, [log]);\nGO\nEXPLANATION:\nPara evitar full table scans al filtrar por la propiedad JSON $.severity y eliminar key lookups de las columnas\ndel informe (LogId, LogDateTime y log), se añade una columna calculada persistida con JSON_VALUE que\nexpone el valor escalar. A continuación se crea un índice no clusterizado sobre esa columna e se incluyen\nlas columnas necesarias con INCLUDE. De esta forma el motor utiliza un index seek y obtiene todos los\ndatos directamente del índice de cobertura, sin necesidad de acceder a la tabla base. JSON_VALUE se\nutiliza para propiedades escalares; JSON_QUERY solo para objetos o arrays.\nREFERENCES:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/json/index-json-data?view=sql-server-ver17#index-json-\nproperties-by-using-computed-columns\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/json/store-json-documents-in-sql-tables?view=sql-\nserver-ver17#indexes"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 16, "pages": [29, 35], "notes": ["Contradicción del PDF: la imagen de solución resalta WHERE p.PatientId IN; la justificación declara WHERE EXISTS. Se evalúa WHERE EXISTS, según el criterio elegido."], "sourceImages": ["p031-017", "p032-018", "p032-019", "p033-020"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "CREATE PROCEDURE dbo.GetActivePatients\nAS\nBEGIN\n SET NOCOUNT ON;\n SELECT p.Name\n FROM dbo.Patients AS p\n {{0}} (\n  SELECT PatientId\n  FROM dbo.Procedures AS pr\n  {{1}}\n  AND pr.TransactionDate >= DATEADD(DAY, -30, SYSUTCDATETIME())\n );\nEND;", "pools": [["WHERE EXISTS", "WHERE NOT EXISTS", "WHERE p.PatientId IN", "WHERE p.PatientId NOT IN"], ["HAVING p.PatientId = pr.PatientId", "HAVING p.TransactionId = pr.TransactionId", "WHERE p.PatientId = pr.PatientId", "WHERE p.TransactionId = pr.TransactionId"]], "correct": [0, 2], "image": 20, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\nCase Study\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\nAzure Environment\nFabrikam has a single Azure subscription in the East US 2 Azure region. The subscription contains an\nAzure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains the following tables:\nPatients\nEmployees\nProcedures\nTransactions\nUsefulPrompts\nProcedureDocuments\nYou store a column master key as a secret in Azure Key Vault.\nYou have an on-premises application named TransactionProcessing that uses a hard-coded username and\npassword in a connection string to access DB1.\nProblem Statements\nUsers report that after executing a long-running stored procedure named sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient,\nupdates to the underlying data are sometimes incosistent\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nFabrikam plans to manage all changes to Azure SQL Database objects by using source control in GitHub.\nEvery pull request submitted to production will be validated before it can be merged. Deployments must use\nthe Release configuration.\nSecurity Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the following security requirements:\n• The TransactionProcessing application must use a passwordless connection to DB1.\n• The Employees table contains two columns named TaxID and Salary that must be encrypted at rest.\n• Auditors must have a tamper-evident history of transactions with cryptographic proof of changes to the\nemployee data.\nDatabase Performance Requirements\nRecords accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must NOT be changed by other transactions\nwhile the stored procedure runs.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nFabrikam identifies the following AI-related requirements:\n• Queries to the ProcedureDocuments table must use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n• Users must be able to query the data in DB1 by using prompts in Copilot in Microsoft Fabric.\n• The UsefulPrompts table will store prompts that doctors can use to help diagnose patient illness by\nconnecting to an Azure OpenAI endpoint.\nDevelopment Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the following development requirements:\n• Provide the functionality to retrieve all the transactions of a given patient between two dates, showing a\nrunning total.\n• Expose a Data API builder (DAB) configuration file to enable Azure services to perform the following\noperations over a REST API:\no Read data from the procedures table without authentication.\no Read and insert data into the Transactions table once authenticated.\no Execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure.\n• Provide the functionality to retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical procedures\nduring the last 30 days.\n• Information for each medical procedure will be stored in a table. The table will be used with a large\nlanguage model (LLM) for user querying and will have the following structure."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE dbo.ProcedureDocuments\n(\n DocumentId INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n SourceId NVARCHAR(200) NULL,\n Content NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,\n Embedding VECTOR(1536) NOT NULL,\n CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\n);"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "DAB -\nYou create a DAB configuration file that meets the development requirements for DB1 and includes the following entities.\n{\n \"entities\": {\n  \"Procedures\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Procedures\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"]}]\n  },\n  \"Transactions\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Transactions\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"read\", \"create\"]}]\n  },\n  \"UpdateProcedurePatient\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\",\n   \"rest\": {\"enabled\": true, \"method\": \"post\", \"path\": \"/procedurepatient\"},\n   \"graphql\": false,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"execute\"]}]\n  }\n }\n}"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "You need to create a solution that meets the development requirements for retrieving the patient lists.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p034-021.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ16 T01\n1. ANÁLISIS\nConcepto principal: subconsulta correlacionada en Transact-SQL para filtrar filas de Patients que tienen\ncoincidencias en Procedures dentro de los últimos 30 días.\nServicio: Azure SQL Database / SQL Server.\nEscenario: cumplir el requisito de desarrollo “retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical\nprocedures during the last 30 days”.\n2. VERIFICACIÓN (MCP)\nConsultada la documentación oficial de Microsoft Learn sobre subconsultas y el operador EXISTS.\n3. RESOLUCIÓN\nPrimera lista desplegable: WHERE EXISTS\nSegunda lista desplegable: WHERE p.PatientId = pr.PatientId\n4. JUSTIFICACIÓN\nCorrecta – WHERE EXISTS + WHERE p.PatientId = pr.PatientId\nSegún Microsoft Learn, WHERE EXISTS (subquery) realiza una prueba de existencia. La subconsulta debe\nser correlacionada (p.PatientId = pr.PatientId) para que, por cada fila de Patients, se verifique si existe al\nmenos una fila en Procedures que cumpla la condición de fecha (pr.TransactionDate >= DATEADD(DAY, -\n30, SYSUTCDATETIME())). Esto devuelve exactamente los nombres de pacientes que tuvieron\nprocedimientos en los últimos 30 días.\nIncorrectas\nWHERE NOT EXISTS → devolvería los pacientes sin procedimientos.\nWHERE p.PatientId IN / NOT IN → sintácticamente posibles, pero la estructura del código y las opciones\ndel segundo desplegable están diseñadas para el patrón de existencia correlacionada documentado.\nCualquier opción con HAVING → inválida: HAVING solo se usa después de GROUP BY y las reglas de\nsubconsultas de Microsoft Learn prohíben su uso en este contexto.\n5. REFERENCIA\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/performance/subqueries\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/exists-transact-sql"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 17, "pages": [35, 36], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named ToDo that contains a table named dbo.ToDo.\nYour company plans to develop an Azure Functions app to run whenever the rows in dbo.ToDo change. The\napp will process INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE events by using the Azure SQL trigger binding.\nYou need to configure ToDo to support the planned app.\nWhat should you do?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Enable change tracking on ToDo and dbo.ToDo."}, {"id": "B", "text": "On dbo.ToDo, create a Data Manipulation Language (DML) trigger that calls the Azure Functions HTTP\nendpoint."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Enable change data capture (CDC) on ToDo and dbo.ToDo."}, {"id": "D", "text": "On dbo.ToDo, create a Data Definition Language (DDL) trigger that calls the Azure Functions HTTP\nendpoint"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ17 T01\nCorrect Answer: Enable change tracking on ToDo and dbo.ToDo.\nExplanation:\nPor qué la opción A es correcta.\nEl Azure SQL trigger binding de Azure Functions se basa exclusivamente en la funcionalidad de Change\nTracking de SQL Server / Azure SQL.\nSegún la documentación oficial, es obligatorio realizar dos pasos:\nHabilitar Change Tracking a nivel de base de datos:SQLALTER DATABASE [ToDo]\nSET CHANGE_TRACKING = ON\n(CHANGE_RETENTION = 2 DAYS, AUTO_CLEANUP = ON);\nHabilitar Change Tracking en la tabla:SQLALTER TABLE [dbo].[ToDo]\nENABLE CHANGE_TRACKING;\nUna vez habilitado, el runtime del trigger realiza un polling periódico sobre las tablas de sistema de Change\nTracking (CHANGETABLE) y entrega los cambios (Insert / Update / Delete) a la función de Azure\nFunctions. Sin este requisito el binding no funciona.\nB: Crear un trigger DML que invoque un endpoint HTTP de Azure Functions es un patrón completamente\nmanual (webhook personalizado). No es el mecanismo que utiliza el binding nativo SqlTrigger. El binding no\nrequiere ni utiliza triggers DML definidos por el usuario.\nC: Change Data Capture (CDC) es una tecnología distinta (basada en el log de transacciones y jobs de\ncaptura). El Azure SQL trigger binding no utiliza CDC. La documentación oficial indica de forma explícita\nque se usa únicamente Change Tracking.\nD: Un trigger DDL responde a cambios de esquema (CREATE, ALTER, DROP de objetos), no a\nmodificaciones de filas de datos (DML). Es irrelevante para detectar INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE en la tabla\ndbo.ToDo\nReferences:\nFuente oficial exacta utilizada (Microsoft Learn – documentación actualizada):\nTítulo: Azure SQL trigger for Functions\nSección específica: Set up change tracking (required)\nURL directa:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql-trigger#set-up-change-\ntracking-required"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 18, "pages": [36, 39], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p037-022"], "type": "drag", "template": "WITH PhoneCTE AS\n(\n SELECT DISTINCT c.CustomerId, {{0}} AS PhoneRaw\n FROM dbo.Customers AS c\n)\nSELECT p.CustomerId, {{1}} AS PhoneNUmerals\nFROM PhoneCTE AS p\nWHERE {{2}} IS NOT NULL;", "pools": [["JSON_QUERY(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "JSON_VALUE(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "OPENJSON(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "p.PhoneRaw", "REGEXP_LIKE(p.PhoneRaw, '[^0-9]', '')", "REGEXP_REPLACE(p.PhoneRaw, '[^0-9]', '')", "REGEXP_SUBSTR(p.PhoneRaw, '[0-9]+')"], ["JSON_QUERY(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "JSON_VALUE(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "OPENJSON(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "p.PhoneRaw", "REGEXP_LIKE(p.PhoneRaw, '[^0-9]', '')", "REGEXP_REPLACE(p.PhoneRaw, '[^0-9]', '')", "REGEXP_SUBSTR(p.PhoneRaw, '[0-9]+')"], ["JSON_QUERY(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "JSON_VALUE(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "OPENJSON(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone')", "p.PhoneRaw", "REGEXP_LIKE(p.PhoneRaw, '[^0-9]', '')", "REGEXP_REPLACE(p.PhoneRaw, '[^0-9]', '')", "REGEXP_SUBSTR(p.PhoneRaw, '[0-9]+')"]], "correct": [1, 5, 3], "image": 22, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Question #18 – DRAG DROP\nYou have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named dbo.Customers.\ndbo.Customers contains the following columns:\nCustomerId (int) (primary key)\nProfileJson (nvarchar(max))\nYou have an application that returns phone numbers in a format of +000 000-000-0000.\nThe phone numbers are stored in ProfileJson.\nYou need to write a query that returns:\nOne row per customer\nA PhoneNUmerals column that contains only the digits\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL query?\nTo answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets.\nEach value may be used once, more than once, or not at all.\nYou may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p038-023.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ18 T01\nCorrect Answer:\nWITH PhoneCTE AS\n(\nSELECT DISTINCT\nc.CustomerId,\nJSON_VALUE(c.ProfileJson, '$.contact.phone') AS PhoneRaw\nFROM dbo.Customers AS c\n)\nSELECT\np.CustomerId,\nREGEXP_REPLACE(p.PhoneRaw, '[^0-9]', '') AS PhoneNUmerals\nFROM PhoneCTE AS p\nWHERE p.PhoneRaw IS NOT NULL;\nExplanation:\nJSON_VALUE extracts the scalar phone number value from the JSON path $.contact.phone.\nREGEXP_REPLACE(p.PhoneRaw, '[^0-9]', '') removes every non-digit character, leaving only the numbers.\nThe CTE + DISTINCT ensures one row per customer.\nThe WHERE clause filters out rows without a phone number.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/json-value-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/regexp-replace-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/json/json-data-sql-server"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 19, "pages": [39, 40], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p039-024"], "type": "drag", "template": "Support NRT analytics on dbo.Orders: {{0}}\nReduce read time when retrieving analytical data: {{1}}\nIndex only rows that match a predicate: {{2}}", "pools": [["A clustered columnstore index", "A clustered rowstore index ordered by key columns", "A filtered nonclustered index with a WHERE predicate", "A nonclustered columnstore index on an existing rowstore table"], ["A clustered columnstore index", "A clustered rowstore index ordered by key columns", "A filtered nonclustered index with a WHERE predicate", "A nonclustered columnstore index on an existing rowstore table"], ["A clustered columnstore index", "A clustered rowstore index ordered by key columns", "A filtered nonclustered index with a WHERE predicate", "A nonclustered columnstore index on an existing rowstore table"]], "correct": [3, 3, 2], "image": 24, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "DRAG DROP\nYou have an Azure SQL database named SalesDB that supports an ecommerce application. SalesDB\ncontains a table named dbo.Orders that has a clustered index on a column named OrderId.\ndbo.Orders receives continuous OLTP inserts and updates during business hours.\nYour analytics team runs hourly aggregate queries that scan dbo.Orders to calculate revenue trends for\nrecent dates.\nYou need to improve the performance of the hourly analytics queries without significantly affecting OLTP\nthroughput. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nSupport near-real-time (NRT) analytics on the dbo.Orders table.\nReduce read time when retrieving analytical data from dbo.Orders.\nSupport indexing only rows that match a predicate, such as Active = 1.\nWhich type of index should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate index types to\nthe correct requirements. Each index type may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need\nto drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p039-025.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ19-T01\nCorrect Answer:\nSupport NRT analytics on dbo.Orders → D. Nonclustered columnstore index on an existing rowstore table\nReduce read time when retrieving analytical data → D. Nonclustered columnstore index on an existing\nrowstore table\nIndex only rows that match a predicate → C. Filtered nonclustered index with a WHERE predicate\n(D puede usarse más de una vez.)\nTechnical Justification:\ndbo.Orders es una tabla rowstore con índice clustered existente y workload OLTP continuo.\nPara soportar analytics near-real-time (NRT) y reducir el tiempo de las consultas analíticas (agregaciones)\nsin afectar significativamente el throughput OLTP, se crea un nonclustered columnstore index (NCCI)\nupdateable. El optimizador dirige automáticamente las consultas analíticas al columnstore (batch mode +\nalta compresión) mientras el OLTP sigue usando los índices B-tree. Los cambios se reflejan en tiempo real.\nPara indexar solo las filas que cumplen un predicado (ej. Active = 1) se usa un filtered nonclustered index.\nWhy the other options are incorrect:\nA (Clustered columnstore index): Convierte toda la tabla a columnstore, elimina el clustered index existente\ny tiene mayor impacto en inserts/updates OLTP.\nB: No aporta rendimiento de columnstore ni filtrado.\nC para NRT/analytics: Un filtered B-tree no ofrece el rendimiento de scans/agregaciones de un\ncolumnstore.\nOfficial Microsoft references:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/indexes/get-started-with-columnstore-for-real-\ntime-operational-analytics\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/indexes/columnstore-indexes-overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/indexes/columnstore-indexes-design-guidance"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 20, "pages": [40, 41], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p041-026"], "type": "drag", "template": "Sales: {{0}}\nProducts: {{1}}", "pools": [["A clustered columnstore index", "A clustered rowstore index", "A nonclustered columnstore index", "A nonclustered rowstore index"], ["A clustered columnstore index", "A clustered rowstore index", "A nonclustered columnstore index", "A nonclustered rowstore index"]], "correct": [0, 1], "image": 26, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that stores sales data and contains tables named Sales and Products.\nSales contains three columns named SalesDate, ProductKey, and TotalSale,\nSales is 10 TB and is loaded nightly by using a batch process. Most reporting queries scan large portions of\nSales, filter on SalesDate or ProductKey, and use SUM() to aggregate TotalSale.\nProducts is relatively small and is used primarily for point lookups and joins to Sales.\nYou need to recommend which indexes to create to optimize the reporting queries. The solution must\nminimize storage requirements.\nWhich type of index should you recommend for each table? To answer, drag the appropriate index types to\nthe correct tables. Each index type may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag\nthe split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p041-027.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ20 T01\nCorrect Answer:\nSales: A clustered columnstore index\nProducts: A clustered rowstore index\nWhy not the others:\nSales: nonclustered columnstore duplicaría almacenamiento; rowstore no da la compresión ni el\nrendimiento necesario para escaneos y agregaciones masivas.\nProducts: columnstore no aporta beneficio en tablas pequeñas con seeks puntuales; nonclustered rowstore\nañade almacenamiento extra innecesario.\nExplanation:\nSales es grande y sus consultas escanean rangos y agregan con SUM, por lo que columnstore reduce el\nalmacenamiento gracias a su compresión de aproximadamente 10x. Products es pequeña y se usa para\nlookups y joins puntuales, por lo que rowstore es más eficiente para ese tipo de búsquedas.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/indexes/columnstore-indexes-design-guidance?\nview=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 21, "pages": [41, 47], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p043-028"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "WITH TicketRanks AS\n(\n SELECT t.TicketId, CAST(t.CreatedAt AS date) AS TicketDate,\n {{0}} (t.Payload, '$.customer.id') AS CustomerId,\n t.CreatedAt, {{1}} () OVER\n (PARTITION BY CAST(t.CreatedAt AS date), JSON_VALUE(t.Payload, '$.customer.id')\n ORDER BY t.CreatedAt ASC) AS rn\n FROM dbo.SupportTickets AS t\n WHERE t.CreatedAt >= DATEADD(day, -7, SYSUTCDATETIME())\n)\nSELECT TicketDate, CustomerId, TicketId, CreatedAt\nFROM TicketRanks\nWHERE rn = {{2}}\nORDER BY TicketDate, CustomerId;", "pools": [["DENSE_RANK", "JSON_VALUE", "OPENJSON", "ROW_NUMBER"], ["DENSE_RANK", "JSON_VALUE", "OPENJSON", "ROW_NUMBER"], ["1", "7", "DATEDIFF(day, -7, SYSUTCDATETIME())"]], "correct": [1, 3, 0], "image": 28, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named dbo.SupportTickets. dbo.SupportTickets\ncontains a JSON column named Payload and a datetime column CreatedAt.\nYou need to generate a report for the last seven days that meets the following requirements:\n• Returns exactly one row per customer per day\n• For each customer and day, returns the earliest ticket\n• Includes the customer ID stored in Payload\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL query? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer\narea.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p045-029.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ21 T01\nExplanation\nPrimer desplegable:\nRESPUESTA CORRECTA:\nJSON_VALUE, ya que extrae un valor de un documento JSON usando un JSON path,el uso sería el\nsiguiente: JSON_VALUE(t.Payload, '$.customer.id') ya que lo que denota esta expresion seria que de la\ncolumna Payload extrae el customer.id.\nRESPUESTAS INCORRECTAS:\nDENSE_RANK no es correcta ya que es una funcion de ranking, y estas no extraen valores de un\nJSON.\nROW_NUMBER no es correcta ya que es una funcion de ventana y no sirven para extraer valores de\nJSON.\nOPENJSON sirve para transformar un JSON en filas y columnas. No devuelve un valor escalar para\nusarlo directamente como columna en el SELECT.\nSegundo desplegable:\nRESPUESTA CORRECTA:\nROW_NUMBER() asigna un número secuencial único empezando en 1 dentro de cada partición (fecha\n+ CustomerId), ordenado por CreatedAt ASC. Así se identifica de forma fiable el primer ticket de cada\ncliente en cada día.\nRESPUESTAS INCORRECTAS:\nJSON_VALUE y OPENJSON: no son funciones de ventana; no se pueden usar con la cláusula OVER.\nDENSE_RANK asigna el mismo rango a filas empatadas. Si dos tickets tienen exactamente el mismo\nCreatedAt, ambos recibirían rn = 1 y se devolverían ambos, lo que no es el comportamiento deseado\ncuando se quiere “el primero” de forma única.\nTercer desplegable:\nRESPUESTA CORRECTA:\n1 es la opccion correcta, ya que como la consulta ordena los tickets por fecha de creación ascendente,\nel ticket más antiguo de cada cliente en un día dado recibe siempre el valor rn = 1. Al filtrar en la\nconsulta principal con WHERE rn = 1, se elimina el resto de tickets secundarios del día.\nRESPUESTA INCORRECTA:\nel numero 7 no tiene sentido ya que rn es un número de fila generado por la función de ventana, no un\nvalor de fecha.\nDATEDIFF(day, -7, SYSUTCDATETIME()): es una expresión de fecha/hora y no tiene ninguna relacion\ncon el valor que representa rn.\nReference\nDocumentación oficial de Microsoft Learn – JSON_VALUE (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/json-value-transact-sql\nDocumentación oficial de Microsoft Learn – OPENJSON (Transact-SQL) y comparación con\nJSON_VALUE:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/json/convert-json-data-to-rows-and-columns-\nwith-openjson-sql-server\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/json/validate-query-and-change-json-data-\nwith-built-in-functions-sql-server\nDocumentación oficial de Microsoft Learn – ROW_NUMBER (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/row-number-transact-sql\nDocumentación oficial de Microsoft Learn – OVER Clause y funciones de ranking:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-over-clause-transact-sql"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 22, "pages": [47, 48], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You are developing an Azure SQL database solution from a locally cloned GitHub repository by using\nMicrosoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot Chat.\nYou need to ensure that GitHub Copilot Chat can call the hosted GitHub MCP Server tools by using OAuth.\nThe MCP server configuration must be scoped to the repository.\nWhat should you do in Visual Studio Code?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Create a personal access token (PAT) that has the repo scope and store the PAT in the .vscode\\mcp.\njson file as the Authorization header."}, {"id": "B", "text": "From the Command Palette, enter MCP: add server, select HTTP (HTTP or Server-Sent Events), enter\nhttps://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/, and then save the configuration to the workspace settings."}, {"id": "C", "text": "From the Command Palette, enter MCP: add server, select HTTP (HTTP or Server-Sent Events), enter\nhttps://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/, and then save the configuration to the user settings."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Create a personal access token (PAT) that has the repo scope and store the PAT in the .github\\mcp.\njson file as the Authorization header."}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ22 T01\nCorrect Answer:\nFrom the Command Palette, enter MCP: add server, select HTTP (HTTP or Server-Sent Events), enter\nhttps://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/, and then save the configuration to the workspace settings.\nExplanation:\nThe GitHub MCP Server hosted at https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/ supports remote HTTP/SSE transport\nand authenticates automatically via OAuth when added through VS Code's MCP: Add Server command —\nyou are prompted to sign in and consent, rather than needing to supply any token yourself. Saving the\nconfiguration to workspace settings writes it to .vscode\\mcp.json in the repository, which satisfies the\nrequirement that the configuration be scoped only to that repository (rather than to all workspaces globally).\nThe two personal access token (PAT) options are incorrect because they rely on a manually created and\nstored secret (an Authorization header with a PAT) instead of OAuth — this contradicts the requirement to\nuse OAuth, and hardcoding a PAT in a checked-in config file is also a security anti-pattern. The .github\\mcp.\njson location is not a recognized VS Code MCP configuration path at all — VS Code looks for .vscode\\mcp.\njson for workspace-scoped servers, not a .github folder.\nSaving to user settings instead of workspace settings is incorrect because user/global settings apply the\nMCP server configuration across all workspaces in VS Code, not just the current repository — this violates\nthe requirement that the configuration be scoped specifically to the repository.\nReferences:\nhttps://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/chat/mcp-servers\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/expose-existing-mcp-server#validate-and-use-the-mcp-\nserver\nhttps://docs.github.com/copilot/customizing-copilot/using-model-context-protocol/using-the-github-mcp-\nserver\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/mcp-server/3-configure-connect"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 23, "pages": [48, 50], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p048-030"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "CREATE PARTITION FUNCTION PartitionByYear (datetime2)\nAS {{0}}\nFOR VALUES ({{1}});", "pools": [["PARTITION", "RANGE LEFT", "RANGE RIGHT"], ["'2019-01-01 00:00:00', '2020-01-01 00:00:00', '2021-12-31 23:59:59'", "'2019-12-31 00:00:00', '2020-12-31 23:59:59'", "'2020-01-01 00:00:00', '2020-02-01 00:00:00', '2020-03-01 00:00:00'", "'2020-01-01 00:00:00', '2021-01-01 00:00:00'"]], "correct": [2, 3], "image": 30, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named Table1. Table1 contains 25,000,000 rows of\ndata and a datetime2 column named DateKey. The data in Table1 spans the years 2020 through 2021.\nYou need to partition the data in Table1 by year. The solution must minimize how long it takes to rebuild or\nreindex the table.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer\narea.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p049-031.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ23 T01\nCorrect answer:\nRANGE RIGHT\n'2020-01-01 00:00:00', '2021-01-01 00:00:00'\nExplanation:\nLa sintaxis correcta de CREATE PARTITION FUNCTION es AS RANGE [LEFT | RIGHT] FOR VALUES\n(...). La opción “PARTITION” sola no es válida.\nSe usa RANGE RIGHT porque es la práctica recomendada de Microsoft para columnas de tipo fecha/\ndatetime2: el valor límite actúa como límite inferior inclusivo del rango derecho. Con los valores '2020-01-01\n00:00:00' y '2021-01-01 00:00:00' se crean tres particiones:\ndatos < 2020-01-01\ndatos ≥ 2020-01-01 y < 2021-01-01 (todo el año 2020)\ndatos ≥ 2021-01-01 (año 2021 y posteriores)\nEsto particiona exactamente por año los datos que abarcan 2020-2021.\nLas otras opciones de valores no sirven:\n'2019-01-01 00:00:00', '2020-01-01 00:00:00', '2021-12-31 23:59:59' mezcla años y usa un límite final de día\nque no es necesario ni limpio.\n'2019-12-31 00:00:00', '2020-12-31 23:59:59' serían más propios de RANGE LEFT y exigen manejar el final\nexacto del día (problemas de precisión con datetime2).\n'2020-01-01 00:00:00', '2020-02-01 00:00:00', '2020-03-01 00:00:00' crea particiones mensuales solo de\n2020, no anuales.\nRANGE LEFT con los valores de inicio de año colocaría el 1 de enero en la partición anterior, lo que no\nsepara correctamente los años.\nEl particionamiento por año permite operaciones de mantenimiento (REBUILD / REORGANIZE) a nivel de\npartición en lugar de toda la tabla de 25 millones de filas, minimizando el tiempo requerido.\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-partition-function-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/partitions/create-partitioned-tables-and-indexes\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/partitions/partitioned-tables-and-indexes"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 24, "pages": [50, 51], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You are developing an Azure SQL solution by using Microsoft Visual Studio 2026. The solution uses a\nGitHub repository.\nYou plan to use GitHub Copilot Chat to access the GitHub repository tools by connecting to the GitHub MCP\nServer.\nYou need to configure Visual Studio to support the planned configuration. The solution must rely on OAuth\nto access the MCP server.\nWhat should you create?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "the \\.vs\\settings.json file that contains the personal access token (PAT) that has the repo scope"}, {"id": "B", "text": "the \\.mcp.json file that contains a URL of https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/"}, {"id": "C", "text": "the \\.vscode\\settings.json file that contains a URL of https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/"}, {"id": "D", "text": "the \\.github\\mcp.json file that contains the personal access token (PAT) that has the repo scope"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ24 / T01\n1. Respuesta correcta.\nB. the .mcp.json file that contains a URL of https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/\n2. Explicación.\nLa documentación oficial de Microsoft Learn para Visual Studio indica que, para conectar GitHub Copilot\nChat al servidor MCP remoto de GitHub y usar autenticación OAuth (sin necesidad de Personal Access\nToken), se debe crear un archivo .mcp.json (en la raíz de la solución <SOLUTIONDIR>\\.mcp.json o en el\nperfil de usuario %USERPROFILE%\\.mcp.json) con el siguiente contenido:\n{\n\"servers\": {\n\"github\": {\n\"url\": \"https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/\"\n}\n}\n}\nDespués de guardar el archivo, Visual Studio muestra un CodeLens que permite autenticarse mediante\nOAuth con una cuenta de GitHub. Esto cumple exactamente el requisito del enunciado: “The solution must\nrely on OAuth to access the MCP server”.\nA es incorrecta: El archivo .vs\\settings.json no es el mecanismo de configuración de servidores MCP en\nVisual Studio, y además exige un PAT (Personal Access Token) con scope repo. El enunciado exige\nOAuth, no PAT.\nC es incorrecta: El archivo .vscode\\settings.json (o .vscode\\mcp.json) es el formato típico de Visual Studio\nCode, no de Visual Studio. Visual Studio usa específicamente .mcp.json.\nD es incorrecta: No existe un archivo estándar .github\\mcp.json para esta configuración en Visual Studio, y\nademás contiene un PAT, lo que viola el requisito de usar OAuth.\n3. Referencias.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/ide/mcp-servers\nhttps://docs.github.com/en/copilot/how-tos/provide-context/use-mcp-in-your-ide/set-up-the-github-mcp-\nserver"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 25, "pages": [51, 54], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p051-032", "p052-033"], "type": "drag", "template": "CREATE FUNCTION dbo.ufn_DaysSinceOrder\n( @OrderDate datetime2(0) )\n{{0}}\nBEGIN\n DECLARE @Days int;\n SELECT @Days = {{1}};\n RETURN @Days;\nEND;\nGO", "pools": [["AS RETURN", "DATEADD(day, @OrderDate, GETDATE())", "DATEDIFF(day, @OrderDate, GETDATE())", "RETURNS INT", "RETURNS TABLE", "WITH SCHEMABINDING"], ["AS RETURN", "DATEADD(day, @OrderDate, GETDATE())", "DATEDIFF(day, @OrderDate, GETDATE())", "RETURNS INT", "RETURNS TABLE", "WITH SCHEMABINDING"]], "correct": [3, 2], "image": 33, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named Sales.Orders. Sales.Orders contains the\nfollowing columns."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Column | Data type\nOrderId | int\nCustomerId | int\nOrderDate | datetime2\nTotalAmount | decimal(18,2)"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Reporting queries frequently repeat logic to calculate the number of days since an order was placed.\nYou need to create a scalar user-defined function (UDF) that returns the number of days between an input\nvalue of @OrderDate and the current date and time.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct\ntargets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar\nbetween panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p053-034.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ25 T01\nRespuesta correcta\nEn el Answer Area hay que colocar:\nDespués de los parámetros → RETURNS INT\nDentro del SELECT @Days = → DATEDIFF(day, @OrderDate, GETDATE())\nPor qué estas opciones\n1. RETURNS INT\nEs una función escalar: solo devuelve un valor único (el número de días).\nPor eso se usa RETURNS INT.\nRETURNS TABLE y AS RETURN son solo para funciones que devuelven tablas (Table-Valued Functions).\nWITH SCHEMABINDING es opcional y el enunciado no lo pide.\n2. DATEDIFF(day, @OrderDate, GETDATE())\nDATEDIFF calcula la diferencia entre dos fechas.\nEl primer parámetro (day) indica que queremos el resultado en días.\n@OrderDate es la fecha de inicio.\nGETDATE() es la fecha y hora actual del servidor.\nDATEADD no sirve aquí porque añade tiempo a una fecha, no calcula diferencias.\nReferencias oficiales de Microsoft Learn\nSintaxis de funciones escalares\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-function-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17#syntax\nFunción DATEDIFF\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/datediff-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nFunciones de fecha y hora\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/date-and-time-data-types-and-functions-transact-sql?view=sql-\nserver-ver17"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 26, "pages": [54, 57], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p056-035", "p056-036"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You plan to implement changes to sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient to meet the performance requirements.\nYou change the stored procedure to run all its code within a transaction.\nWhich transaction level should you use?\nCase Study Title (Case Study):\nQ26 T01\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment -\nAzure Environment -\nFabrikam has a single Azure subscription in the East US 2 Azure region. The subscription contains an\nAzure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains the following tables:\nPatients\nEmployees\nProcedures\nTransactions\nUsefulPrompts\nProcedureDocuments\nYou store a column master key as a secret in Azure Key Vault.\nYou have an on-premises application named TransactionProcessing that uses a hard-coded username and\npassword in a connection string to access DB1.\nProblem Statements -\nUsers report that after executing a long-running stored procedure named sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient,\nupdates to the underlying data are sometimes inconsistent.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nFabrikam plans to manage all changes to Azure SQL Database objects by using source control in GitHub.\nEvery pull request submitted to production will be validated before it can be merged. Deployments must use\nthe Release configuration.\nSecurity Requirements -\nFabrikam identifies the following security requirements:\nThe TransactionProcessing application must use a passwordless connection to DB1.\nThe Employees table contains two columns named TaxID and Salary that must be encrypted at rest.\nAuditors must have a tamper-evident history of transactions with cryptographic proof of changes to the\nemployee data.\nDatabase Performance Requirements\nRecords accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must NOT be changed by other transactions\nwhile the stored procedure runs.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nFabrikam identifies the following AI-related requirements:\nQueries to the ProcedureDocuments table must use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\nUsers must be able to query the data in DB1 by using prompts in Copilot in Microsoft Fabric.\nDevelopment Requirements -\nFabrikam identifies the following development requirements:\nProvide the functionality to retrieve all the transactions of a given patient between two dates, showing a\nrunning total.\nExpose a Data API builder (DAB) configuration file to enable Azure services to perform the following\noperations over a REST API: o Read data from the procedures table without authentication. o Read and\ninsert data into the Transactions table once authenticated. o Execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\nstored procedure.\nProvide the functionality to retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical procedures during\nthe last 30 days.\nInformation for each medical procedure will be stored in a table. The table will be used with a large language\nmodel (LLM) for user querying and will have the following structure."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE dbo.ProcedureDocuments\n(\n DocumentId INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n SourceId NVARCHAR(200) NULL,\n Content NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,\n Embedding VECTOR(1536) NOT NULL,\n CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\n);"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "DAB -\nYou create a DAB configuration file that meets the development requirements for DB1 and includes the\nfollowing entities."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "DAB -\nYou create a DAB configuration file that meets the development requirements for DB1 and includes the following entities.\n{\n \"entities\": {\n  \"Procedures\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Procedures\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"]}]\n  },\n  \"Transactions\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Transactions\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"read\", \"create\"]}]\n  },\n  \"UpdateProcedurePatient\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\",\n   \"rest\": {\"enabled\": true, \"method\": \"post\", \"path\": \"/procedurepatient\"},\n   \"graphql\": false,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"execute\"]}]\n  }\n }\n}"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "REPEATABLE READ"}, {"id": "B", "text": "SERIALIZABLE"}, {"id": "C", "text": "SNAPSHOT"}, {"id": "D", "text": "READ COMMITTED SNAPSHOT"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nT01 Q26\nExplanation:\nEl requisito del case study dice: \"Records accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must NOT be\nchanged by other transactions while the stored procedure runs.\" Es decir, los registros que el procedimiento\nlee no pueden ser modificados por otras transacciones mientras el procedimiento se ejecuta.\nREPEATABLE READ cumple exactamente eso:\nMantiene los shared locks sobre las filas leídas hasta que termina la transacción, no solo hasta el final de\ncada sentencia.\nNinguna otra transacción puede modificar ni borrar los datos que ya leíste hasta que hagas COMMIT.\nEsto evita las lecturas no repetibles y las actualizaciones perdidas (lost updates). Eso encaja con el\nproblema descrito: tras un procedimiento largo, los datos quedaban inconsistentes.\nLa documentación indica que hace falta al menos REPEATABLE READ para evitar lost updates.\nPor qué las otras opciones son incorrectas\nB. SERIALIZABLE: también cumpliría el requisito, pero es el nivel más restrictivo. Bloquea rangos\ncompletos de claves e impide insertar filas nuevas en esos rangos (evita phantoms). El requisito solo pide\nproteger los registros accedidos, no impedir inserciones nuevas. Usarlo reduce la concurrencia más de lo\nnecesario. En un examen se elige el nivel mínimo que cumple el requisito.\nC. SNAPSHOT: usa versionado de filas y no adquiere locks sobre los datos, así que otras transacciones sí\npueden modificar las filas mientras corre tu procedimiento. Si tu transacción intenta confirmar cambios\nsobre datos que ya cambiaron, se hace rollback con error. No garantiza que nadie las cambie. Además,\nrequiere activar ALLOW_SNAPSHOT_ISOLATION en la base de datos.\nD. READ COMMITTED SNAPSHOT: es una opción de la base de datos\n(READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT ON) que cambia el comportamiento de READ COMMITTED, no un\nnivel de aislamiento que se pueda fijar en la transacción con SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL.\nTampoco retiene bloqueos sobre lo leído.\nReferences\nSET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/\nset-transaction-isolation-level-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nSnapshot isolation in SQL Server (describes each level, including REPEATABLE READ and\nSERIALIZABLE): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/connect/ado-net/sql/snapshot-isolation-sql-server?\nview=sql-server-ver17\nUnderstanding isolation levels (table of side effects and the note about lost updates): https://learn.microsoft.\ncom/sql/connect/jdbc/understanding-isolation-levels?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 27, "pages": [57, 63], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p059-037", "p059-038", "p061-039", "p062-040"], "type": "yesno", "template": "The code meets the database performance requirements for partitioning.\nThe code meets the database performance requirements for JSON property querying.\nQueries that filter on $.location.heading will use the JI_VehicleTelemetry_Location index.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [1, 0, 1], "image": 40, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT -\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment -\nAzure Environment -\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupportTickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); FleetId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(256))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(2000)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusLastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n  \"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill.\",\n  \"category\": \"Battery\",\n  \"metadata\": {\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\", \"device\": \"Android\", \"language\": \"en-GB\"}\n}"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso is deploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the\nfollowing:\nAI workloads -\nVector search -\nModernized API access -\nRetrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines\nSometimes the ingestion pipeline fails due to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads.\nThe engineers at Contoso report that the following dashboard query runs slowly.\nSELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;\nYou review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports.\nSecurity Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following security requirements:\nRestrict the support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email\naddresses and phone numbers.\nEnforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which they are assigned. The\nanalysts can be assigned to multiple fleets.\nDatabase Performance and Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following telemetry requirements:\nTelemetry data must be stored in a partitioned table.\nTelemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention operations. Latitude,\nlongitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change.\nAvoid recursive updates.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing -\nContoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following requirements:\nEmbeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables.\nEmbeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\nChunking must preserve semantic coherence.\nHybrid search must combine the following:\nVector similarity -\nKeyword filtering or boosting -\nDevelopment Requirements -\nThe development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot and will\nretrieve live metadata from the databases.\nContoso identifies the following requirements for querying data in the FeedbackJson column of the\nCustomerFeedback table:\nExtract the customer feedback text from the JSON document.\nFilter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword.\nCalculate a fuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description.\nOrder the results by similarity score, with the highest score first.\nYou are creating a table that will store customer profiles.\nYou have the following Transact-SQL code."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE dbo.VehicleTelemetry\n(\n TelemetryId BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL,\n VehicleId NVARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,\n TelemetryTimeUtc DATETIME2(3) NOT NULL,\n BatteryPercent TINYINT NULL,\n SpeedKmh SMALLINT NULL,\n LocationJson JSON NULL,\n ErrorCodesJson JSON NULL,\n RawPayload NVARCHAR(MAX) NULL,\n SysStartTime DATETIME2(7) GENERATED ALWAYS AS ROW START NOT NULL,\n SysEndTime DATETIME2(7) GENERATED ALWAYS AS ROW END NOT NULL,\n PERIOD FOR SYSTEM_TIME (SysStartTime, SysEndTime),\n CONSTRAINT PK_VehicleTelemetry PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED (TelemetryId)\n)\nWITH (SYSTEM_VERSIONING = ON (HISTORY_TABLE = dbo.VehicleTelemetryHistory));\nGO\nCREATE INDEX IX_VehicleTelemetry_Time ON dbo.VehicleTelemetry (TelemetryTimeUtc);\nCREATE JSON INDEX JI_VehicleTelemetry_Location ON dbo.VehicleTelemetry (LocationJson) FOR\n('$.location.latitude', '$.location.longitude', '$.location.accuracy');"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p062-041.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ27 T02\nRESPUESTAS CORRECTAS:\nAfirmación 1 - \"El código cumple los requisitos de rendimiento de la base de datos para el\nparticionamiento.\" = NO\nAfirmación 2 - \"El código cumple los requisitos de rendimiento de la base de datos para la consulta de\npropiedades JSON.\" = SÍ\nAfirmación 3 - \"Las consultas que filtran por $.location.heading usarán el índice\nJI_VehicleTelemetry_Location.\" = NO\nEXPLICACIÓN:\nAfirmación 1 (NO):\nLa sentencia CREATE TABLE de dbo.VehicleTelemetry no tiene función de partición, ni esquema de\npartición, ni la cláusula \"ON <partition_scheme>(<columna>)\" en la tabla ni en la clave primaria\nagrupada PK_VehicleTelemetry. SYSTEM_VERSIONING (tabla temporal) no tiene relación con el\nparticionamiento — una tabla puede ser temporal y estar particionada al mismo tiempo, pero aquí\nsimplemente falta toda la infraestructura de partición. El requisito del caso de estudio \"Telemetry data\nmust be stored in a partitioned table\" no se cumple. Una implementación conforme necesitaría algo como:\nCREATE PARTITION FUNCTION PF_TelemetryTime (DATETIME2(3)) AS RANGE RIGHT FOR VALUES\n(...);\nCREATE PARTITION SCHEME PS_TelemetryTime AS PARTITION PF_TelemetryTime ALL TO\n([PRIMARY]);\nCREATE TABLE dbo.VehicleTelemetry (...) ON PS_TelemetryTime (TelemetryTimeUtc);\nNada de esto está presente, por lo que el requisito falla.\nAfirmación 2 (SÍ):\nCREATE JSON INDEX JI_VehicleTelemetry_Location ON dbo.VehicleTelemetry (LocationJson) FOR ('$.\nlocation.latitude', '$.location.longitude', '$.location.accuracy');\nEsta es la sintaxis nativa de SQL Server 2025 para CREATE JSON INDEX: CREATE JSON INDEX name\nON table_name (json_column_name) [ FOR ( sql_json_path [, ...n] ) ]. Requiere una clave agrupada en la\ntabla, que existe (PK_VehicleTelemetry PRIMARY KEY CLUSTERED). Como el índice cubre\nexplícitamente latitude, longitude y accuracy — exactamente las tres propiedades que exige el requisito —\nlas consultas que usan JSON_VALUE(LocationJson,'$.location.latitude' RETURNING FLOAT) = @lat (y de\nforma equivalente para longitude/accuracy) pueden resolverse mediante **index seek** en lugar de\nescanear/parsear el documento JSON completo. Esto satisface el requisito \"Latitude, longitude, and\naccuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\"\nAfirmación 3 (NO):\nSegún la documentación oficial de CREATE JSON INDEX, cada sql_json_path de la cláusula FOR indexa\nrecursivamente solo a partir de esa ruta en adelante, y las rutas no pueden solaparse. El índice se\ncreó únicamente FOR ('$.location.latitude', '$.location.longitude', '$.location.accuracy') — **'$.location.\nheading' no forma parte de la definición del índice**. Por lo tanto, una consulta que filtre por JSON_VALUE\n(LocationJson,'$.location.heading') **no puede usar** JI_VehicleTelemetry_Location para un seek; recurriría\na un escaneo/parseo del JSON. Para cubrir heading, el índice tendría que **recrearse** (según la\ndocumentación, \"Modification of paths requires recreating the JSON index\") incluyendo esa ruta, o crearse\nsin cláusula FOR (indexando todo el documento desde la raíz $).\nREFERENCIAS:\n- CREATE JSON INDEX (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-json-index-\ntransact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\n- JSON data in SQL Server - SQL Server 2025 changes: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-\ndatabases/json/json-data-sql-server?view=sql-server-ver17#sql-server-2025-changes\n- Index JSON data: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/json/index-json-data?view=sql-\nserver-ver17\n- sys.json_index_paths (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/system-\ncatalog-views/sys-json-index-paths-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\n- Partitioned tables and indexes: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/partitions/partitioned-\ntables-and-indexes"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 28, "pages": [63, 68], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p064-042", "p067-043", "p067-044"], "type": "yesno", "template": "The schema meets the security requirements for PII data.\nAdministrators of the Azure SQL server can see all the rows in dbo.CustomerProfiles when they use an application.\nThe masking rules will apply even when row-level security (RLS) filters out rows.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [0, 1, 1], "image": 44, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Case Study\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer your questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation about this case study, including environment and problem statements. If the case study has an\nAll Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\nAzure Environment\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupporttickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(256))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(2000)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusLastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format:\n{\n\"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill\",\n\"category\": \"Battery\",\n\"metadata\": {\n\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\",\n\"device\": \"Android\",\n\"language\": \"en-GB\"\n}\n}\nThe support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission.\nProblem Statements\nContoso is deploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the\nfollowing:\n-AI workloads\n-Vector search\n-Modernized API access\n-Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines\nSometimes the ingestion pipeline fails to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads.\nThe engineers at Contoso report that the following dashboard query runs slowly.\nSELECT VehicleId, LastUpdateUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdateUtc DESC;\nYou review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports.\nSecurity Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security requirements:\nRestrict the support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email\naddresses and phone numbers.\nEnforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which they are assigned. The\nanalysts can be assigned to multiple fleets.\nDatabase Performance and Requirements\nContoso identifies the following telemetry requirements:\nTelemetry data must be stored in a partitioned table.\nTelemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention operations.\nLatitude, longitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change.\nAvoid recursive updates.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nContoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following requirements:\nEmbeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables.\nEmbeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\nChunking must preserve semantic coherence.\nHybrid search must combine the following:\nVector similarity\nKeyword filtering or boosting\nDevelopment Requirements\nThe development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot and will\nretrieve live metadata from the databases.\nContoso identifies the following requirements for querying data in the FeedbackJson column of the\nCustomerFeedback table:\nExtract the customer feedback text from the JSON document.\nFilter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword.\nCalculate a fuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description.\nOrder the results by similarity score, with the highest score first.\nYou are creating a table that will store customer profiles.\nYou have the following Transact-SQL code."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE dbo.CustomerProfiles\n(\n CustomerId BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1) PRIMARY KEY,\n FullName NVARCHAR(200) MASKED WITH (FUNCTION = 'partial(1,\"XXXX\",1)'),\n EmailAddress NVARCHAR(200) MASKED WITH (FUNCTION = 'email()'),\n PhoneNumber NVARCHAR(50) MASKED WITH (FUNCTION = 'default()'),\n RegionCode NVARCHAR(10) NOT NULL\n);\nGO\nCREATE FUNCTION dbo.fn_FilterByRegion(@RegionCode NVARCHAR(10))\nRETURNS TABLE\nAS RETURN\n(\n SELECT 1 FROM dbo.UserRegionAccess ura\n WHERE ura.UserPrincipalName = SUSER_SNAME()\n AND ura.RegionCode = @RegionCode\n);\nGO\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY CustomerRegionPolicy\nADD FILTER PREDICATE dbo.fn_FilterByRegion(RegionCode)\nON dbo.CustomerProfiles\nWITH (STATE = ON);\nGO"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p067-045.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ28 T02\nExplanation:\n1 - The schema meets the security requirements for PII data → Sí\nEl requisito exige restringir al support staff (sin permiso UNMASK) de ver correos y teléfonos completos.\nEmailAddress usa email() (expone solo la primera letra y \".com\") y PhoneNumber usa default()\n(enmascarado total). Ambas cumplen la definición de \"PII\" del caso.\n2 - Administrators of the Azure SQL server can see all the rows in dbo.CustomerProfiles when they use an\napplication → No\nRLS se aplica a todos los usuarios, incluidos dbo/db_owner. Solo verían todas las filas si el predicado lo\npermitiera explícitamente; fn_FilterByRegion no tiene esa excepción, así que también se les filtra.\n3 - The masking rules will apply even when row-level security (RLS) filters out rows → No\nRLS filtra las filas antes de generar el resultado; DDM solo actúa sobre el conjunto de resultados ya\ndevuelto. Una fila eliminada por RLS nunca llega a ese conjunto, por lo que no hay nada que enmascarar.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/security/dynamic-data-masking?view=sql-server-\nver17#define-a-dynamic-data-mask (Dynamic data masking – Define a mask)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/security/row-level-security?view=sql-server-\nver17#permissions (Row-level security – Permissions)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security#predicate-based-row-level-security\n(Row-level security in Fabric – Predicate-based RLS)"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 29, "pages": [68, 74], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p069-046", "p071-047"], "type": "drag", "template": "CREATE TRIGGER dbo.trgMaintenanceEvents_UpdateTimestamp\nON dbo.MaintenanceEvents\nAFTER UPDATE\nAS\nBEGIN\n UPDATE m SET LastModifiedUtc = SYSUTCDATETIME()\n FROM dbo.MaintenanceEvents m\n INNER JOIN inserted i ON {{0}}\n WHERE {{1}};\nEND;\nGO", "pools": [["i.MaintenanceId IS NOT NULL", "m.LastModifiedUtc <> i.LastModifiedUtc", "m.MaintenanceId = i.MaintenanceId", "m.VehicleId = i.VehicleId"], ["i.MaintenanceId IS NOT NULL", "m.LastModifiedUtc <> i.LastModifiedUtc", "m.MaintenanceId = i.MaintenanceId", "m.VehicleId = i.VehicleId"]], "correct": [2, 1], "image": 47, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Case Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment -\nAzure Environment -\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupportTickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); FleetId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(255))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(2000)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format.\n{\n\"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill.\",\n\"category\": \"Battery\",\n\"metadata\": {\n\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\",\n\"device\": \"Android\",\n\"language\": \"en-GB\"\n}\n}\nThe support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso is deploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the\nfollowing:\nAI workloads -\nVector search -\nModernized API access -\nRetrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines\nSometimes the ingestion pipeline fails due to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads.\nThe engineers at Contoso report that the following dashboard query runs slowly.\nSELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;\nYou review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports.\nSecurity Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following security requirements:\nRestrict the support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email\naddresses and phone numbers.\nEnforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which they are assigned. The\nanalysts can be assigned to multiple fleets.\nDatabase Performance and Requirements\nContoso identifies the following telemetry requirements:\nTelemetry data must be stored in a partitioned table.\nTelemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention operations. latitude,\nlongitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change.\nAvoid recursive updates.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nContoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following requirements:\nEmbeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables.\nEmbeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\nChunking must preserve semantic coherence.\nHybrid search must combine the following:\nVector similarity -\nKeyword filtering or boosting -\nDevelopment Requirements -\nThe development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot and will\nretrieve live metadata from the databases.\nContoso identifies the following requirements for querying data in the FeedbackJson column of the\nCustomerFeedback table:\nExtract the customer feedback text from the JSON document.\nFilter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword.\nCalculate a fuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description.\nOrder the results by similarity score, with the highest score first.\nYou need to meet the database performance requirements for maintenance data.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct\ntargets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar\nbetween panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p072-048.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ29 T02\nCorrect Answer:\n- ON (m.MaintenanceId = i.MaintenanceId)\n- WHERE (m.LastModifiedUtc <> i.LastModifiedUtc)\nExplanation:\nEl ejercicio establece dos requisitos para MaintenanceEvents:\nCuando se modifique una fila, actualizar LastModifiedUtc.\nEvitar actualizaciones recursivas.\nLa primera selección importante es:\nON m.MaintenanceId = i.MaintenanceId\nMaintenanceId es la clave primaria de MaintenanceEvents. La tabla especial inserted de un trigger contiene\nlas filas afectadas por la operación UPDATE. Por tanto, relacionar ambas tablas mediante la clave primaria\npermite identificar exactamente qué fila de MaintenanceEvents corresponde a cada fila de inserted.\nMicrosoft recomienda implementar los triggers mediante lógica basada en conjuntos porque una sola\nsentencia UPDATE puede modificar varias filas.\nEs decir:\nMaintenanceEvents inserted\n----------------- --------\nMaintenanceId = 15 <--------------> MaintenanceId = 15\nMaintenanceId = 27 <--------------> MaintenanceId = 27\nPor eso no se utiliza:\nm.VehicleId = i.VehicleId\nVehicleId no es la clave primaria. Un mismo vehículo puede disponer de varios eventos de mantenimiento,\npor lo que relacionar por VehicleId podría afectar a más de una fila cuando solo se pretende actualizar el\nevento concreto que fue modificado.\nLa segunda selección esperada por el ejercicio es:\nWHERE m.LastModifiedUtc <> i.LastModifiedUtc\nLa intención de esta condición en la respuesta del ejercicio es evitar actualizaciones repetidas del\ntimestamp. El valor que se asignará es:\nSYSUTCDATETIME()\ny Microsoft documenta que SYSUTCDATETIME() devuelve un datetime2 con la fecha y hora actuales en\nUTC, por lo que es apropiado para una columna denominada LastModifiedUtc.\nNo se selecciona:\ni.MaintenanceId IS NOT NULL\nporque MaintenanceId es la PRIMARY KEY. Esta condición no identifica cambios en LastModifiedUtc ni\naporta un mecanismo para controlar actualizaciones repetidas.\nImportante: matiz técnico de este ejercicio\nAquí hay un detalle relevante de cara a estudiar correctamente SQL Server: la combinación mostrada\narriba es la respuesta esperada por este ejercicio, pero la justificación del WHERE es discutible\ntécnicamente.\nMicrosoft documenta que, en un UPDATE, primero se actualiza la tabla base y después se copia la nueva\nversión de la fila a inserted. Por tanto, en un trigger AFTER UPDATE, inserted representa el nuevo estado\nde la fila.\nLa secuencia real es:\n1. Estado antiguo → deleted\n2. Se modifica la tabla\n3. Estado nuevo → inserted\n4. Se ejecuta el AFTER trigger\nEsto significa que, en una implementación real, usar únicamente:\nm.LastModifiedUtc <> i.LastModifiedUtc\nno es un mecanismo sólido para controlar la recursividad, porque m e inserted normalmente representan ya\nel mismo estado después del UPDATE.\nMicrosoft documenta que la recursividad directa de un trigger AFTER depende de la opción de base de\ndatos RECURSIVE_TRIGGERS; con ella habilitada, un trigger que actualice de nuevo su propia tabla\npuede volver a dispararse.\nEn código de producción, un mecanismo explícito como comprobar si el propio campo LastModifiedUtc fue\nactualizado sería más claro, por ejemplo mediante UPDATE(LastModifiedUtc), función que Microsoft\nproporciona específicamente para comprobar si una columna fue objeto de un UPDATE dentro de un\ntrigger.\nReferences:\nMicrosoft Learn — Use the inserted and deleted tables\nEs la referencia principal para entender por qué se utiliza inserted, qué contiene después de un UPDATE y\ncómo se relaciona con la tabla original. Se aplica a SQL Server, Azure SQL Database y SQL Database en\nMicrosoft Fabric.\nMicrosoft Learn — Create DML Triggers to Handle Multiple Rows of Data\nSirve para justificar que un trigger debe trabajar mediante lógica basada en conjuntos y considerar que un\nUPDATE puede afectar a múltiples filas, razón por la que es fundamental relacionar correctamente\nMaintenanceEvents con inserted.\nMicrosoft Learn — SYSUTCDATETIME (Transact-SQL)\nJustifica específicamente por qué SYSUTCDATETIME() proporciona el valor adecuado para\nLastModifiedUtc: devuelve fecha y hora en UTC con tipo datetime2.\nMicrosoft Learn — Create Nested Triggers / Recursive Triggers\nEsta referencia sirve exclusivamente para el requisito “Avoid recursive updates”. Explica cuándo un trigger\nAFTER puede volver a invocarse al actualizar su propia tabla."}], "points": 2}, {"id": 30, "pages": [74, 76], "notes": ["El PDF repite las opciones dentro del texto del caso. Se mantienen en la transcripción; para responder se usan las opciones enumeradas del área de respuesta."], "sourceImages": ["p075-049"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "This is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nPNG\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nPNG\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nPNG\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nPNG\nExisting Environment -\nAzure Environment -\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupportTickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); FleetId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescript50); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(256))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(2000)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusLastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format:\nJSON{\n\"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill.\",\n\"category\": \"Battery\",\n\"metadata\": {\n\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\",\n\"device\": \"Android\",\n\"language\": \"en-GB\"\n}\n}\nThe support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission. Problem Statements -Contoso is\ndeploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the following: AI\nworkloads Modernized API access Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines Sometimes the\ningestion pipeline fails due to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads. The engineers at Contoso report\nthat the following dashboard query runs slowly: SQLSELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus,\nBatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;\nYou review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details. Requirements -Planned Changes -\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports. Security Requirements -Contoso identifies the following security requirements: Restrict\nthe support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email addresses\nand phone numbers. Enforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which\nthey are assigned. The analysts can be assigned to multiple fleets. Database Performance and\nRequirements -Contoso identifies the following telemetry requirements: Telemetry data must be stored in a\npartitioned table. Telemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention\noperations. Latitude, longitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change. Avoid recursive updates. AI Search, Embeddings, and\nVector Indexing -Contoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following\nrequirements: Embeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables. Embeddings must\nbe generated from rich natural language fields. Chunking must preserve semantic coherence. Hybrid\nsearch must combine the following: Vector similarity Keyword filtering or boosting Development\nRequirements -The development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot\nand will retrieve live metadata from the databases. Contoso identifies the following requirements for\nquerying data in the FeedbackJson column of the CustomerFeedback table: Extract the customer\nfeedback text from the JSON document. Filter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword. Calculate a\nfuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description. Order the results by\nsimilarity score, with the highest score first. QuestionYou need to recommend a solution to resolve the\nslow dashboard query issue.What should you recommend? A. Create a clustered index on\nLastUpdatedUtc. B. On FleetId, create a nonclustered index that includes LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus,\nand BatteryHealth. C. On LastUpdatedUtc, create a nonclustered index that includes FleetId. D. On\nFleetId, create a filtered index where LastUpdatedUtc > DATEADD(DAY, -7, SYSDATETIME())."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Create a clustered index on LastUpdatedUtc."}, {"id": "B", "text": "On FleetId, create a nonclustered index that includes LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, and BatteryHealth."}, {"id": "C", "text": "On LastUpdatedUtc, create a nonclustered index that includes FleetId."}, {"id": "D", "text": "On FleetId, create a filtered index where LastUpdatedUtc > DATEADD(DAY, -7, SYSUTCDATETIME())."}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ30 T02\nCorrect Answer: On FleetId, create a nonclustered index that includes LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, and\nBatteryHealth.\nExplanation:\nLa consulta es:\nSELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;\nEl plan de ejecución muestra un clustered index scan, lo que significa que no existe un índice adecuado\npara filtrar de forma eficiente por FleetId ni para cubrir las columnas del SELECT y el ORDER BY.\nLa respuesta correcta crea un índice no agrupado (nonclustered) sobre la columna de filtrado (FleetId) e\nincluye las columnas LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus y BatteryHealth. De esta forma el índice se convierte\nen un covering index: el motor puede hacer un Index Seek sobre FleetId y obtener todas las columnas\nnecesarias directamente del índice, sin necesidad de Key Lookups ni de acceder a la tabla base. Esto\nmejora notablemente el rendimiento. VehicleId se incluye automáticamente porque normalmente forma\nparte de la clave del índice clustered.\nCreate a clustered index on LastUpdatedUtc: Solo puede existir un índice clustered por tabla. Cambiarlo\nafectaría a todas las consultas y no optimiza el filtro por FleetId.\nOn LastUpdatedUtc, create a nonclustered index that includes FleetId: El filtro principal de la consulta es\npor FleetId (=). Un índice cuya clave principal es LastUpdatedUtc no permite un seek eficiente sobre\nFleetId.\nOn FleetId, create a filtered index where LastUpdatedUtc > DATEADD(DAY, -7, SYSUTCDATETIME()): El\nfiltro de los últimos 7 días no aparece en la consulta original y limitaría el índice de forma innecesaria.\nReferences:\nMicrosoft Learn: Index Architecture and Design Guide – Covering indexes y uso de INCLUDE\nMicrosoft Learn: Create Indexes with Included Columns\nMicrosoft Learn: CREATE INDEX (Transact-SQL) – cláusula INCLUDE\nDocumentación de Azure SQL Database / SQL Server sobre optimización de índices para consultas con\nWHERE + SELECT + ORDER BY"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 31, "pages": [76, 81], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p078-050", "p079-051", "p079-052"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Order the results by similarity score, with the highest score first.\nYou need to recommend a solution that will resolve the ingestion pipeline failure issues.\nWhich two actions should you recommend? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nCase Study Title (Case Study):\nCase Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment -\nAzure Environment -\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupportTickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); FleetId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(256))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(2000)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusLastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n  \"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill.\",\n  \"category\": \"Battery\",\n  \"metadata\": {\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\", \"device\": \"Android\", \"language\": \"en-GB\"}\n}"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission.\nProblem Statements -\nContoso is deploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the\nfollowing:\nAI workloads -\nVector search -\nModernized API access -\nRetrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines\nSometimes the ingestion pipeline fails due to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads.\nThe engineers at Contoso report that the following dashboard query runs slowly."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "SELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "You review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports.\nSecurity Requirements -\nContoso identifies the following security requirements:\nRestrict the support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email\naddresses and phone numbers.\nEnforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which they are assigned. The\nanalysts can be assigned to multiple fleets.\nDatabase Performance and Requirements\nContoso identifies the following telemetry requirements:\nTelemetry data must be stored in a partitioned table.\nTelemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention operations. latitude,\nlongitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change.\nAvoid recursive updates.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nContoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following requirements:\nEmbeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables.\nEmbeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\nChunking must preserve semantic coherence.\nHybrid search must combine the following:\nVector similarity -\nKeyword filtering or boosting -\nDevelopment Requirements -\nThe development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot and will\nretrieve live metadata from the databases.\nContoso identifies the following requirements for querying data in the FeedbackJson column of the\nCustomerFeedback table:\nExtract the customer feedback text from the JSON document.\nFilter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword.\nCalculate a fuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Enable snapshot isolation on the database."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Use a trigger to automatically rewrite malformed JSON."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Add foreign key constraints on the table."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Create a unique index on a hash of the payload."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Add a check constraint that validates the JSON structure."}], "correct": ["D", "E"], "type": "multiple", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "DE\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ31 T02\nAnalisis del Escenario\nEl caso de estudio indica que el pipeline de ingesta falla ocasionalmente por dos causas concretas:\nJSON mal formado (payloads que no son JSON válido).\nPayloads duplicados.\nSe necesita una solución declarativa a nivel de base de datos que rechace automáticamente ambos tipos\nde datos incorrectos en el momento de la inserción, sin modificar el código de la aplicación ni introducir\nlógica procedural frágil.\nDominio evaluado: Diseño y optimización de soluciones de base de datos (integridad de datos, restricciones\nCHECK e índices únicos en Azure SQL).\nSolucion Correcta\nCreate a unique index on a hash of the payload..\nAdd a check constraint that validates the JSON structure.\nEjemplo de implementación típica:\nSQL-- Columna calculada para el hash (persistida)\nALTER TABLE dbo.TelemetryData\nADD PayloadHash AS HASHBYTES('SHA2_256', RawPayload) PERSISTED;\n-- Índice único sobre el hash (impide duplicados)\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX IX_TelemetryData_PayloadHash\nON dbo.TelemetryData (PayloadHash);\n-- Restricción CHECK para validar JSON\nALTER TABLE dbo.TelemetryData\nADD CONSTRAINT CK_TelemetryData_RawPayload_IsJSON\nCHECK (ISJSON(RawPayload) = 1);\nJustificacion Tecnica\nLa restricción CHECK con ISJSON() se evalúa en cada INSERT/UPDATE. Si el texto no es JSON válido, la\noperación se rechaza de inmediato, eliminando los fallos por JSON mal formado.\nEl índice único sobre el hash del payload garantiza que no puedan existir dos filas con el mismo contenido.\nComo el hash es determinista y de longitud fija, el índice es eficiente y evita la comparación directa de\ncolumnas nvarchar(max).\nAmbas técnicas son declarativas, se ejecutan en el motor de base de datos y no requieren triggers ni\ncódigo de aplicación, lo que cumple el requisito de estabilidad del pipeline de ingesta.\nDescarte de Alternativas\nHabilitar aislamiento de instantáneas (snapshot isolation): solo afecta a la concurrencia y el bloqueo; no\nvalida la estructura del JSON ni impide duplicados.\nUsar un trigger para reescribir JSON mal formado: introduce lógica procedural, riesgo de actualizaciones\nrecursivas y no es el patrón recomendado por Microsoft para validación de esquema.\nAgregar restricciones de clave foránea: solo aplican integridad referencial entre tablas; no tienen efecto\nsobre la validez del contenido JSON ni sobre la unicidad de los payloads.\nReferencias Oficiales\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/isjson-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/json-features\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/json/store-json-documents-in-sql-tables\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/indexes/create-unique-indexes\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/tables/unique-constraints-and-check-constraints\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/hashbytes-transact-sql"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 32, "pages": [81, 83], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p082-053"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a Microsoft SQL Server 2025 instance that has a managed identity enabled.\nYou have a database that contains a table named dbo.ManualChunks. dbo.ManualChunks contains product\nmanuals.\nA retrieval query already returns the top five matching chunks as nvarchar(max) text.\nYou need to call an Azure OpenAI REST endpoint for chat completions. The solution must provide the\nhighest level of security.\nYou write the following Transact-SQL code."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "01 CREATE DATABASE SCOPED CREDENTIAL AzureOpenAIHeaders\n02\n03 GO\n04 CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE dbo.AskManuals\n05\n06 SELECT @chunks =\n07 ...\n08 SET @payload =\n09 ...\n10 EXEC @retval = sys.sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint\n11  @url = N'https://<your-resource>.openai.azure.com/openai/deployments/<your-deployment>/chat/completions?api-version=2024-02-15-preview',\n12  @method = N'POST',\n13  @credential = N'AzureOpenAIHeaders',\n14  @payload = @payload,\n15  @response = @response OUTPUT;\n16 END;\n17 GO"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "WITH IDENTITY = 'HTTPEndpointQuerystring',\nSECRET = N'{\"api-key\":\"<value>\"}';"}, {"id": "B", "text": "WITH IDENTITY = 'Managed Identity',\nSECRET ='{\"resourceid\":\"<value>\"}';"}, {"id": "C", "text": "WITH IDENTITY = 'Managed Identity',\nSECRET = N'{\"api-key\":\"<value>\"}';"}, {"id": "D", "text": "WITH IDENTITY = 'Managed Identity',\nSECRET = N'{\"api-key\":\"<value>\"}';"}, {"id": "E", "text": "WITH IDENTITY = 'AzureOpenAI',\nSECRET = N'{\"resourceid\":\"<value>\"}';"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ032 T02\nCorrect Answer:\nWITH IDENTITY = 'Managed Identity',\nSECRET ='{\"resourceid\":\"<value>\"}';\nExplanation:\nLa solución requiere el nivel más alto de seguridad al llamar a un endpoint REST de Azure OpenAI desde\nMicrosoft SQL Server 2025 usando sys.sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint. Managed Identity es el método\nde autenticación recomendado porque evita almacenar o transmitir secretos como claves de API.\nLa sintaxis correcta para una credencial de ámbito de base de datos que usa Managed Identity es:\nWITH IDENTITY = 'Managed Identity', SECRET = N'{\"resourceid\":\"<value>\"}';\nEsto coincide exactamente con la opción indicada. El valor de IDENTITY 'Managed Identity' indica a SQL\nServer que obtenga un token de acceso de la identidad administrada asignada por el sistema (o la\nidentidad primaria asignada por el usuario) de la instancia y lo pase en los encabezados de la solicitud. El\nobjeto JSON de SECRET debe contener la clave resourceid cuyo valor es el identificador de audiencia/\nrecurso esperado por el servicio de destino (comúnmente https://cognitiveservices.azure.com para Azure\nOpenAI).\nWITH IDENTITY = 'HTTPEndpointQueryString', SECRET = N'{\"api-key\":\"\"}';\nes incorrecta porque HTTPEndpointQueryString coloca el secreto en la cadena de consulta y espera un\nvalor de api-key. Esto es menos seguro que Managed Identity y no utiliza la identidad administrada de la\ninstancia de SQL Server.\nWITH IDENTITY = 'Managed Identity', SECRET = N'{\"api-key\":\"\"}';\nes incorrecta porque, aunque usa la identidad 'Managed Identity', el SECRET aún suministra una api-key.\nLa autenticación con Managed Identity no utiliza claves de API; el SECRET debe contener un resourceid.\nWITH IDENTITY = 'HTTPEndpointHeaders', SECRET = N'{\"api-key\":\"\"}';\nes incorrecta porque HTTPEndpointHeaders con una api-key almacena y transmite un secreto estático en\nlos encabezados de la solicitud. Esto representa un nivel de seguridad inferior al de Managed Identity.\nWITH IDENTITY = 'AzureOpenAI', SECRET = N'{\"resourceid\":\"\"}';\nes incorrecta porque el valor de IDENTITY 'AzureOpenAI' no es un tipo de identidad admitido para las\ncredenciales de ámbito de base de datos usadas con sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint. Los únicos\nvalores de identidad admitidos son HTTPEndpointHeaders, HTTPEndpointQueryString, Managed Identity y\nShared Access Signature.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-invoke-external-rest-\nendpoint-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17#credentials\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql?view=sql-server-\nver17#catalog-view"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 33, "pages": [83, 84], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named SalesDB that contains a table named dbo.Articles. dbo.Articles\ncontains two million articles with embeddings. The articles are updated frequently throughout the day.You\nquery the embeddings by using VECTOR_SEARCH.Users report that semantic search results do NOT\nreflect the updates until the following day.You need to ensure that the embeddings are updated whenever\nthe articles change. The solution must minimize CPU usage on SalesDB.Which embedding maintenance\nmethod should you implement?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Modify the query to use VECTOR_DISTANCE instead of VECTOR_SEARCH."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Enable change data capture (CDC) on dbo.Articles and use an Azure Functions app to process CDC\nchanges."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Run an hourly Transact-SQL job that regenerates embeddings for all the rows in dbo.Articles."}, {"id": "D", "text": "On dbo.Articles, create a trigger that calls AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS for each inserted or updated\nrow."}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ33 T02\nCorrect Answer:\nEnable change data capture (CDC) on dbo.Articles and use an Azure Functions app to process CDC\nchanges.\nExplanation:\nPor qué es correcta\nCDC permite capturar de forma asíncrona y eficiente únicamente las filas que han cambiado (inserciones/\nactualizaciones) en dbo.Articles, sin necesidad de escanear ni recalcular las dos millones de filas\ncompletas. Una Azure Function puede leer periódicamente (o casi en tiempo real) los cambios registrados\nen las tablas de captura de CDC y regenerar embeddings solo para esas filas modificadas.\nEsto cumple los dos requisitos del enunciado:\nActualidad: los embeddings se actualizan poco después de que el artículo cambia, en lugar de esperar al\ndía siguiente.\nMínimo uso de CPU en SalesDB: CDC tiene un overhead muy bajo sobre la base de datos (se basa en leer\nel log de transacciones), y el procesamiento pesado (llamar al modelo de embeddings) se delega fuera de\nSQL, en la Azure Function, en lugar de ejecutarse dentro del motor de base de datos.\nPor qué las otras opciones son incorrectas:\nA. Usar VECTOR_DISTANCE en lugar de VECTOR_SEARCH — Esto solo cambia la forma de calcular\nsimilitud en la consulta, pero no soluciona el problema real: los embeddings almacenados siguen sin\nactualizarse cuando cambian los artículos. No resuelve la causa del retraso.\nC. Job horario en T-SQL que regenera embeddings para todas las filas — Aunque mejoraría la frecuencia\nde actualización, regenerar los embeddings de las dos millones de filas cada hora (en vez de solo las filas\nmodificadas) consume una cantidad de CPU muy alta e innecesaria en SalesDB, incumpliendo el requisito\nde minimizar el uso de CPU.\nD. Trigger que llama a AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS en cada inserción/actualización — Un trigger\nsíncrono ejecuta el cálculo del embedding dentro de la misma transacción, en el propio motor de SQL, cada\nvez que se modifica una fila. Con actualizaciones frecuentes a lo largo del día sobre una tabla de dos\nmillones de artículos, esto genera una carga de CPU alta y constante sobre SalesDB, además de aumentar\nla latencia de cada transacción.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/change-data-capture-overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-functions/functions-bindings-azure-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/vector-search-overview"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 34, "pages": [84, 85], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a GitHub Codespaces enviroment tha has GitHub Copilot Chat installed and is connected to a\nSQL database in Microsoft Fabric named DB1. DB1 contains tables named Sales.Orders and Sales.\nCustomers\nYou use GitHub Copilot in the context of DB1.\nA company policy prohibits sharing customer Personally Identifiable Information (PII), secrets, and query\nresult sets with any AI service.\nYou are asked to use GitHub Copilot Chat to write and review Transact-SQL code for a new stored\nprocedure that will join Sales.Customers and return customer names and email addresses. The solution\nmust NOT share the actual data in the tables with GitHub Copilot Chat.\nWhat should you do?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "From Sales.Customers, paste several rows that include email addresses into a chat, so that GitHub\nCopilot Chat can infer edge cases."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Run a SELECT statement that returns customer names and email addresses and provide the result set\nto GitHub Copilot Chat so that GitHub Copilot Chat can generate the stored procedure."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Provide the database connection string to GitHub Copilot Chat so that GitHub Copilot Chat can validate\nthe stored procedure."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Ask GitHub Copilot Chat to generate the stored procedure by using schema details only."}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ34 T02\nRespuesta Correcta:\nAsk GitHub Copilot Chat to generate the stored procedure by using schema details only.\nExplicación:\nLa opción Ask GitHub Copilot Chat to generate the stored procedure by using schema details only es\ncorrecta porque Copilot puede utilizar los detalles del esquema —tablas, columnas y relaciones— para\ngenerar el código T-SQL sin necesidad de acceder a los datos reales.\nEsto permite crear el procedimiento almacenado sin compartir PII, secretos ni resultados de consultas,\ncumpliendo la política de la compañía.\n¿Por qué el resto son incorrectas?\nFrom Sales.Customers, paste several rows that include email addresses into a chat, so that GitHub Copilot\nChat can infer edge cases. Comparte filas reales de Sales.Customers, incluyendo direcciones de correo\nelectrónico, que constituyen PII.\nRun a SELECT statement that returns customer names and email addresses and provide the result set to\nGitHub Copilot Chat so that GitHub Copilot Chat can generate the stored procedure. Comparte con Copilot\nel resultado de una consulta que contiene datos reales de clientes, algo expresamente prohibido por la\npolítica.\nProvide the database connection string to GitHub Copilot Chat so that GitHub Copilot Chat can validate the\nstored procedure. Una cadena de conexión puede contener información sensible o credenciales, por lo que\nno debe compartirse con un servicio de IA.\nReferencia:\nMicrosoft Learn – GitHub Copilot con la extensión MSSQL: Microsoft Learn – https://learn.microsoft.com/\nen-us/sql/tools/visual-studio-code-extensions/github-copilot/overview?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 35, "pages": [85, 87], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p085-054"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "Authentication for sales-api: {{0}}\nNetwork access for SalesDB: {{1}}", "pools": [["Configure client certificate authentication for sales-api.", "Use a connection string with rotated SQL credentials weekly.", "Enable a managed identity and use Microsoft Entra authentication.", "Create a SQL login and store the SQL credentials in Azure Key Vault."], ["Allow Azure services and keep the public endpoint enabled.", "Create a private endpoint and disable public network access.", "Add IP firewall rules for the App Service outbound IP addresses.", "Configure a database-level firewall rule for support IP addresses."]], "correct": [2, 1], "image": 54, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT-\nYou have an Azure subscription. The subscription contains an Azure SQL database named SalesDB and an\nAzure App Service app named sales-api. sales-api uses virtual network integration to a subnet named vnet-\nprod/subnet-app and reads from SalesDB.\nYou need to configure authentication and network access to meet the following requirements:\nEnsure that sales-api connects to SalesDB by using passwordless authentication.\nEnsure that all the database traffic remains within the subscription.\nThe solution must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you configure? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p086-055.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ35 T02\nCorrect answer:\n1.Enable a managed identity and use microsoft entra authentication\n2.Create a private endpoint and disable public network access.\nExplanation:\nAuthentication for sales-api\nCorrecta: \"Enable a managed identity and use Microsoft Entra authentication.\" Una managed identity de\nApp Service permite que sales-api se autentique en Azure SQL Database con un token de Microsoft Entra,\nsin contraseñas ni secretos en la connection string, que es el requisito de passwordless. Además Azure\ngestiona el ciclo de vida de la identidad, por lo que no hay que rotar ni custodiar credenciales y el esfuerzo\nadministrativo es mínimo.\nIncorrectas:\n- \"Configure client certificate authentication for sales-api.\" La autenticación por certificado de cliente en App\nService sirve para autenticar a los clientes que llaman a la app, no para que la app se autentique contra\nAzure SQL Database. Además implicaría gestionar certificados.\n- \"Use a connection string with rotated SQL credentials weekly.\" Sigue usando usuario y contraseña, así\nque no es passwordless, y rotar credenciales cada semana aumenta el esfuerzo administrativo.\n- \"Create a SQL login and store the SQL credentials in Azure Key Vault.\" Key Vault protege el secreto, pero\nla autenticación sigue basada en contraseña, por lo que no cumple passwordless, y añade más\ncomponentes que administrar.\nNetwork access for SalesDB\nCorrecta: \"Create a private endpoint and disable public network access.\" El private endpoint asigna a\nSalesDB una IP privada dentro de la VNet. Como sales-api usa VNet integration con vnet-prod/subnet-app,\nsu tráfico saliente llega a SalesDB por esa IP privada a través de la red de Microsoft. Al desactivar el\nacceso de red público (Deny public network access) se garantiza que todo el tráfico de base de datos\npermanece privado y no puede usarse el endpoint público. Nota: crear el private endpoint no bloquea por sí\nsolo el acceso público, por eso hay que desactivarlo explícitamente.\nIncorrectas:\n- \"Allow Azure services and keep the public endpoint enabled.\" El tráfico sigue yendo al endpoint público, y\nesta opción permite conexiones desde cualquier recurso de Azure, incluso de otras suscripciones, lo que\nincumple el requisito.\n- \"Add IP firewall rules for the App Service outbound IP addresses.\" Las reglas de firewall IP siguen usando\nel endpoint público. Además las IP salientes de App Service son compartidas y pueden cambiar, lo que\nexige mantenimiento continuo.\n- \"Configure a database-level firewall rule for support IP addresses.\" Solo autoriza las IP de soporte, no\nresuelve la conectividad privada de sales-api y mantiene el endpoint público habilitado.\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/tutorial-connect-msi-azure-database\n//learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/private-endpoint-overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/connectivity-settings#deny-public-network-\naccess"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 36, "pages": [87, 90], "notes": ["Errata del PDF: la explicación escribe «git pr create»; la opción disponible y resaltada es «gh pr create». Se conserva la opción del área de respuesta."], "sourceImages": ["p088-056"], "type": "drag", "image": 56, "template": "git checkout feature/db1-add-staticdata\n{{0}}\n{{1}}\n{{2}} \\\n --title \"Feature update: DB1\" \\\n --body \"Apply latest improvements and updates for review\" \\\n --head feature/db1-add-staticdata \\\n --base main \\\n --repo <GitHubOwner>/DB1 \\\n --web", "pools": [["gh pr create", "gh pr merge", "gh pr ready", "git checkout main", "git fetch origin", "git merge origin/main", "git pull origin main"], ["gh pr create", "gh pr merge", "gh pr ready", "git checkout main", "git fetch origin", "git merge origin/main", "git pull origin main"], ["gh pr create", "gh pr merge", "gh pr ready", "git checkout main", "git fetch origin", "git merge origin/main", "git pull origin main"]], "correct": [4, 5, 0], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "DRAG DROP -\nYou have a database named DB1. The schema is stored in a GitHub repository as an SDK-style SQL\ndatabase project.\nYou use a feature branch workflow to deploy changes to DB1.\nYou need to update the local feature branch with the latest changes to main, and then create a pull request\nto merge the feature branch into main for review.\nHow should you complete the GitHub CLI script? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct\ntargets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar\nbetween panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "DRAG DROP -\nYou have a database named DB1. The schema is stored in a GitHub repository as an SDK-style SQL database project.\nYou use a feature branch workflow to deploy changes to DB1.\nYou need to update the local feature branch with the latest changes to main, and then create a pull request to merge the feature branch into main for review.\nHow should you complete the GitHub CLI script? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.\n[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p089-057.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ36 T02\nThe correct answers are:\nBlank 1: git fetch origin\nBlank 2: git merge origin/main\nBlank 3: git pr create\nSolution\nGiven that git checkout feature/db1-add-staticdata is already provided in the script (switching to the feature\nbranch), the three blanks need to accomplish:\nBlank 1 - git fetch origin\nDownloads the latest commits from the remote repository without modifying local branches\nUpdates the remote-tracking branch origin/main with the latest state\nBlank 2 - git merge origin/main\nIntegrates the latest changes from the remote main branch into the local feature branch\nEnsures the feature branch is up-to-date before creating the pull request\nBlank 3 - git pr create\nCreates the pull request using the GitHub CLI\nUses the parameters provided (--title, --body, --head, --base, --repo, --web) to configure the pull request\nWhy the other values are not used:\ngit pr merge: Used to merge an existing pull request, not create one.\ngit pr ready: Not a valid GitHub CLI command in this workflow context.\ngit checkout main: Would switch away from the feature branch, which is not needed here since you must\nstay on the feature branch to create the pull request from it.\ngit pull origin main: Combines fetch and merge into one command, but the script's design (with two separate\nblanks) indicates fetch and merge should be explicit separate steps.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Learn - Adopt a Git branching strategy\nURL: https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/devops/repos/git/git-branching-guidance?view=azure-devops\nTopic: Feature branch workflow and best practices\nMicrosoft Learn - Update code with fetch, merge, and pull\nURL: https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/devops/repos/git/pulling?view=azure-devops\nTopic: Understanding fetch, merge, and pull operations\nMicrosoft Learn - Manage repository changes by using pull requests on GitHub\nURL: https://learn.microsoft.com/training/modules/manage-changes-pull-requests-github/\nTopic: Creating and managing pull requests\nDP-800 Study Guide - Version Control with Git and GitHub\nTopic: Understanding Git workflows and GitHub CLI for database schema management"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 37, "pages": [90, 92], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains tables named dbo.ProductDocs and dbo.\nProductDocsEmbeddings. dbo.ProductDocs contains\nproduct documentation and the following columns:\nDocID (int)\nTitle (nvarchar(200))\nBody (nvarchar(max))\nLastModified (datetime2)\nThe documentation is edited throughout the day.\ndbo.ProductDocsEmbeddings contains the following columns:\nDoclD (int)\nChunkOrder (int)\nChunkText (nvarchar(max))\nEmbedding (vector(1536))\nThe current embedding pipeline runs once per night.\nYou need to ensure that embeddings are updated every time the underlying documentation content\nchanges. The solution must NOT require a\nnightly batch process.\nWhat should you include in the solution?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "fixed-size chunking"}, {"id": "B", "text": "a smaller embedding model"}, {"id": "C", "text": "table triggers"}, {"id": "D", "text": "change tracking on dbo.ProductDocs"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ037 T02\nEspañol\nRespuesta\nd. Change tracking en dbo.ProductDocs\nExplicación\nPor qué es la correcta: Change tracking es una característica del motor que registra qué filas de una tabla\nse han insertado, actualizado o eliminado desde una versión concreta. Con ella, un proceso de microlotes\n(un job programado, una Azure Function con SQL trigger binding o Logic Apps) consulta cada pocos\nsegundos qué documentos han cambiado. Solo llama al modelo de embeddings para esos documentos,\nvuelve a dividir el texto en fragmentos y actualiza dbo.ProductDocsEmbeddings. Esto sustituye al lote\nnocturno: los embeddings se actualizan de forma continua, casi en tiempo real. Además tiene muy poca\nsobrecarga, y la llamada al modelo queda fuera de la transacción del usuario que edita el documento. Ese\npunto es importante aquí, porque la documentación se edita durante todo el día y cada documento genera\nvarios fragmentos (ChunkOrder).\nPor qué las demás no:\na. Fragmentación de tamaño fijo: define cómo se divide el texto en chunks, pero no detecta cambios ni\ndispara ninguna actualización. Sigue haciendo falta un mecanismo de detección.\nb. Modelo de embeddings más pequeño: reduce dimensiones y coste, pero obligaría a regenerar todo,\nporque la columna es de 1536 dimensiones. No resuelve el problema de la frescura.\nc. Triggers de tabla: técnicamente es posible, y Microsoft lo describe como la opción más simple. Pero el\ntrigger forma parte de la transacción que modifica los datos, así que la llamada al modelo bloquea recursos\ny reduce la concurrencia. Microsoft lo recomienda sobre todo para pruebas de concepto o tablas con poco\nacceso concurrente. Con un Body grande, varios fragmentos por documento y ediciones constantes, no\nescala.\nReferencias\nMicrosoft Learn:\nVector and embeddings frequently asked questions (FAQ) (apartado \"How do I keep embeddings up to\ndate?\"): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/sql-server/ai/vectors-frequently-asked-questions?view=sql-server-\nver17#how-do-i-keep-embeddings-up-to-date\nAbout Change Tracking (SQL Server): https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/track-\nchanges/about-change-tracking-sql-server?view=sql-server-ver16\nAzure Functions SQL trigger binding: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/functions-\nbindings-azure-sql-trigger\nSQL Server:\nDatabase and AI: solutions for keeping embeddings updated (blog oficial de Azure SQL, no es Learn):\nhttps://devblogs.microsoft.com/azure-sql/database-and-ai-solutions-for-keeping-embeddings-updated/\nClave DP-800\nPara mantener embeddings al día sin lote nocturno y con mucha concurrencia, usa change tracking\n(detección incremental ligera) y no triggers que llamen al modelo dentro de la transacción.\nEnglish\nAnswer\nd. Change tracking on dbo.ProductDocs\nExplanation\nWhy it's correct: Change tracking records which rows were inserted, updated or deleted since a given\nversion. A micro-batch process (a scheduled job, an Azure Function with the SQL trigger binding, or Logic\nApps) polls for changed documents every few seconds. It calls the embedding model only for those\ndocuments, re-chunks their text, and updates dbo.ProductDocsEmbeddings. This replaces the nightly\nbatch, so embeddings are refreshed continuously, close to real time. It also has minimal overhead, and the\nmodel call stays outside the editing user's transaction. That matters here because documentation is edited\nall day and each document produces several chunks (ChunkOrder).\nWhy the others are wrong:\na. Fixed-size chunking: it defines how text is split, but it doesn't detect changes or trigger any refresh. You\nstill need a change-detection mechanism.\nb. Smaller embedding model: it lowers dimensions and cost, but the column is 1536-dimensional, so\neverything would have to be regenerated. It doesn't solve freshness.\nc. Table triggers: technically possible, and Microsoft describes them as the simplest option. But the trigger\nruns inside the transaction that modifies the data, so the model call holds locks and reduces concurrency.\nMicrosoft recommends this mainly for proofs of concept or tables with very limited concurrent access. With\na large Body, multiple chunks per document and constant edits, it doesn't scale."}], "points": 1}, {"id": 38, "pages": [92, 94], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p092-058", "p093-059"], "type": "yesno", "template": "Unit tests run automatically whenever changes are pushed to main.\nSchema validation occurs during the Build step.\nSchema validation occurs during the Deploy step.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [0, 0, 1], "image": 59, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a database named db1. The schema is stored in a Git repository as an SDK-style SQL database\nproject. The repository contains the following GitHub Action workflow."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "name: Database CI/CD\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\n  pull_request:\n    branches:\n      - main\njobs:\n  build-and-deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - name: Checkout code\n      uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Setup .NET\n      uses: actions/setup-dotnet@v3\n      with:\n        dotnet-version: '8.x'\n    - name: Build\n      run: dotnet build db1.sqlproj --configuration Release\n    - name: Deploy\n      run: |\n        SqlPackage /Action:Publish \\\n          /SourceFile:./bin/Release/db1.dacpac \\\n          /TargetConnectionString:\"${{ secrets.Target_Connection_String }}\"\n  unit-tests:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    needs: build-and-deploy\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    steps:\n    - name: Checkout code\n      uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - name: Setup .NET\n      uses: actions/setup-dotnet@v3\n      with:\n        dotnet-version: '8.x'\n    - name: Run unit tests\n      run: dotnet test UnitTests.csproj --configuration Release"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p093-060.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ38 T02\nCorrect Answer:\nUnit tests run automatically whenever changes are pushed to main. → Yes\nSchema validation occurs during the Build step. → Yes\nSchema validation occurs during the Deploy step. → No\nExplanation:\nEl job unit-tests tiene la condición if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' y depende del job build-and-deploy.\nCuando se hace push a la rama main, el workflow se dispara y los unit tests se ejecutan automáticamente.\nEl proceso de Build (dotnet build) valida las relaciones entre objetos y la sintaxis del proyecto SQL frente a\nla plataforma de destino, generando el archivo .dacpac.\nEl paso Deploy (SqlPackage /Action:Publish) aplica el .dacpac a la base de datos de destino; no realiza la\nvalidación de esquema del proyecto (esa validación ya ocurrió en el Build).\nEnglish translation of the Explanation:\nThe unit-tests job has the condition if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' and depends on the build-and-deploy\njob. When a push is made to the main branch, the workflow is triggered and the unit tests run automatically.\nThe Build process (dotnet build) validates the relationships between objects and the syntax of the SQL\nproject against the target platform, generating the .dacpac file.\nThe Deploy step (SqlPackage /Action:Publish) applies the .dacpac to the target database; it does not\nperform the project schema validation (that validation already occurred in the Build).\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/get-started?view=sql-server-ver17\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/tutorials/create-deploy-sql-project?view=sql-\nserver-ver17"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 39, "pages": [94, 94], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a Microsoft SQL Server 2025 instance that contains a database named SalesDB. SalesDB\nsupports a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pattern for internal support tickets. The SQL Server\ninstance runs without any outbound network connectivity.\nYou plan to generate embeddings inside the SQL Server instance and store them in a table for vector\nsimilarity queries.\nYou need to ensure that only a database user account named AlApplicationUser can run embedding\ngeneration by using the model.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Grant the CONTROL permission on SalesDB to AlApplicationUser."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Create a database audit specification on SalesDB owned by AlApplicationUser."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Grant the EXECUTE permission on the external model project to AlApplicationUser."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Create an external model project by using ONNX runtime and local paths."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Create an external model project that points to a Microsoft Foundry REST endpoint."}], "correct": ["C", "D"], "type": "multiple", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "CD\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ39 T02\nCorrect Answer:\nC. Grant the EXECUTE permission on the external model project to AlApplicationUser.\nD. Create an external model project by using ONNX runtime and local paths.\nExplanation:\nPara generar embeddings sin conectividad de red saliente, debe crearse un modelo externo que utilice\nONNX Runtime y rutas locales. LOCATION indica la carpeta que contiene el modelo y\nLOCAL_RUNTIME_PATH la carpeta que contiene los archivos ejecutables de ONNX Runtime. De esta\nforma, SQL Server puede ejecutar el modelo localmente sin llamar a un endpoint REST externo.\nAdemás, para que AIApplicationUser pueda utilizar el modelo externo mediante\nAI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS, debe concedérsele el permiso EXECUTE sobre ese modelo. El permiso\nCONTROL sobre toda la base de datos sería excesivo y no sigue el principio de privilegios mínimos.\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql?view=sql-server-\nver17#permissions\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql?view=sql-server-\nver17#example-with-onnx-runtime-running-locally"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 40, "pages": [94, 98], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p095-061"], "type": "drag", "template": "WITH cteBL (session_id, blocking_these) AS\n(\n SELECT s.session_id, blocking_these = x.blocking_these\n FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions AS s\n CROSS APPLY\n ( SELECT ISNULL(CONVERT(varchar(6), er.session_id), '') + ', '\n {{0}} AS er\n WHERE er.blocking_session_id = ISNULL(s.session_id, 0)\n AND er.blocking_session_id <> 0\n FOR XML PATH('')\n ) AS x(blocking_these)\n)\nSELECT s.session_id, blocked_by = r.blocking_session_id,\n bl.blocking_these, batch_text = t.text, input_buffer = ib.event_info\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions AS s\n{{1}} AS r ON r.session_id = s.session_id\nINNER JOIN cteBL AS bl ON s.session_id = bl.session_id\n{{2}} AS t\n{{3}} AS ib\nWHERE bl.blocking_these IS NOT NULL OR r.blocking_session_id > 0\nORDER BY LEN(bl.blocking_these) DESC, r.blocking_session_id DESC, r.session_id;", "pools": [["CROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)", "FROM sys.dm_exec_requests", "FROM sys.dm_tran_locks", "INNER JOIN sys.dm_tran_locks", "LEFT OUTER JOIN sys.dm_exec_requests", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, 0)", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, NULL)", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)"], ["CROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)", "FROM sys.dm_exec_requests", "FROM sys.dm_tran_locks", "INNER JOIN sys.dm_tran_locks", "LEFT OUTER JOIN sys.dm_exec_requests", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, 0)", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, NULL)", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)"], ["CROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)", "FROM sys.dm_exec_requests", "FROM sys.dm_tran_locks", "INNER JOIN sys.dm_tran_locks", "LEFT OUTER JOIN sys.dm_exec_requests", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, 0)", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, NULL)", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)"], ["CROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)", "FROM sys.dm_exec_requests", "FROM sys.dm_tran_locks", "INNER JOIN sys.dm_tran_locks", "LEFT OUTER JOIN sys.dm_exec_requests", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, 0)", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, NULL)", "OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)"]], "correct": [1, 4, 7, 6], "image": 61, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that supports an OLTP application.\nYou need to write Transact-SQL code that returns blocking chain details. The output must return only\nsessions that are blocked or are blocking other sessions.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each\nvalue may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or\nscroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p096-062.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ40 T02\nCORRECT ANSWER:\nWITH cteBL (session_id, blocking_these) AS\n(\nSELECT\ns.session_id,\nblocking_these = x.blocking_these\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions AS s\nCROSS APPLY\n(\nSELECT\nISNULL(CONVERT(varchar(6), er.session_id), '') + ', '\nFROM sys.dm_exec_requests AS er\nWHERE er.blocking_session_id = ISNULL(s.session_id, 0)\nAND er.blocking_session_id <> 0\nFOR XML PATH('')\n) AS x(blocking_these)\n)\nSELECT\ns.session_id,\nblocked_by = r.blocking_session_id,\nbl.blocking_these,\nbatch_text = t.text,\ninput_buffer = ib.event_info\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions AS s\nLEFT OUTER JOIN sys.dm_exec_requests AS r ON r.session_id = s.session_id\nINNER JOIN cteBL AS bl ON s.session_id = bl.session_id\nOUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle) AS t\nOUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, NULL) AS ib\nWHERE bl.blocking_these IS NOT NULL\nOR r.blocking_session_id > 0\nORDER BY LEN(bl.blocking_these) DESC, r.blocking_session_id DESC, r.session_id;\nEXPLANATION:\nThis is Microsoft's blocking-chain script. Microsoft's Azure SQL Database article presents it as a query that\nidentifies sessions blocking other sessions, including a list of the session_ids blocked per session_id. The\nexam version is nearly identical to the published one. The main difference is that the published version also\nreturns extra columns with *. The WHERE and ORDER BY at the end are the same as in Microsoft's Azure\nDatabase Support blog, which filters on blocking_these is not null or r.blocking_session_id > 0 and orders\nby the length of the blocking list, then by blocker, then by session. That filter is what satisfies the\nrequirement to return only sessions that are blocked or are blocking other sessions.\nIt works in two steps:\nThe CTE (cteBL) builds, for each session, a comma-separated list of the sessions it is blocking. It uses\nFOR XML PATH('') to concatenate the session_id values.\nThe main query joins that list to session and request information, and adds the SQL text (batch_text) and\nthe last command sent (input_buffer).\nWhy each blank is correct\n1. FROM sys.dm_exec_requests (inside the CROSS APPLY)\nThe subquery looks for every request whose blocking_session_id equals the current session's id, and that\ncolumn belongs to sys.dm_exec_requests. It also uses the alias er, which the WHERE clause and the\nSELECT both reference (er.session_id, er.blocking_session_id).\n2. LEFT OUTER JOIN sys.dm_exec_requests\nsys.dm_exec_requests only contains sessions that currently have an active request. A session that blocks\nothers is often idle, for example a session with an open transaction that is not running anything. With an\nINNER JOIN, those sessions would disappear, and they are usually the head of the blocking chain. The\nLEFT OUTER JOIN keeps every row from sys.dm_exec_sessions.\n3. OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text (r.sql_handle)\nWhen a session has no active request, r.sql_handle is NULL because of the left join above. OUTER APPLY\nkeeps that row and returns NULL for batch_text. CROSS APPLY would remove it, which brings back the\nsame problem as in blank 2.\n4. OUTER APPLY sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(s.session_id, NULL)\nAccording to the article, the event_info field of sys.dm_exec_input_buffer contains the last command string\npassed to the SQL engine. This is how you can see what an idle blocker last ran, even when it has no active\nrequest. Two details matter here:\n- It must use s.session_id, not r.session_id, because r.session_id is NULL when there is no request.\n- The second parameter is the request id. Microsoft's documentation shows an example that cross-applies\nthe function to sys.dm_exec_sessions and passes NULL as the request id. The other option, (r.session_id,\n0), would fail for the reason above.\nREFERENCES:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/understand-resolve-blocking?view=azuresql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/sql/database-engine/performance/understand-resolve-\nblocking\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-dynamic-management-views/sys-dm-\nexec-input-buffer-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver16\nhttps://techcommunity.microsoft.com/t5/azure-database-support-blog/can-certain-sql-user-roles-avoid-\nblocking/ba-p/3677550"}], "points": 4}, {"id": 41, "pages": [98, 99], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that supports a customer-facing API. The API calls a stored procedure\nnamed dbo.GetCustomerOrders thousands of times per hour.\nAfter a deployment that updated indexes and statistics, users report that the API endpoint backed by dbo.\nGetCustomerOrders is slower. In Query Store, the same query now has two persisted execution plans.\nDuring the last hour, the newer plan had a significantly higher average duration and CPU time than the older\nplan.\nYou need to restore the previous performance quickly, without changing the API code.\nWhich Transact-SQL command should you run?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "EXEC sys.sp_query_store_set_hints"}, {"id": "B", "text": "DBCC FREEPROCCACHE"}, {"id": "C", "text": "EXEC sp_query_store_force_plan"}, {"id": "D", "text": "ALTER DATABASE"}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ41 T02\n1. ANÁLISIS\nConcepto principal: regresión de plan de ejecución detectada en Query Store.\nServicio: Azure SQL Database + Query Store.\nEscenario: después de cambios de índices/estadísticas aparecen dos planes; el nuevo es\nsignificativamente más lento. Se requiere forzar el plan anterior de forma inmediata y sin modificar el código\nde la API.\n2. VERIFICACIÓN (MCP)\nDocumentación oficial de Microsoft Learn consultada sobre sp_query_store_force_plan y escenarios de\nregresión de planes en Query Store.\n3. RESOLUCIÓN\nC. EXEC sp_query_store_force_plan\n4. JUSTIFICACIÓN\nCorrecta – C\nMicrosoft Learn documenta que sp_query_store_force_plan permite forzar un plan concreto ya capturado\npor Query Store para una consulta determinada. Al forzar el plan antiguo (el de menor duración y CPU), se\nrestaura el rendimiento anterior de forma inmediata y sin cambiar el código de la API ni el procedimiento\nalmacenado.\nIncorrectas\nA. EXEC sys.sp_query_store_set_hints → se utiliza para aplicar hints (OPTION) a una consulta; no fuerza\nun plan de ejecución completo.\nB. DBCC FREEPROCCACHE → solo limpia la caché de planes; no garantiza que se vuelva a elegir el plan\nantiguo y puede provocar recompilaciones adicionales.\nD. ALTER DATABASE → se usa para cambiar opciones de base de datos (incluido el estado de Query\nStore), no para forzar un plan específico.\n5. REFERENCIA\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-query-store-force-plan-\ntransact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/performance/tune-performance-with-the-query-store\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/performance/query-store-usage-scenarios"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 42, "pages": [99, 101], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p099-063", "p100-064"], "type": "yesno", "template": "To avoid the explicit sort for the query, create a nonclustered index on (CustomerId, OrderDate DESC) that includes (Status, TotalAmount).\nTo eliminate the sort and make the query use an ordered seek, add CustomerId as an included column to IX_Orders_OrderDate.\nThe plan indicates a bottleneck from a suboptimal query plan, rather than locking or blocking.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [0, 1, 0], "image": 64, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT -\nYou have an Azure SQL database that has Query Store enabled.\nQuery Performance Insight shows that one stored procedure has the longest runtime. The procedure runs\nthe following parameterized query."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE dbo.GetRecentOrders\n @CustomerId int, @SinceDate datetime2\nAS\nSELECT TOP (50) o.OrderId, o.OrderDate, o.Status, o.TotalAmount\nFROM dbo.Orders AS o\nWHERE o.CustomerId = @CustomerId\n AND o.OrderDate >= @SinceDate\nORDER BY o.OrderDate DESC;"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The dbo.Orders table has approximately 120 million rows. CustomerId is highly selective, and OrderDate is\nused for range filtering and sorting.\nYou have the following indexes:\nClustered index: PK_Orders on (OrderId)\nNonclustered index: IX_Orders_OrderDate on (OrderDate) with no included columns\nAn actual execution plan captured from Query Store for slow runs shows the following:\nAn index seek on IX_Orders_OrderDate followed by a Key Lookup (Clustered) on PK_Orders for\nCustomerId, Status, and TotalAmount\nA sort operator before Top (50), because the results are ordered by OrderDate DESC\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p100-065.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ42 T02\nCorrect Anwser:\nTo avoid the explicit sort for the query, create a nonclustered index on (CustomerId, OrderDate DESC) that\nincludes (Status, TotalAmount). Yes\nTo eliminate the sort and make the query use an ordered seek, add CustomerId as an included column to\nIX_Orders_OrderDate. No\nThe plan indicates a bottleneck from a suboptimal query plan, rather locking or blocking.Yes\nExplanation:\nStatement: To avoid the explicit sort for the query, create a nonclustered index on (CustomerId, OrderDate\nDESC) that includes (Status, TotalAmount). → Yes\nCrear un índice no clusterizado compuesto:\nSQLCREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Orders_CustomerId_OrderDate\nON dbo.Orders (CustomerId, OrderDate DESC)\nINCLUDE (Status, TotalAmount);\nLa clave (CustomerId, OrderDate DESC) permite un ordered index seek (igualdad en CustomerId + rango\nordenado en OrderDate DESC).\nLas columnas incluidas hacen que el índice sea de cobertura → se elimina el Key Lookup.\nEl orden físico del índice coincide con el ORDER BY OrderDate DESC, por lo que el optimizador puede\neliminar el operador Sort.\nEsto es exactamente lo que describe la guía oficial de diseño de índices de Microsoft.\nStatement: To eliminate the sort and make the query use an ordered seek, add CustomerId as an included\ncolumn to IX_Orders_OrderDate. → No\nAñadir solo CustomerId como columna incluida al índice existente IX_Orders_OrderDate (cuya clave es\núnicamente OrderDate) no cambia el orden de las claves.\nEl seek seguiría siendo por OrderDate.\nEl filtro de CustomerId se aplicaría como predicado residual o después del Key Lookup.\nNo se obtiene un ordered seek que satisfaga tanto el predicado de igualdad + rango como el ORDER BY.\nEl Sort sigue siendo necesario.\nStatement: The plan indicates a bottleneck from a suboptimal query plan, rather locking or blocking → Yes\nEl plan capturado muestra claramente:\nIndex Seek → Key Lookup → Sort → Top.\nEsto es un clásico síntoma de plan de ejecución subóptimo causado por la ausencia de un índice adecuado\n(falta de cobertura y orden incorrecto de las claves).\nNo hay ninguna mención en el enunciado de waits de bloqueo (LCK_*), deadlocks o contención de locks.\nQuery Store / Query Performance Insight están señalando un problema de diseño de índices, no de\nconcurrency.\nReferences: Fuentes oficiales exactas utilizadas (Microsoft Learn):\nIndex architecture and design guide\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/sql-server-index-design-guide\n(secciones “General index design guidelines” y “Use included columns in nonclustered indexes” – explica\neliminación del Sort y covering indexes).\nCreate indexes with included columns\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/indexes/create-indexes-with-included-columns\nDocumentación de operadores de plan de ejecución (Sort, Index Seek, Key Lookup) referenciada desde la\nmisma guía de índices."}], "points": 3}, {"id": 43, "pages": [101, 102], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a database named DB1. The schema is stored in a Git repository as an SDK-style SQL database\nproject.\nYou have a GitHub Actions workflow that already runs dotnet build and produces a database artifact.\nYou need to add a deployment step that publishes the .dacpac file to an Azure SQL database by using the\nsecrets stored in GitHub repository secrets.\nWhat should you include in the workflow?\nenv:\nSQL_CONNECTION_STRING: Server=tcp:myserver.database.windows.net;\n...\nsteps:"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "- name: Publish\nuses: azure/sql-action@v2\nwith:\naction: publish\npath: bin/Debug/db1.dacpac\nconnection-string: ${{ env.SQL_CONNECTION_STRING }}"}, {"id": "B", "text": "- name: Publish\nuses: azure/sql-action@v2\nwith:\naction: extract\npath: bin/Debug/db1.dacpac\nconnection-string: ${{ secrets.SQL_CONNECTION_STRING }}"}, {"id": "C", "text": "- name: Publish\nuses: azure/sql-action@v2\nwith:\naction: publish\npath: bin/Debug/db1.dacpac\nconnection-string: ${{ secrets.SQL_CONNECTION_STRING }}"}, {"id": "D", "text": "- name: Publish\nrun: |\ndotnet build db1.sqlproj \\\n/p:TargetConnectionString=\"${{ secrets.SQL_CONNECTION_STRING }}\""}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ017 #Topic 2\nCorrect Answer:\n- name: Publish\nuses: azure/sql-action@v2\nwith:\naction: publish\npath: bin/Debug/db1.dacpac\nconnection-string: ${{ secrets.SQL_CONNECTION_STRING }}\nTechnical Justification:\nEl workflow ya genera el artefacto .dacpac mediante dotnet build. Para publicarlo en Azure SQL Database\nusando un secreto del repositorio de GitHub se debe usar la acción oficial azure/sql-action@v2 con:\naction: publish\npath apuntando al .dacpac generado\nconnection-string: ${{ secrets.SQL_CONNECTION_STRING }}\nEsta es la sintaxis recomendada por Microsoft para desplegar un DACPAC desde GitHub Actions.\nWhy the other options are incorrect:\nA: Usa una variable de entorno (env.SQL_CONNECTION_STRING) en lugar del secreto del repositorio\n(secrets).\nB: Usa action: extract (incorrecto; debe ser publish).\nD: Intenta usar dotnet build con /p:TargetConnectionString, que no es el método correcto ni soportado para\npublicar un .dacpac ya generado.\nOfficial Microsoft references:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/connect-github-actions-sql-db\nhttps://github.com/Azure/sql-action\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-projects-automation"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 44, "pages": [102, 104], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p103-066", "p103-067", "p103-068"], "type": "yesno", "template": "The members of SqlUsers can modify the data in SalesDB via SalesAPI.\nThe members of SqlUsers can view the data in SalesDB via SalesAPI.\nThe members of SqlUsers can change the field mappings of SalesAPI.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [1, 1, 0], "image": 68, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a Microsoft Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a SQL database named SalesDB\nand an API for GraphQL item named SalesApi.\nYou have a Microsoft Entra group named SqlUsers.\nFrom Workspace1, you assign permission to SalesApi as shown in the following exhibit:"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "SqlUsers\nAdditional permissions\n[ ] Allow sharing the GraphQL item\n[x] View and Edit GraphQL item\n[ ] Run Queries and Mutations\nNotification Options\n[x] Notify recipients by email\nAdd a message (optional)"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "From Workspace1, you assign permission to SalesApi as shown in the following exhibit:"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Choose connectivity option\nThe selected option will be used for all datasources in the API. Learn more\n(selected) Connect to Fabric data sources with single sign-on (SSO) authentication\n(unselected) Connect to Fabric or external data sources using a saved credential\nOK | Cancel"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "SqlUsers has the Viewer role for Workspace1.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p104-069.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n1. Respuesta Correcta:\nThe members of SqlUsers can modify the data in SalesDB via SalesAPI. → No\nThe members of SqlUsers can view the data in SalesDB via SalesAPI. → No\nThe members of SqlUsers can change the field mappings of SalesAPI. → Yes\n2. Explicación Técnica Detallada:\nSolo se otorgó el permiso View and Edit GraphQL item.\nNo se otorgó Run Queries and Mutations (permiso Execute).\nLa conectividad está configurada con SSO.\nSqlUsers tiene solo el rol Viewer en el workspace (Read/ReadData en SalesDB, sin Write).\nPor tanto:\nNo pueden ejecutar queries ni mutations a través de la API (falta Execute).\nNo pueden modificar datos (falta Execute + falta Write en SalesDB).\nSí pueden editar la definición del ítem GraphQL (incluyendo field mappings) gracias al permiso View and\nEdit GraphQL item.\n3. Análisis de Opciones Incorrectas:\nNo aplica (pregunta de tipo Yes/No).\n4. Referencias Oficiales de Microsoft & SQL Server:\nConnect applications to Fabric API for GraphQL\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/connect-apps-api-graphql\nCreate and add data to an API for GraphQL\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/get-started-api-graphql\nMicrosoft Fabric API for GraphQL FAQ\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-engineering/graphql-faq\nPermission model\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/security/permission-model"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 45, "pages": [104, 105], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that supports the OLTP workload of an order-processing application.\nDuring a 10-minute incident window, you run a dynamic management view query and discover the following:\nSession 72 is sleeping with open_transaction_count = 1. Multiple other sessions show blocking_session_id\n= 72 in sys.dm_exec_requests. sys.dm_exec_input_buffer(72, NULL) returns only BEGIN TRANSACTION\nUPDATE Sales.Orders. Users report that updates to Sales.Orders intermittently time out during the incident\nwindow. The timeouts stop only after you manually terminate session 72. What is a possible cause of the\nblocking?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "A long-running SELECT statement is blocking writers."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Session 72 caused a deadlock"}, {"id": "C", "text": "An explicit transaction was started but not committed or rolled back."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A lock escalation occurred."}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#45 Q19-T02\nCorrect Answer:\nAn explicit transaction was started but not committed or rolled back.\nExplanation:\nLa sesión 72 está en estado Sleeping pero con open_transaction_count = 1, y el input buffer solo muestra\nBEGIN TRANSACTION sin COMMIT/ROLLBACK. Esto indica una transacción explícita abierta y nunca\ncerrada, que retiene bloqueos sobre Sales.Orders hasta que se termina la sesión manualmente.\nWhy not the others:\nA) una SELECT larga aparecería como Running, no Sleeping.\nB) un deadlock lo resuelve el motor automáticamente.\nD) una lock escalation no deja la sesión inactiva con una transacción sin cerrar.\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-dynamic-management-views/sys-dm-\nexec-sessions-transact-sql"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 46, "pages": [105, 107], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p106-070"], "type": "yesno", "template": "You can use GETDATE() in dbo.ufn_GetTaxMultiplier to produce nondeterministic results.\nRewriting sales.mstvf_OrderStatus() as an inline table TVF will reduce the number of inconsistent execution plans.\nReplacing GetTaxMultiplier with dbo.ufn_GetTaxMultiplier in the EXEC function statement will resolve the error.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [0, 0, 0], "image": 70, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT - You have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains the following functions:\nA multi-statement table-valued function (TVF) named Sales.mstvf_OrderStatus() that returns order status\ninformation\nA scalar user-defined function (UDF) named dbo.ufn_GetTaxMultiplier (@TaxAmt money, @StateCode\nchar(2)) that returns a numeric multiplier used in tax calculations\nReporting queries frequently join Sales.mstvf_OrderStatus() to a table named Sales.SalesOrderHeader and\nreturn large result sets. A performance review shows that the queries produce inconsistent execution plans.\nDuring a code review, a developer discovers that the following Transact-SQL statement produced an error.\nEXEC @ret = ufn_GetTaxMultiplier @TaxAmt = 100.00, @StateCode = 'WA'; For each of the following\nstatements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p106-071.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nVCE 46 / 020 Topic 02\nExplanation\nYou can use GETDATE() in dbo.ufn_GetTaxMultiplier to produce nondeterministic results. → Yes\nGETDATE() es una función del sistema no determinista y está explícitamente permitida dentro de funciones\ndefinidas por el usuario (UDFs) de Transact-SQL. Al usarla dentro de la scalar UDF, la función se vuelve no\ndeterminista (puede devolver resultados diferentes en cada ejecución aunque los parámetros de entrada y\nel estado de la base de datos sean los mismos).\nRewriting Sales.mstvf_OrderStatus() as an inline table TVF will reduce the number of inconsistent execution\nplans. → Yes\nLas multi-statement table-valued functions (MSTVF) usan una estimación de cardinalidad fija (normalmente\n100 filas a partir de SQL Server 2014). Esto provoca planes de ejecución subóptimos e inconsistentes\ncuando se unen a tablas grandes. Al reescribirla como inline table-valued function, el optimizador de\nconsultas puede expandirla como una vista, usar estadísticas reales de las tablas subyacentes y generar\nplanes más consistentes y eficientes.\nReplacing ufn_GetTaxMultiplier with dbo.ufn_GetTaxMultiplier in the EXEC function statement will resolve\nthe error. → Yes\nLas scalar user-defined functions deben invocarse con el nombre de dos partes (schema.function). El\nstatement original EXEC @ret = ufn_GetTaxMultiplier ... falla porque falta el esquema. Cambiarlo a EXEC\n@ret = dbo.ufn_GetTaxMultiplier ... es la forma correcta (y documentada) de ejecutar una scalar UDF con\nla sintaxis EXEC.\nNinguna de las tres afirmaciones es falsa. Todas son verdaderas según la documentación oficial de SQL\nServer / Microsoft Fabric.\nReference\nRequisito del nombre de dos partes y ejemplo de EXEC con scalar UDF.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/relational-databases/user-defined-functions/execute-user-defined-\nfunctions?view=sql-server-ver17\nGETDATE() es no determinista y está permitida en UDFs.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/relational-databases/user-defined-functions/deterministic-and-\nnondeterministic-functions?view=sql-server-ver17\nLista de funciones no deterministas permitidas (incluye GETDATE).\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/relational-databases/user-defined-functions/user-defined-functions?\nview=sql-server-ver17\nDiferencias de rendimiento y cardinalidad entre multi-statement TVF e inline TVF.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/relational-databases/post-migration-validation-and-optimization-\nguide?view=sql-server-ver17\nCREATE FUNCTION (Transact-SQL).\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/statements/create-function-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 47, "pages": [107, 108], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named SalesDB on a logical server named sales-sql01.\nYou have an Azure App Service web app named OrderApi that connects to SalesDB by using SQL\nauthentication.\nYou enable a user-assigned managed identity named OrderApi-Id for OrderApi.\nYou need to configure OrderApi to connect to SalesDB by using Microsoft Entra authentication. The\nmanaged identity must have read and write permissions to SalesDB.\nWhich Transact-SQL statements should you run in SalesDB?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "CREATE LOGIN [OrderApi-Id] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;\nALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];\nALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];"}, {"id": "B", "text": "CREATE USER [OrderApi-Id] WITH PASSWORD = 'P@ssw0rd!';\nALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];\nALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];"}, {"id": "C", "text": "CREATE USER [OrderApi-Id] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;\nALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];\nALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];"}, {"id": "D", "text": "CREATE LOGIN [OrderApi-Id] WITH PASSWORD = 'P@ssw0rd!';\nALTER SERVER ROLE sysadmin ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];"}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nVCE 21 / 47 Topic 2\nCorrect Answer:\nCREATE USER [OrderApi-Id] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;\nALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];\nALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [OrderApi-Id];\nExplanation:\nAzure SQL Database (a single/pooled database) does not have a traditional master database that the app\nserver can log into the way SQL Managed Instance or SQL Server does, so managed identities and other\nMicrosoft Entra principals are added as contained database users directly inside the target database using\nCREATE USER ... FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER. This creates a database principal tied to the Microsoft\nEntra identity (the managed identity's display name), without requiring a server-level login. Adding that user\nto the db_datareader and db_datawriter fixed database roles grants exactly the read and write permissions\nrequired.\nCREATE LOGIN [OrderApi-Id] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER (with role assignments) is incorrect because\nCREATE LOGIN creates a server-level principal in the logical server's master database. While this is valid\nsyntax for Managed Instance, in single Azure SQL Database the recommended and required approach for\napp/managed identity access is a contained database user created directly in the user database, not a\nserver-level login.\nCREATE USER ... WITH PASSWORD = '...' is incorrect because specifying a password creates a SQL\nauthentication user, not a Microsoft Entra/managed identity-based user. Managed identities authenticate via\nMicrosoft Entra tokens, not passwords.\nCREATE LOGIN ... WITH PASSWORD plus ALTER SERVER ROLE sysadmin is incorrect on two counts: it\nuses SQL authentication (password) instead of Microsoft Entra authentication, and it grants full server\nadministrative rights (sysadmin), which violates the principle of least privilege and is not even a valid server\nrole in Azure SQL Database.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/authentication-aad-configure?view=azuresql#create-\nmicrosoft-entra-principals-in-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-user-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17#remarks\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/authentication-aad-overview?\nview=azuresql#microsoft-entra-principals"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 48, "pages": [108, 109], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a column named Notes.\nA security review discovers that Notes contains sensitive data.\nYou need to ensure that the data is protected so that neither the stored values nor the query inputs reveal\ninformation about the actual data. The solution must prevent a user from inferring relationships or repetitions\nin the data based on the encrypted output.\nWhich should you use?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Always Encrypted with secure enclaves"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Always Encrypted with randomized encryption"}, {"id": "C", "text": "row-level security (RLS)"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Always Encrypted with deterministic encryption"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 048 / 022 Topic 2\nCorrect answer: Always Encrypted with randomized encryption\nExplanation:\nAlways Encrypted with randomized encryption is the right choice because it generates a different ciphertext\nevery time the same plaintext value is encrypted. This fully hides any patterns, repetitions or relationships in\nthe data, so an observer looking at the stored encrypted values (or at query parameters that also get\nencrypted client-side) cannot infer anything about the original content.\nAlways Encrypted with deterministic encryption always produces the same ciphertext for the same plaintext.\nThat would allow an attacker to detect equal values and therefore infer relationships or repetitions, which the\nrequirement explicitly forbids.\nAlways Encrypted with secure enclaves is an enhancement that lets the database engine perform richer\noperations (range comparisons, pattern matching, etc.) inside a trusted enclave on randomized columns; it\nis not required here because the question only demands protection against inference from the encrypted\noutput, not advanced querying.\nRow-level security (RLS) only filters which rows a user can see; it does not encrypt column values and\ntherefore does not protect the sensitive data stored in Notes.\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/always-encrypted-database-\nengine\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/always-encrypted-\ncryptography\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/always-encrypted-enclaves"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 49, "pages": [109, 110], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that stores order data.\nA reporting query aggregates monthly revenue per customer and runs frequently.\nYou need to reduce how long it takes to retrieve the calculated values. The solution must NOT alter any\nunderlying table structure.\nWhat should you do?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Create a view by using ORDER BY without TOP, and then create a unique clustered index on the view."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Create a view without using WITH SCHEMABINDING, and then create a nonclustered index on the\nview."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Create a view by using GROUP BY, and then create a unique clustered index on the view."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Create a view by using WITH SCHEMABINDING, include COUNT_BIG(*), and then create a unique\nclustered index on the view."}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 49 / 023 Topic 2\nLa respuesta correcta es la D: La solución adecuada es crear una vista indexada (indexed view)\nque materialice los resultados agregados de la consulta de reporting (ingresos mensuales por cliente). Así\nse evitan los cálculos pesados cada vez que se ejecuta la consulta, sin modificar la estructura de las tablas\nbase.\nPor qué D es correcta: Los requisitos oficiales de Microsoft para crear una vista indexada en Azure\nSQL Database / SQL Server son:\n1. La vista debe crearse con WITH SCHEMABINDING.\n2. Si la vista usa GROUP BY (necesario para agregaciones como SUM de ingresos mensuales), debe\nincluir COUNT_BIG(*).\n3. El primer índice que se crea sobre la vista debe ser un unique clustered index.\nEsto materializa físicamente los resultados agregados, de forma que las consultas posteriores pueden leer\ndirectamente los datos precalculados (mucho más rápido).\nPor qué las demás opciones son incorrectas:\nA.“Create a view by using ORDER BY without TOP, and then create a unique clustered index on the\nview.”: Las vistas no pueden contener ORDER BY (salvo con TOP o OFFSET/FETCH en algunos casos\nmuy limitados). Además, aunque se pudiera, faltan los requisitos obligatorios de SCHEMABINDING y\nCOUNT_BIG(*).\nB. “Create a view without using WITH SCHEMABINDING, and then create a nonclustered index on\nthe view.”: Sin SCHEMABINDING no se puede crear ningún índice sobre una vista. El primer índice debe\nser clustered (unique), no nonclustered.\nC. “Create a view by using GROUP BY, and then create a unique clustered index on the view.”: Falta\nel WITH SCHEMABINDING (obligatorio). Falta el COUNT_BIG(*) (obligatorio cuando hay GROUP BY). Sin\nestos dos elementos, SQL Server rechaza la creación del índice.\nReferencias\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/views/create-indexed-views\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-index-transact-sql (sección sobre vistas\nindexadas)\nDocumentación relacionada de COUNT_BIG: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/count-big-\ntransact-sql"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 50, "pages": [110, 111], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a GitHub Actions workflow that builds and deploys an Azure SQL database. The schema is stored\nin a GitHub repository as an SDK-style SQL database project.\nFollowing a code review, you discover that you need to generate a report that shows whether the production\nschema has diverged from the model in source control.\nWhich action should you add to the pipeline?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "SqlPackage.exe /Action:DriftReport"}, {"id": "B", "text": "SqlPackage.exe /Action:DeployReport"}, {"id": "C", "text": "SqlPackage.exe /Action:Extract"}, {"id": "D", "text": "SqlPackage.exe /Action:Script"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 50 / 024 Topic 2\nRespuesta correcta:\nSqlPackage.exe /Action:DriftReport\nExplicación:\nA: SqlPackage.exe /Action:DriftReport (correcta)\nPor qué es la correcta:\nDriftReport crea un informe XML con los cambios aplicados a una base de datos registrada desde la última\nvez que se registró. Eso es justo \"producción ha divergido de lo que se desplegó desde el modelo\".\nmicrosoft\nEl enunciado pide un informe sobre divergencia (drift) del esquema de producción. Ninguna otra acción\nproduce un informe de ese tipo.\nSe puede ejecutar contra Azure SQL con /TargetConnectionString y /OutputPath para guardar el XML, y\nluego publicarlo como artefacto del workflow.\nRequisito: la base de datos debe estar registrada como DAC (por ejemplo, desplegada con /p:\nRegisterDataTierApplication=True).\nB: /Action:DeployReport (incorrecta)\nDeployReport crea un informe XML de los cambios que realizaría una acción de publicación.\nmicrosoft\nEs una vista previa hacia adelante (\"qué pasaría si despliego el .dacpac\"), no una comprobación de si\nproducción cambió por fuera del pipeline.\nEs la trampa principal, porque también genera un informe XML. Pero compara un .dacpac con un destino\npara planificar un despliegue, no detecta cambios no controlados.\nC: /Action:Extract (incorrecta)\nExtract crea un archivo .dacpac con el esquema (o esquema y datos de usuario) de una base de datos\nSQL conectada.\nmicrosoft\nProduce un artefacto (snapshot), no un informe. Ese .dacpac serviría como material para comparar, pero\npor sí solo no dice si hay divergencia.\nD: /Action:Script (incorrecta)\nScript crea un script incremental de Transact-SQL que actualiza el esquema del destino para que coincida\ncon el origen.\nmicrosoft\nProduce un script T-SQL de despliegue, no un informe. Sirve para revisar o aplicar cambios, no para\nreportar deriva.\nReferencias:\nPágina específica de DeployReport y DriftReport (la más importante para esta pregunta: parámetros,\npropiedades y ejemplos):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage-deploy-drift-report\nVisión general de SqlPackage (lista y descripción de todas las acciones: Extract, Publish, DeployReport,\nDriftReport, Script...):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sqlpackage/sqlpackage\nDespliegue en Azure SQL Database con Azure Pipelines (muestra la sintaxis de Extract, DriftReport y Script\ncon SqlPackage.exe /Action:..., que es el formato exacto de las opciones del examen):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/pipelines/targets/azure-sqldb"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 51, "pages": [111, 112], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the\nseries contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more\nthan one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions\nwill not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains a table named dbo.Orders. dbo.Orders has a\nclustered index, contains three years of data, and is partitioned by a column named OrderDate by month.\nYou need to remove all the rows for the oldest month. The solution must minimize the impact on other\nqueries that access the data in dbo.Orders.\nSolution: Identify the partition number for the oldest month, and then run the following Transact-SQL\nstatement.\nTRUNCATE TABLE dbo.Orders -\nWITH (PARTITIONS (partition number));\nDoes this meet the goal?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Yes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "No"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "binary", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 51 / 025 Topic 2\nAnswer: A. Yes\nExplanation:\nTRUNCATE TABLE ... WITH (PARTITIONS (n)) elimina todas las filas de la partición indicada y nada más.\nCumple el objetivo por estas razones:\nEstá soportado en esta plataforma. La sintaxis de SQL database in Microsoft Fabric (y de SQL Server 2016\n+ y Azure SQL Database) incluye WITH ( PARTITIONS ( ... ) ). Solo el Warehouse de Fabric y Synapse\ntienen una sintaxis reducida sin esa cláusula. Esta pregunta usa una base de datos SQL en Fabric, así que\nla cláusula está disponible.\nElimina solo el mes más antiguo. dbo.Orders está particionada por mes sobre OrderDate, así que cada\npartición contiene exactamente un mes. Al truncar el número de partición del mes más antiguo, solo se\nborran esas filas y los demás meses no se tocan.\nTiene un impacto mínimo en otras consultas. TRUNCATE trabaja sobre metadatos: desasigna páginas sin\nregistrar cada fila borrada en el log. Usa menos bloqueos, menos log de transacciones y menos recursos\nque un DELETE ... WHERE OrderDate < .... Ese DELETE fila a fila generaría mucho log, posible escalado\nde bloqueos, bloqueos entre consultas y fragmentación de índices.\nSe cumple el requisito de alineación. Para truncar una tabla particionada, la tabla y sus índices deben estar\nalineados (particionados con la misma función de partición). El enunciado dice que la tabla tiene un índice\nclúster y está particionada por OrderDate, por lo que el índice clúster está alineado.\nEs fácil obtener el número de partición. Puedes usar $PARTITION.<función_de_partición>(OrderDate) o\nconsultar sys.partitions (columna partition_number).\nReferences:\nTRUNCATE TABLE (Transact-SQL), con la sintaxis para SQL Server, Azure SQL Database y Fabric SQL\ndatabase, y el argumento WITH PARTITIONS: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/truncate-\ntable-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nEjemplo B, \"Truncate table partitions\": https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/truncate-table-\ntransact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17#examples\n$PARTITION (Transact-SQL), para obtener el número de partición de un valor: https://learn.microsoft.com/\nsql/t-sql/functions/partition-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nsys.partitions (Transact-SQL), para la columna partition_number: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-\ndatabases/system-catalog-views/sys-partitions-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nManage retention of historical data (patrones de switch y merge de particiones): https://learn.microsoft.com/\nsql/relational-databases/tables/temporal/manage-retention?view=sql-server-ver17#use-table-partitioning"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 52, "pages": [112, 114], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Question #26 Topic 2\nNote: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the\nseries contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more\nthan one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions\nwill not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains a table named dbo.Orders. dbo.Orders has a\nclustered index, contains three years of data, and is partitioned by a column named OrderDate by month.\nYou need to remove all the rows for the oldest month. The solution must minimize the impact on other\nqueries that access the data in dbo.Orders.\nSolution: Run the following Transact-SQL statement.\nDELETE FROM dbo.Orders\nWHERE OrderDate < DATEADD(month, -36, SYSUTCDATETIME());\nDoes this meet the goal?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Yes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "No"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "binary", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 52 / 026 Topic 2\n1. Respuesta Oficial [DP-800]\nLa respuesta correcta es la B: No.\nUn DELETE con filtro por fecha elimina las filas una a una y con logging completo, lo que genera bloqueos\ny crecimiento del log que afectan a las demás consultas sobre dbo.Orders. No aprovecha el\nparticionamiento por mes para hacer una operación de metadatos.\n2. Justificación Técnica y Arquitectura\nDELETE FROM dbo.Orders\nWHERE OrderDate < DATEADD(month, -36, SYSUTCDATETIME());\nOperación fila por fila y fully logged. Cada fila borrada genera registros en el transaction log, y también se\nmantienen los índices (el clustered index y cualquier nonclustered). Con un mes de datos de una tabla de\ntres años, el volumen es considerable.\nLocking y escalation. El DELETE toma locks de fila o página y puede escalar a un lock de tabla (lock\nescalation), bloqueando lecturas y escrituras concurrentes. Esto contradice directamente el requisito de\n\"minimizar el impacto en otras consultas\".\nNo usa el particionamiento como palanca. Aunque el predicado sobre OrderDate permite partition\nelimination para localizar las filas, el borrado sigue siendo por filas, no por partición.\nRiesgo adicional en el predicado. OrderDate < DATEADD(month, -36, ...) no acota \"el mes más antiguo\":\nborra todo lo anterior al límite, y con SYSUTCDATETIME() el límite cambia a diario, por lo que el resultado\nno es determinista respecto a los límites de partición.\nLas soluciones que sí cumplen el objetivo operan a nivel de partición (operaciones de metadatos, mínimo\nlogging y locks):\n-- Opción 1: TRUNCATE de una partición concreta\nTRUNCATE TABLE dbo.Orders WITH (PARTITIONS (1));\n-- Opción 2: SWITCH a una tabla de staging y luego eliminarla\nALTER TABLE dbo.Orders SWITCH PARTITION 1 TO dbo.Orders_Staging;\nDROP TABLE dbo.Orders_Staging;\nSWITCH requiere que la tabla de staging tenga la misma estructura, índices y filegroup, y un CHECK\nconstraint compatible con el rango de la partición.\n3. Desglose de Opciones\nA. Yes (incorrecta): Funcionalmente eliminaría datos antiguos, pero no cumple la restricción clave\nde minimizar el impacto sobre otras consultas, por el logging masivo, los locks y el riesgo de lock\nescalation.\nB. No (correcta): El DELETE masivo no es la técnica adecuada para retirar una partición completa.\nLo apropiado es TRUNCATE TABLE ... WITH (PARTITIONS (n)) o ALTER TABLE ... SWITCH\nPARTITION.\n4. Referencias Oficiales de Microsoft Learn\nTRUNCATE TABLE (Transact-SQL), incluye la cláusula WITH (PARTITIONS ...): https://learn.microsoft.\ncom/sql/t-sql/statements/truncate-table-transact-sql\nALTER TABLE (Transact-SQL), incluye SWITCH PARTITION: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/\nstatements/alter-table-transact-sql\nPartitioned tables and indexes: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/partitions/partitioned-\ntables-and-indexes\nResolve blocking problems / lock escalation: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/sql-server-\ntransaction-locking-and-row-versioning-guide\nOjo: en esta serie de preguntas suele aparecer la variante con TRUNCATE TABLE ... WITH\n(PARTITIONS ...) o con SWITCH PARTITION como solución correcta, así que si la ves con \"Yes\" ahí\ncuadra con lo anterior."}], "points": 1}, {"id": 53, "pages": [114, 115], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the\nseries contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more\nthan one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions\nwill not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains a table named dbo.Orders. dbo.Orders has a\nclustered index, contains three years of data, and is partitioned by a column named OrderDate by month.\nYou need to remove all the rows for the oldest month. The solution must minimize the impact on other\nqueries that access the data in dbo.Orders.\nSolution: Identify the partition scheme for the oldest month, and then run the following Transact-SQL\nstatement.\nALTER TABLE dbo.Orders -\nDROP PARTITION SCHEME (partition_scheme_name);\nDoes this meet the goal?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Yes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "No"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "binary", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 53 / 027 Topic 2\nCorrect Answer:\nB. No\nExplanation:\nALTER TABLE ... DROP PARTITION SCHEME no es T-SQL válido. DROP PARTITION SCHEME es una\nsentencia independiente.\nUn esquema de partición solo asigna particiones a filegroups y no contiene filas. Además, no se puede\neliminar mientras la tabla lo use, así que daría error.\nEl esquema cubre toda la tabla, no solo el mes más antiguo, por lo que no cumple el objetivo ni minimiza el\nimpacto.\nLo correcto sería TRUNCATE TABLE ... WITH (PARTITIONS (n)) o SWITCH PARTITION seguido de\nMERGE RANGE.\nReferences:\nReferencias (Microsoft Learn)\nDROP PARTITION SCHEME (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/drop-\npartition-scheme-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nTRUNCATE TABLE (Transact-SQL), argumento WITH (PARTITIONS ...): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-\nsql/statements/truncate-table-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nPartitioned tables and indexes: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/partitions/partitioned-\ntables-and-indexes?view=sql-server-ver17\nManage retention of historical data in system-versioned temporal tables (patrón de ventana deslizante con\nSWITCH PARTITION y MERGE RANGE): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/tables/\ntemporal/manage-retention?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 54, "pages": [115, 116], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the\nseries contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more\nthan one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions\nwill not appear in the review screen.\nYou have an SDK-style SQL database project stored in a Git repository. The project targets an Azure SQL\ndatabase.\nThe CI build fails with unresolved reference errors when the project references system objects.\nYou need to update the SQL database project to ensure that dotnet build validates successfully by including\nthe correct system objects in the database model for Azure SQL Database.\nSolution: Add the Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master NuGet package to the project.\nDoes this meet the goal?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Yes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "No"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "binary", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 534 / 028 Topic 2\nCorrect answer:\na. Yes\nExplanation:\nSí, la solución cumple el objetivo.\nEl punto fundamental del ejercicio es que los SQL Database Projects validan las referencias a objetos\ndurante la compilación. De forma predeterminada, los objetos del sistema no están incluidos en el modelo\ndel proyecto, por lo que una referencia a un objeto del sistema puede provocar errores de referencias no\nresueltas durante dotnet build. Microsoft indica que estos errores se solucionan incorporando una\nreferencia al master.dacpac correspondiente a la plataforma de destino.\nEn este caso, el proyecto cumple dos condiciones importantes:\n1. Es un proyecto SQL SDK-style.\nLos proyectos basados en Microsoft.Build.Sql permiten utilizar paquetes NuGet como referencias de base\nde datos. Microsoft señala además que las referencias mediante paquetes son el método recomendado\npara nuevos desarrollos y que esta funcionalidad de NuGet está disponible para proyectos SQL SDK-style.\n2. El destino es Azure SQL Database.\nPor tanto, no debemos utilizar el master.dacpac genérico de SQL Server. Microsoft proporciona\nespecíficamente el paquete:\nMicrosoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master\nEste paquete contiene el modelo de objetos del sistema de master correspondiente a Azure SQL Database.\nMicrosoft proporciona incluso el siguiente comando como ejemplo para añadirlo:\ndotnet add <path-to-sqlproj> package Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master\nEsto genera una referencia equivalente a:\n<ItemGroup>\n<PackageReference\nInclude=\"Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master\"\nVersion=\"...\" />\n</ItemGroup>\nDe esta manera, durante dotnet build, el modelo del proyecto dispone de las definiciones de los objetos del\nsistema necesarias para validar las referencias.\nPor qué \"No\" es incorrecto: rechazar la solución implicaría que Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master\nno es la referencia apropiada para incorporar los objetos del sistema de Azure SQL Database. Sin\nembargo, la documentación oficial de Microsoft identifica explícitamente ese paquete para este escenario.\nHay además una distinción importante para no confundir este ejercicio con Microsoft Fabric:\nAzure SQL Database\n↓\nMicrosoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master\nSQL Database in Microsoft Fabric\n↓\nMicrosoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.DbFabric\nMicrosoft documenta Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.DbFabric para los objetos del sistema de SQL Database\nin Fabric. Como el enunciado especifica Azure SQL Database, Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master\nes el paquete correspondiente.\nReferences:\nMicrosoft Learn — SQL Projects System Objects\nEs la referencia principal para este ejercicio. Explica exactamente el problema descrito: los objetos del\nsistema no se incluyen por defecto en el modelo, esto puede provocar errores de validación y, para Azure\nSQL Database, se puede añadir Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/system-objects?view=sql-server-\nver17\nMicrosoft Learn — SQL Projects Package References\nConfirma que los paquetes de bases de datos del sistema contienen sus esquemas y pueden utilizarse\ncomo referencias en proyectos SQL. También establece que las referencias NuGet están soportadas en\nproyectos SQL SDK-style.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/package-references?view=sql-\nserver-ver17\nMicrosoft Learn — What are SQL Database Projects?\nDocumenta que Microsoft.Build.Sql es el SDK utilizado por los proyectos SQL SDK-style y que el proceso\nde compilación valida las referencias entre objetos y genera un .dacpac.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/sql-database-projects?view=sql-server-\nver17"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 55, "pages": [116, 117], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the\nseries contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more\nthan one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions\nwill not appear in the review screen.\nYou have an SDK-style SQL database project stored in a Git repository. The project targets an Azure SQL\ndatabase.\nThe CI build fails with unresolved reference errors when the project references system objects.\nYou need to update the SQL database project to ensure that dotnet build validates successfully by including\nthe correct system objects in the database model for Azure SQL Database.\nSolution: Add the Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Master NuGet package to the project.\nDoes this meet the goal?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Yes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "No"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "binary", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 55 / 029 Topic 2\n2. Correct Answer:\nb.No\n3. Explanation:\nLa respuesta correcta es No.\nEl proyecto SQL es de estilo SDK y tiene como plataforma de destino Azure SQL Database. Microsoft\nLearn indica que, durante la compilación, los proyectos SQL validan las referencias a objetos de base de\ndatos. De forma predeterminada, los objetos del sistema no están incluidos en el modelo, lo que puede\nprovocar errores de referencias no resueltas cuando el proyecto utiliza dichos objetos. Microsoft Learn\nPara solucionar estos errores, se debe incluir una referencia al master.dacpac correspondiente\nespecíficamente a la plataforma de destino del proyecto. En el caso de Azure SQL Database, Microsoft\nindica que debe utilizarse el paquete NuGet Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master. Microsoft Learn\nLa solución propuesta utiliza Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Master, pero Microsoft Learn identifica este\npaquete como el correspondiente a la base de datos master de SQL Server. Por ejemplo, la documentación\nlo muestra como el paquete master de SQL Server 2022. Microsoft Learn\nPor tanto, Yes es incorrecta porque el paquete propuesto corresponde a SQL Server y no al master de\nAzure SQL Database. No es la respuesta correcta porque, para este escenario, debería agregarse\nMicrosoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master.\n4. References:\nMicrosoft Learn — SQL Projects System Objects:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/system-objects?view=sql-server-\nver17\nMicrosoft Learn — SQL Projects Package References:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/package-references?view=sql-\nserver-ver17"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 56, "pages": [117, 118], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an SDK-style SQL database project stored in a Git repository. The project targets an Azure SQL\ndatabase.\nThe CI build fails with unresolved reference errors when the project references system objects.\nYou need to update the SQL database project to ensure that dotnet build validates successfully by including\nthe correct system objects in the database model for Azure SQL Database.\nSolution: Add an artifact reference to the Azure SQL Database master.dacpac file.\nDoes this meet the goal?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Yes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "No"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "binary", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 56 / 030 Topic 2\nRespuesta Correcta:\nNo\nExplicación:\nLa solución propuesta (añadir una referencia de artefacto al archivo master.dacpac de Azure SQL\nDatabase) no cumple el objetivo de forma fiable en un proyecto SQL de estilo SDK.\nEn proyectos SQL de estilo SDK (Microsoft.Build.Sql), la forma recomendada y portable de incluir los\nobjetos del sistema de Azure SQL Database es mediante una referencia de paquete NuGet:\ndotnet add package Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master\nUna ArtifactReference depende de una ruta local específica de Visual Studio (por ejemplo,\n$(DacPacRootPath)\\Extensions\\Microsoft\\SQLDB\\Extensions\\SqlServer\\AzureV12\\SqlSchemas\\master.\ndacpac). Esa ruta no está disponible de forma consistente en entornos de CI/CD (como GitHub Actions o\nAzure DevOps) donde solo se ejecuta dotnet build, por lo que el build seguiría fallando con errores de\nreferencias no resueltas a objetos del sistema.\nPor tanto, esta solución no garantiza que dotnet build valide correctamente el modelo de base de datos\npara Azure SQL Database.\nReferencias:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/system-objects\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/package-references"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 57, "pages": [118, 123], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p120-072", "p121-073", "p122-074"], "type": "yesno", "template": "Applications can read data in the Procedures table without authentication.\nApplications can read and update data in the Transactions table once authenticated.\nApplications can execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [0, 1, 0], "image": 74, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Case Study\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete al questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\n-\nTo display the first question in this case study, dick the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, dick the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\n-\nAzure Environment\nFabrikam has a single Azure subscription in the East US 2 Azure region. The subscription contains an\nAzure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains the flowing tables:\n• Patients\n• Employees\n• Procedures\n• Transactions\n• UsefulPrompts\n• ProcedureDocuments\nYou store a column master key as a secret in Azure Key Vault.\nYou have an on-premises application named TransactionProcessing that uses a hard-coded username and\npassword in a connection string to access DB1.\nProblem Statements\nUsers report that after executing a long-running stored procedure named sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient,\nupdates to the underlying data are sometimes inconsistent.\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nFabrikam plans to manage all changes to Azure SQL Database objects by using source control in GitHub.\nEvery pull request submitted to production will be validated before it can be merged. Deployments must use\nthe Release configuration.\nSecurity Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the folowing security requirements:\n• The TransactionProcessing application must use a passwordless connection to DB1. • The Employees\ntable contains two columns named TaxID and Salary that must be encrypted at rest. • Auditors must have a\ntamper-evident history of transactions with cryptographic proof of changes to the employee data.\nDatabase Performance Requirements Records accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must\nNOT be changed by other transactions while the stored procedure runs. Al Search, Embeddings, and Vector\nIndexing Fabrikam identifies the folowing Al-related requirements:\n• Queries to the ProcedureDocuments table must use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n• Users must be able to query the data in DB1 by using prompts in Copilot in Microsoft Fabric.\n• The UsefulPrompts table will store prompts that doctors can use to help diagnose patient illness by\nconnecting to an Azure OpenAI endpoint.\nDevelopment Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the folowing development requirements:\n• Provide the functionality to retrieve all the transactions of a given patient between two dates, showing a\nrunning total.\n• Expose a Data API builder (DAB) configuration file to enable Azure services to perform the following\noperations over a REST API:\no Read data from the procedures table without authentication.\no Read and insert data into the Transactions table once authenticated.\no Execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure.\n• Provide the functionality to retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical procedures\nduring the last 30 days.\n• Information for each medical procedure wil be stored in a table. The table will be used with a large\nlanguage model (LLM) for user querying and wil have the following structure."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE dbo.ProcedureDocuments\n(\n DocumentId INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n SourceId NVARCHAR(200) NULL,\n Content NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,\n Embedding VECTOR(1536) NOT NULL,\n CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\n);"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "You create a DAB configuration file that meets the development requirements for DB1 and includes the\nfollowing entities:"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n \"entities\": {\n  \"Procedures\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Procedures\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"]}]\n  },\n  \"Transactions\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Transactions\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"read\", \"create\"]}]\n  },\n  \"UpdateProcedurePatient\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\",\n   \"rest\": {\"enabled\": true, \"method\": \"post\", \"path\": \"/procedurepatient\"},\n   \"graphql\": false,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"execute\"]}]\n  }\n }\n}"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "You are evaluating the DAB configuration.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p122-075.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 057 / 031 Topic 02\nCorrect Answer:\nYes\nNo\nYes\nExplanation:\n1. Applications can read data in the Procedures table without authentication. → Yes (Verdadera)\nEn la configuración DAB, la entidad Procedures tiene el permiso:\n\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [ \"read\" ].\nEl rol anonymous permite acceso sin autenticación. Por eso las aplicaciones pueden leer la tabla\nProcedures sin credenciales.\n2. Applications can read and update data in the Transactions table once authenticated. → No (Falsa)\nLa entidad Transactions tiene el permiso:\n\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [ \"read\", \"create\" ].\nread permite lectura.\ncreate permite solo inserción (INSERT).\nNo existe la acción update. Por lo tanto, las aplicaciones autenticadas no pueden actualizar datos; solo leer\ne insertar. La afirmación es falsa porque dice “read and update”.\n3. Applications can execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure. → Yes (Verdadera)\nLa entidad UpdateProcedurePatient (que apunta a dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient) tiene:\n\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [ \"execute\" ]\ny está habilitada en REST con método POST.\nLas aplicaciones autenticadas pueden ejecutar el procedimiento almacenado.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authorization-overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities#permissions-entity-name-entities"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 58, "pages": [123, 131], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p124-076", "p125-077", "p126-078", "p127-079", "p128-080", "p128-081", "p129-082"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "CREATE {{0}} dbo.CustomerTransactionsBetweenDates\n(\n @CustomerId INT,\n @StartDate DATETIME2,\n @EndDate DATETIME2\n)\nRETURNS TABLE\nAS\nRETURN\n(\n SELECT t.TransactionId, t.CustomerId, t.TransactionDate, t.Amount,\n SUM(t.Amount) OVER (\n {{1}}\n {{2}}\n ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n ) AS RunningTotal\n FROM dbo.Transactions AS t\n WHERE t.CustomerId = @CustomerId\n AND t.TransactionDate >= @StartDate\n AND t.TransactionDate <= @EndDate\n);", "pools": [["FUNCTION", "PROCEDURE", "VIEW"], ["GROUP BY t.CustomerId", "GROUP BY t.TransactionDate", "PARTITION BY t.CustomerId", "PARTITION BY t.TransactionDate"], ["HAVING DATEDIFF(DAY, t.TransactionDate, GETDATE()) = 1", "HAVING SUM(t.Amount) > 0", "ORDER BY t.TransactionDate, t.TransactionId", "ORDER BY t.TransactionId, t.CustomerId"]], "correct": [0, 2, 2], "image": 82, "body": [{"kind": "transcription", "text": "HOTSPOT\nCase Study\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You must manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the time provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information about the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions in this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers and to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you cannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to explore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays information such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study has an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the subsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the question.\nExisting Environment\nAzure Environment"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Fabrikam has a single Azure subscription in the East US 2 Azure region. The subscription contains an Azure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains the following tables:\n• Patients\n• Employees\n• Procedures\n• Transactions\n• UsefulPrompts\n• ProcedureDocuments\nYou store a column master key as a secret in Azure Key Vault.\nYou have an on-premises application named TransactionProcessing that uses a hard-coded username and password in a connection string to access DB1.\nProblem Statements\nUsers report that after executing a long-running stored procedure named sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient, updates to the underlying data are "}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "sometimes inconsistent.\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nFabrikam plans to manage all changes to Azure SQL Database objects by using source control in GitHub. Every pull request submitted to production will be validated before it can be merged. Deployments must use the Release configuration.\nSecurity Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the following security requirements:\n• The TransactionProcessing application must use a passwordless connection to DB1.\n• The Employees table contains two columns named TaxID and Salary that must be encrypted at rest.\n• Auditors must have a tamper-evident history of transactions with cryptographic proof of changes to the employee data.\nDatabase Performance Requirements\nRecords accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must NOT be changed by other transactions while the stored procedure runs.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nFabrikam identifies the following AI-related requirements:\n• Queries to the ProcedureDocuments table must use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n• Users must be able to query the data in DB1 by using prompts in Copilot in Microsoft Fabric.\n• The UsefulPrompts table will store prompts that doctors can use to help diagnose patient illness by connecting to an Azure OpenAI endpoint.\n"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Development Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the following development requirements:\n• Provide the functionality to retrieve all the transactions of a given patient between two dates, showing a running total.\n• Expose a Data API builder (DAB) configuration file to enable Azure services to perform the following operations over a REST API:\n o Read data from the procedures table without authentication.\n o Read and insert data into the Transactions table once authenticated.\n o Execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure.\n• Provide the functionality to retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical procedures during the last 30 days.\n• Information for each medical procedure will be stored in a table. The table will be used with a large language model (LLM) for user querying and will have the following structure.\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ProcedureDocuments\n(\n DocumentId INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n SourceId NVARCHAR(200) NULL,\n Content NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,\n Embedding VECTOR(1536) NOT NULL,\n CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\n);"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n \"entities\": {\n  \"Procedures\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Procedures\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"]}]\n  },\n  \"Transactions\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Transactions\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"read\", \"create\"]}]\n  },\n  \"UpdateProcedurePatient\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\",\n   \"rest\": {\"enabled\": true, \"method\": \"post\", \"path\": \"/procedurepatient\"},\n   \"graphql\": false,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"execute\"]}]\n  }\n }\n}"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "You need to create a solution that meets the development requirements for retrieving the patient transaction data. The solution must ensure that database developers use the resulting data to join to other tables.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p130-083.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nVCE 58 / 032 Topic 2\nRespuesta correcta:\nCREATE FUNCTION dbo.CustomerTransactionsBetweenDates. Dentro de OVER, usar PARTITION BY t.\nCustomerId seguido de ORDER BY t.TransactionDate, t.TransactionId.\nExplicación:\nEl código recibe parámetros (@CustomerId, @StartDate, @EndDate) y usa RETURNS TABLE AS\nRETURN (SELECT ...), que es la sintaxis de una función con valor de tabla en línea (inline table-valued\nfunction), por lo que el objeto debe crearse con CREATE FUNCTION. Después se consulta como una\ntabla: SELECT * FROM dbo.CustomerTransactionsBetweenDates(...).\nPara el total acumulado, PARTITION BY t.CustomerId divide las filas en una ventana por cliente sin\ncolapsarlas, de modo que cada transacción se mantiene en el resultado y RunningTotal se reinicia en cada\ncliente. El marco ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW requiere un\nORDER BY dentro de OVER. Ordenar por t.TransactionDate hace que el total se acumule\ncronológicamente, y t.TransactionId actúa como desempate para que las transacciones de la misma fecha\nproduzcan siempre un total acumulado determinista.\nCREATE PROCEDURE es incorrecta porque un procedimiento almacenado no puede usarse en la\ncláusula FROM de una consulta y no utiliza la sintaxis RETURNS TABLE AS RETURN (...). CREATE VIEW\nes incorrecta porque una vista no acepta parámetros, y este objeto tiene tres (@CustomerId, @StartDate,\n@EndDate).\nGROUP BY t.CustomerId y GROUP BY t.TransactionDate son incorrectas porque GROUP BY no es válido\ndentro de una cláusula OVER (error de sintaxis), y agrupar colapsaría las filas en lugar de conservar cada\ntransacción. PARTITION BY t.TransactionDate es incorrecta porque el total acumulado se reiniciaría en\ncada fecha en lugar de acumularse por cliente.\nHAVING DATEDIFF(DAY, t.TransactionDate, GETDATE()) = 1 y HAVING SUM(t.Amount) > 0 son\nincorrectas porque HAVING filtra grupos después de un GROUP BY y no es válido dentro de OVER;\nninguna aporta el ORDER BY que exige el marco ROWS. ORDER BY t.TransactionId, t.CustomerId es\nincorrecta porque ordena primero por TransactionId, por lo que el total no se acumula en orden de fecha, y\nCustomerId sobra porque ya está en el PARTITION BY.\nReferencias:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-over-clause-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-function-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-procedure-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-view-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-group-by-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/queries/select-having-transact-sql"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 59, "pages": [131, 137], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p132-084", "p133-085", "p134-086", "p135-087", "p136-088"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "What should you include in your recommendation?"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Case Study -\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You must manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the time provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information about the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions in this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers and to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you cannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study -\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to explore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays information such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study has an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the subsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the question.\nExisting Environment -"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Azure Environment -\nFabrikam has a single Azure subscription in the East US 2 Azure region. The subscription contains an Azure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains the following tables:\n• Patients\n• Employees\n• Procedures\n• Transactions\n• UsefulPrompts\n• ProcedureDocuments\nYou store a column master key as a secret in Azure Key Vault.\nYou have an on-premises application named TransactionProcessing that uses a hard-coded username and password in a connection string to access DB1.\nProblem Statements\nUsers report that after executing a long-running stored procedure named sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient, updates to the underlying data are sometimes inconsistent.\nRequirements -\nPlanned Changes -\nFabrikam plans to manage all changes to Azure SQL Database objects by using source control in GitHub. Every pull request submitted to"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "production will be validated before it can be merged. Deployments must use the Release configuration.\nSecurity Requirements -\nFabrikam identifies the following security requirements:\n• The TransactionProcessing application must use a passwordless connection to DB1.\n• The Employees table contains two columns named TaxID and Salary that must be encrypted at rest.\n• Auditors must have a tamper-evident history of transactions with cryptographic proof of changes to the employee data.\nDatabase Performance Requirements\nRecords accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must NOT be changed by other transactions while the stored procedure runs.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nFabrikam identifies the following AI-related requirements:\n• Queries to the ProcedureDocuments table must use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n• Users must be able to query the data in DB1 by using prompts in Copilot in Microsoft Fabric.\n• The UsefulPrompts table will store prompts that doctors can use to help diagnose patient illness by connecting to an Azure OpenAI endpoint.\nDevelopment Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the following development requirements:"}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "• Provide the functionality to retrieve all the transactions of a given patient between two dates, showing a running total.\n• Expose a Data API builder (DAB) configuration file to enable Azure services to perform the following operations over a REST API:\n o Read data from the procedures table without authentication.\n o Read and insert data into the Transactions table once authenticated.\n o Execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure.\n• Provide the functionality to retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical procedures during the last 30 days.\n• Information for each medical procedure will be stored in a table. The table will be used with a large language model (LLM) for user querying and will have the following structure.\nCREATE TABLE dbo.ProcedureDocuments\n(\n DocumentId INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n SourceId NVARCHAR(200) NULL,\n Content NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,\n Embedding VECTOR(1536) NOT NULL,\n CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\n);\nDAB -\nYou create a DAB configuration file that meets the development requirements for DB1 and includes the following entities."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n \"entities\": {\n  \"Procedures\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Procedures\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"]}]\n  },\n  \"Transactions\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Transactions\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"read\", \"create\"]}]\n  },\n  \"UpdateProcedurePatient\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\",\n   \"rest\": {\"enabled\": true, \"method\": \"post\", \"path\": \"/procedurepatient\"},\n   \"graphql\": false,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"execute\"]}]\n  }\n }\n}\nYou need to recommend a solution for the TransactionProcessing application that meets the security requirements."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "a service principal"}, {"id": "B", "text": "a system-assigned managed identity"}, {"id": "C", "text": "a shared access key"}, {"id": "D", "text": "a user-assigned managed identity"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#VCE 59 / 033 Topic 02\nRespuesta Correcta:\na service principal\nExplicación:\na service principal es correcta porque el caso de estudio establece que Transaction Processing es una\naplicación local (on-premises). Para cumplir con el requisito de seguridad de utilizar una conexión sin\ncontraseña (passwordless) hacia la base de datos DB1 desde un entorno externo a Azure, la aplicación\ndebe registrarse en Microsoft Entra ID, lo cual genera una entidad de servicio (Service Principal) que puede\nautenticarse de forma segura (por ejemplo, mediante certificados).\n¿Por qué el resto son incorrectas?\na system-assigned managed identity: Las identidades administradas asignadas por el sistema están\ndiseñadas exclusivamente para integrarse con recursos que se ejecutan de forma nativa dentro de Azure\n(como Máquinas Virtuales de Azure, App Services, etc.) o en servidores locales habilitados con Azure Arc.\nPuesto que la aplicación Transaction Processing es on-premises y no hay mención a Azure Arc en el\nescenario, el servidor no puede solicitar ni utilizar una identidad administrada.\na shared access key: Las claves de acceso compartido (Shared Access Keys) no son un método válido\npara la autenticación de usuarios o aplicaciones en Azure SQL Database (son propias de servicios como\nAzure Storage). Además, incrustar una clave compartida sería funcionalmente equivalente a usar una\ncontraseña estática, lo que incumpliría el requerimiento explícito de usar una conexión \"passwordless\".\na user-assigned managed identity: Al igual que la opción a system-assigned managed identity, las\nidentidades administradas asignadas por el usuario requieren que el recurso de cómputo subyacente esté\nen Azure o gestionado por Azure Arc para poder asignarles dicha identidad. No es un mecanismo que se\npueda configurar para una aplicación on-premises tradicional.\nReferencia:Microsoft Learn – Autenticación de Microsoft Entra con Azure SQL Database (Entidades de\nservicio): https://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/azure/azure-sql/database/authentication-aad-service-principal-\ntutorial"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 60, "pages": [137, 143], "notes": ["Incoherencia del PDF: el caso pide validar antes de fusionar, pero tanto la solución resaltada como la justificación establecen push. Se conserva la clave del PDF."], "sourceImages": ["p139-089", "p140-090", "p141-091"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "name: Validate SQL Project\non:\n {{0}}:\n  branches: [\"main\"]\njobs:\n validate:\n  runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n  steps:\n  - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n  - name: Step 1\n    run: dotnet {{1}}", "pools": [["merge", "pull_request", "push"], ["build Database.sqlproj -c Release", "pack Database.sqlproj", "publish Database.sqlproj -c Production", "publish Database.sqlproj -c Release"]], "correct": [2, 0], "image": 91, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\n-\nCase Study\n-\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\n-\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\n-\nAzure Environment\n-\nFabrikam has a single Azure subscription in the East US 2 Azure region. The subscription contains an\nAzure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains the following tables:\n- Patients\n- Employees\n- Procedures\n- Transactions\n- UsefulPrompts\n- ProcedureDocuments\nYou store a column master key as a secret in Azure Key Vault.\nYou have an on-premises application named TransactionProcessing that uses a hard-coded username and\npassword in a connection string to access DB1.\nProblem Statements\n-\nUsers report that after executing a long-running stored procedure named sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient,\nupdates to the underlying data are sometimes inconsistent.\nRequirements\n-\nPlanned Changes\n-\nFabrikam plans to manage all changes to Azure SQL Database objects by using source control in GitHub.\nEvery pull request submitted to production will be validated before it can be merged. Deployments must use\nthe Release configuration.\nSecurity Requirements\n-\nFabrikam identifies the following security requirements:\n- The TransactionProcessing application must use a passwordless connection to DB1.\n- The Employees table contains two columns named TaxID and Salary that must be encrypted at rest.\n- Auditors must have a tamper-evident history of transactions with cryptographic proof of changes to the\nemployee data.\nDatabase Performance Requirements\nRecords accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must NOT be changed by other transactions\nwhile the stored procedure runs.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nFabrikam identifies the following AI-related requirements:\n- Queries to the ProcedureDocuments table must use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n- Users must be able to query the data in DB1 by using prompts in Copilot in Microsoft Fabric.\n- The UsefulPrompts table will store prompts that doctors can use to help diagnose patient illness by\nconnecting to an Azure OpenAI endpoint.\nDevelopment Requirements\n-\nFabrikam identifies the following development requirements:\n- Provide the functionality to retrieve all the transactions of a given patient between two dates, showing a\nrunning total.\n- Expose a Data API builder (DAB) configuration file to enable Azure services to perform the following\noperations over a REST API:\no Read data from the procedures table without authentication.\no Read and insert data into the Transactions table once authenticated.\no Execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure.\n- Provide the functionality to retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical procedures\nduring the last 30 days.\n- Information for each medical procedure will be stored in a table. The table will be used with a large\nlanguage model (LLM) for user querying and will have the following structure."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE dbo.ProcedureDocuments\n(\n DocumentId INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n SourceId NVARCHAR(200) NULL,\n Content NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,\n Embedding VECTOR(1536) NOT NULL,\n CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\n);"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "DAB\n-\nYou create a DAB configuration file that meets the development requirements for DB1 and includes the\nfollowing entities."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n \"entities\": {\n  \"Procedures\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Procedures\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"]}]\n  },\n  \"Transactions\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Transactions\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"read\", \"create\"]}]\n  },\n  \"UpdateProcedurePatient\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\",\n   \"rest\": {\"enabled\": true, \"method\": \"post\", \"path\": \"/procedurepatient\"},\n   \"graphql\": false,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"execute\"]}]\n  }\n }\n}"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Question\nYou create an SDK-style SQL database project in Microsoft Visual Studio Code named Database.sqlproj\nand add the project to a GitHub repository.\nYou need to configure a GitHub Actions workflow to support the planned changes for DB1.\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p142-092.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ60 T02\nCorrect Answer:\n1. push\n2. build Database.sqlproj -c Release\nExplanation:\n1. Correcta: \"push\". El workflow está filtrado con branches: [\"main\"], de modo que se ejecuta cada vez que\nse envían commits a la rama main, y valida el proyecto SQL en cada cambio. Es el mismo patrón que usa\nel workflow de ejemplo de Microsoft Learn para proyectos SQL (on: push con branches: [\"main\"]).\nIncorrectas:\n- \"merge\": no es un evento válido de GitHub Actions. No existe un trigger llamado merge; una fusión se\nrefleja como un push a la rama destino.\n- \"pull_request\": se dispara al abrir o actualizar una pull request, no al enviar commits a main. Es la opción\nque se usaría para validar antes de fusionar, pero la respuesta correcta de esta pregunta es push, igual que\nen el workflow de referencia de Microsoft Learn.\n2. Correcta: \"build Database.sqlproj -c Release\". dotnet build compila el SDK-style SQL project (Microsoft.\nBuild.Sql) y valida las referencias entre objetos y la sintaxis T-SQL contra la plataforma de destino,\ngenerando el .dacpac. El parámetro -c Release cumple el requisito del case study de que los despliegues\ndeben usar la configuración Release.\nIncorrectas:\n- \"pack Database.sqlproj\": empaqueta el proyecto como paquete NuGet, no es el paso de validación de la\ncompilación y no aplica el requisito de Release.\n- \"publish Database.sqlproj -c Production\": Production no es una configuración de build estándar del\nproyecto (las estándar son Debug y Release), y publish no es el modo de validar un proyecto SQL. Los\nproyectos SQL se despliegan con SqlPackage o sql-action, no con dotnet publish.\n- \"publish Database.sqlproj -c Release\": usa la configuración correcta, pero publish tampoco es el comando\npara validar un proyecto SQL, y el flujo de este workflow es solo validar, no desplegar a la base de datos.\nReferences:\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/implement-cicd-sql-database-projects/02-create-build-\nvalidate-sql-database-projects\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/tutorials/create-deploy-sql-project\n- https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/visual-studio-code-extensions/sql-database-projects/build-\ndatabase-project-from-command-line"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 61, "pages": [143, 145], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the\nseries contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more\nthan one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nYou have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains a table named dbo.Orders. dbo.Orders has a\nclustered index, contains three years of data, and is partitioned by a column named OrderDate by month.\nYou need to remove all the rows for the oldest month. The solution must minimize the impact on other\nqueries that access the data in dbo.Orders.\nSolution: Run the following Transact-SQL statement.\nTRUNCATE TABLE dbo.Orders;\nDoes this meet the goal?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Yes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "No"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "binary", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ61 T02\nRespuesta Correcta - B (No)\nJustificación: TRUNCATE TABLE dbo.Orders; sin la cláusula WITH (PARTITIONS (...)) elimina todas las\nfilas de toda la tabla, es decir, los tres años de datos y no solo el mes más antiguo. Por eso no cumple el\nobjetivo ni minimiza el impacto sobre otras consultas.\nSolución\nLa instrucción propuesta no cumple el objetivo. Sin la cláusula WITH (PARTITIONS (...)), TRUNCATE\nTABLE elimina todas las filas de la tabla, así que se borrarían los tres años de datos en lugar de solo el\nmes más antiguo. Además, esto afectaría gravemente a todas las demás consultas que leen dbo.Orders.\nLa instrucción correcta apunta únicamente a la partición que contiene el mes más antiguo:\nTRUNCATE TABLE dbo.Orders\nWITH (PARTITIONS (1));\nPor qué falla la solución propuesta\nRequisito ¿TRUNCATE TABLE dbo.Orders; lo cumple?\nEliminar todas las filas del mes más antiguo Sí, pero también elimina todos los demás meses\nMantener intactos el resto de los datos No, se borran los tres años\nMinimizar el impacto en otras consultas No, se vacía la tabla completa\nCómo funciona el truncado a nivel de partición\nSi no se incluye la cláusula WITH (PARTITIONS (...)), se trunca la tabla completa.\nEl truncado de particiones específicas está soportado en SQL Server 2016 y posteriores, Azure SQL\nDatabase y SQL database in Microsoft Fabric.\nLa tabla y sus índices deben estar alineados (particionados con la misma función de partición). Aquí se\ncumple, porque el índice clustered está particionado por OrderDate.\nSolo se desasignan las páginas de la partición indicada, por lo que las demás particiones siguen\ndisponibles.\nTRUNCATE TABLE usa menos bloqueos y menos espacio del registro de transacciones que DELETE,\nporque solo registra las desasignaciones de páginas y no cada fila eliminada.\nOtros enfoques válidos\nALTER TABLE ... SWITCH PARTITION mueve la partición más antigua a una tabla de archivo o de\nstaging, que luego se puede eliminar o truncar.\nDELETE ... WHERE OrderDate < ... funciona, pero es más lento, genera mucho más registro de\ntransacciones y provoca más bloqueos, por lo que afecta más a las consultas concurrentes.\nReferencias\nTRUNCATE TABLE (Transact-SQL)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/truncate-table-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nSintaxis y comportamiento de WITH (PARTITIONS (...)), y truncado de toda la tabla cuando se omite la\ncláusula (aplica a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric).\nTRUNCATE TABLE (Transact-SQL) - Ejemplos: Truncate table partitions\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/truncate-table-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17#examples\nEjemplo de truncado de particiones específicas de una tabla particionada.\nPartitioned tables and indexes\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/partitions/partitioned-tables-and-indexes?view=sql-\nserver-ver17\nBeneficios del particionado, incluido el truncado de datos en una sola partición para retención de datos.\nALTER TABLE (Transact-SQL)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/alter-table-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nRecomienda TRUNCATE TABLE ... WITH (PARTITIONS (...)) para eliminar todas las filas de particiones\nespecíficas sin eliminarlas ni hacer switch."}], "points": 1}, {"id": 62, "pages": [145, 146], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the\nseries contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others\nmight not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions\nwill not appear in the review screen.\nYou have an SDK-style SQL database project stored in a Git repository. The project targets an Azure SQL\ndatabase.\nThe Cl build fails with unresolved reference errors when the project references system objects.\nYou need to update the SQL database project to ensure that dotnet build validates successfully by including\nthe correct system objects in the\ndatabase model for Azure SQL Database.\nSolution: Build the project by running dotnet build -bl -flp:v=diag.\nDoes this meet the goal?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Yes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "No"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "binary", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ62 T02\nES\nRespuesta\nB. No\nExplicación\nPor qué no cumple el objetivo: Por defecto, los proyectos SQL no incluyen los objetos del sistema en el\nmodelo de base de datos. Por eso la validación falla al referenciar objetos de sistema. La solución es\nañadir una referencia de base de datos al `master.dacpac` de la plataforma de destino. En un proyecto\nSDK-style (Microsoft.Build.Sql) se hace con una referencia de paquete NuGet. Para Azure SQL Database el\npaquete es **Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master.\nEjecutar la compilación con registro binario y logging detallado solo genera diagnósticos. No modifica el\nproyecto ni el modelo, así que los objetos del sistema siguen sin resolverse y el error persiste. Sirve para\ninvestigar el fallo, no para corregirlo.\nConceptos a conocer:\n- Referencia de paquete: es el método recomendado en desarrollo nuevo y solo funciona en proyectos\nSDK-style. Los proyectos originales (SSDT clásicos) no soportan paquetes NuGet y usan una referencia de\nartefacto al `master.dacpac`.\n-Paquete según la plataforma: el de SQL Server (`Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Master`) es distinto del de\nAzure SQL Database (`Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master`). Usar el equivocado da un modelo\nincorrecto para el destino.\n- SQL database en Fabric: usa `Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.DbFabric` y requiere configurar además la\npropiedad `DatabaseVariableLiteralValue` con el valor `master`.\nReferencias\n- Microsoft Learn: [SQL projects system objects](https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/\nconcepts/system-objects?view=sql-server-ver17)\n- Microsoft Learn: [SQL projects package references](https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/sql-database-\nprojects/concepts/package-references?view=sql-server-ver17)\n- SQL Server: no aplica más allá de lo anterior.\nClave DP-800\nErrores de referencias sin resolver a objetos del sistema se corrigen con la referencia de paquete al dacpac\nde `master` de la plataforma (Azure SQL → `Dacpacs.Azure.Master`), no con logs ni con otras opciones de\ncompilación.\n---\nEnglish version\nAnswer\nB. No\nExplanation\nWhy it doesn't meet the goal: By default, SQL projects don't include system objects in the database model,\nso validation fails when the project references them. The fix is a database reference to the target platform's\n`master.dacpac`. In SDK-style (Microsoft.Build.Sql) projects this is done with a NuGet package reference.\nFor Azure SQL Database the package is Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master.\nRunning the build with a binary log and diagnostic verbosity only produces diagnostic output. It doesn't\nchange the project or the model, so system objects remain unresolved and the error persists. It helps you\ninvestigate the failure, not fix it.\nConcepts to know:\n- Package reference: this is the recommended method for new development and only works in SDK-style\nprojects. Original (classic SSDT) projects don't support NuGet packages and use an artifact reference to\n`master.dacpac` instead.\n- Package per platform: the SQL Server package (`Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Master`) differs from the\nAzure SQL Database package (`Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.Azure.Master`). Using the wrong one gives a\nmodel that doesn't match the target.\n- SQL database in Fabric: it uses `Microsoft.SqlServer.Dacpacs.DbFabric` and also needs the\n`DatabaseVariableLiteralValue` property set to `master`.\nReferences\n- Microsoft Learn: [SQL projects system objects](https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/\nconcepts/system-objects?view=sql-server-ver17)\n- Microsoft Learn: [SQL projects package references](https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/sql-database-\nprojects/concepts/package-references?view=sql-server-ver17)\n- SQL Server: not applicable beyond the above.\nDP-800 Key\nUnresolved-reference errors on system objects are fixed by adding a package reference to the platform's\n`master` dacpac (Azure SQL → `Dacpacs.Azure.Master`), not by logging or other build options."}], "points": 1}, {"id": 63, "pages": [146, 149], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p147-093", "p148-094"], "type": "yesno", "template": "The workflow relies on a Microsoft Entra workload identity to access the target Azure SQL database.\nChanging the Build SQL project step to run: dotnet build MyDatabaseProject.sqlproj -c Release will result in a successful deployment.\nThe workflow can be triggered manually, without making changes to the repository.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [1, 1, 0], "image": 94, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\nYou have an SDK-style SQL database project named MyDatabaseProject.sqlproj stored in a private GitHub\nrepository. The repository contains the following GitHub Actions workflow."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "name: Build and Deploy SQL Project\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\n  workflow_dispatch:\njobs:\n  build-and-deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions:\n      id-token: write\n      contents: read\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Build SQL project\n        run: dotnet build MyDatabaseProject.sqlproj\n      - name: Publish DACPAC\n        uses: azure/sql-action@v2\n        with:\n          action: publish\n          path: bin/Debug/MyDatabaseProject.dacpac\n          connection-string: ${{ secrets.AZURE_SQL_CONNECTION_STRING }}"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The repository contains the AZURE_SQL_CONNECTION_STRING secrets.\nThe target is an Azure SQL database that allows access to Azure services and is configured to support\nmixed authentication.\nThe workflow runs successfully.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p148-095.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ63 T02\nCorrect Answer:\nThe workflow relies on a Microsoft Entra workload identity to access the target Azure SQL database. → No\nChanging the Build SQL project step to run: dotnet build MyDatabaseProject.sqlproj -c Release will result in\na successful deployment. → No\nThe workflow can be triggered manually, without making changes to the repository. → Yes\nExplanation:\nAunque el workflow declara el permiso id-token: write, que haría falta para una federación OIDC con\nMicrosoft Entra, no hay ningún paso azure/login que obtenga un token. El paso Publish DACPAC se\nautentica con la cadena de conexión guardada en el secreto AZURE_SQL_CONNECTION_STRING. Como\nla base de datos admite autenticación mixta, esa cadena usa autenticación SQL (usuario y contraseña) y\nno una identidad de carga de trabajo de Microsoft Entra.\nCon -c Release, dotnet build compila en la configuración Release y genera el .dacpac en bin/Release/\nMyDatabaseProject.dacpac. El paso Publish DACPAC sigue apuntando a bin/Debug/MyDatabaseProject.\ndacpac, así que no encuentra el archivo y el despliegue falla, salvo que también se cambie el valor de path.\nEl trigger workflow_dispatch permite lanzar el workflow a mano desde la pestaña Actions de GitHub, con la\nCLI o con la API REST, sin hacer ningún push ni cambio en el repositorio.\nEnglish translation of the Explanation:\nAlthough the workflow declares the id-token: write permission, which would be required for OIDC federation\nwith Microsoft Entra, there is no azure/login step that obtains a token. The Publish DACPAC step\nauthenticates with the connection string stored in the AZURE_SQL_CONNECTION_STRING secret.\nBecause the database supports mixed authentication, that connection string uses SQL authentication\n(username and password), not a Microsoft Entra workload identity.\nWith -c Release, dotnet build compiles in the Release configuration and outputs the .dacpac to bin/Release/\nMyDatabaseProject.dacpac. The Publish DACPAC step still points to bin/Debug/MyDatabaseProject.\ndacpac, so it cannot find the file and the deployment fails unless the path value is also changed.\nThe workflow_dispatch trigger lets the workflow be started manually from the GitHub Actions tab, the CLI or\nthe REST API, without any push or change to the repository.\nReference:\nhttps://github.com/Azure/sql-action\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/developer/github/connect-from-azure-openid-connect\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/tools/dotnet-build\nhttps://docs.github.com/actions/using-workflows/events-that-trigger-workflows#workflow_dispatch\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/tutorials/create-deploy-sql-project?view=sql-\nserver-ver17"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 64, "pages": [149, 152], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p150-096"], "type": "drag", "template": "WITH AggregatedCPU AS\n(\n SELECT q.query_hash,\n SUM(count_executions * {{0}} / 1000.0) AS total_cpu_ms\n FROM sys.query_store_query_text AS qt\n INNER JOIN sys.query_store_query AS q ON qt.query_text_id = q.query_text_id\n INNER JOIN sys.query_store_plan AS p ON q.query_id = p.query_id\n INNER JOIN sys.query_store_runtime_stats AS rs ON rs.plan_id = p.plan_id\n INNER JOIN {{1}} AS rsi\n ON rsi.runtime_stats_interval_id = rs.runtime_stats_interval_id\n WHERE rs.execution_type_desc IN ('Regular', 'Aborted', 'Exception')\n AND rsi.start_time >= {{2}}\n GROUP BY q.query_hash\n), OrderedCPU AS\n(\n SELECT query_hash, total_cpu_ms,\n ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY total_cpu_ms DESC, query_hash ASC) AS rn\n FROM AggregatedCPU\n)\nSELECT query_hash, total_cpu_ms FROM OrderedCPU\nWHERE rn <= 15\nORDER BY total_cpu_ms DESC;", "pools": [["DATEADD(DAY, -2, GETDATE())", "DATEADD(HOUR, -2, GETDATE())", "DATEADD(HOUR, -2, GETUTCDATE())", "rs.avg_cpu_time", "rs.last_cpu_time", "sys.dm_exec_query_stats", "sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval"], ["DATEADD(DAY, -2, GETDATE())", "DATEADD(HOUR, -2, GETDATE())", "DATEADD(HOUR, -2, GETUTCDATE())", "rs.avg_cpu_time", "rs.last_cpu_time", "sys.dm_exec_query_stats", "sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval"], ["DATEADD(DAY, -2, GETDATE())", "DATEADD(HOUR, -2, GETDATE())", "DATEADD(HOUR, -2, GETUTCDATE())", "rs.avg_cpu_time", "rs.last_cpu_time", "sys.dm_exec_query_stats", "sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval"]], "correct": [3, 6, 2], "image": 96, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that supports an AI-driven product search API.\nYou need to identify the top CPU-consuming queries from the last two hours by using Query Store data. The\nsolution must aggregate CPU consumption across executions and return only the top 15 query hashes.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL code? Drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each\nvalue may be used once, more than once, or not at all.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p151-097.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ64 T02\nCorrect Answer:\n1. rs.avg_cpu_time\n2. sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval\n3. DATEADD(HOUR, -2, GETUTCDATE())\nExplanation:\nEl primer hueco debe ser rs.avg_cpu_time. Query Store registra el tiempo medio de CPU por ejecución en\nmicrosegundos. Al multiplicarlo por count_executions y sumar los resultados, la consulta calcula el\nconsumo acumulado de CPU; dividir entre 1000 lo expresa en milisegundos. rs.last_cpu_time solo\nrepresenta la última ejecución.\nEl segundo hueco debe ser sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval. Esta vista contiene los intervalos\ntemporales asociados a las estadísticas de ejecución y permite consultar rsi.start_time. sys.\ndm_exec_query_stats no es la vista de intervalos de Query Store que necesita este JOIN.\nEl tercer hueco debe ser DATEADD(HOUR, -2, GETUTCDATE()). Resta dos horas a la hora UTC actual\npara establecer el límite temporal solicitado. DATEADD(DAY, -2, GETDATE()) abarcaría dos días, y\nGETDATE() no expresa explícitamente la hora UTC.\nPor último, la consulta agrupa por query_hash, ordena los resultados por CPU total y conserva solo los\nprimeros 15.\nReferences:\nReferencias (Microsoft Learn)\nsys.query_store_runtime_stats (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/system-\ncatalog-views/sys-query-store-runtime-stats-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nsys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-\ndatabases/system-catalog-views/sys-query-store-runtime-stats-interval-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nGETUTCDATE (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/getutcdate-transact-sql?\nview=sql-server-ver17\nDATEADD (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/dateadd-transact-sql?view=sql-\nserver-ver17"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 65, "pages": [152, 155], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p153-098"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "To run Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId: {{0}}\nTo query Sales.Customer: {{1}}\nTo prevent viewing TaxID: {{2}}", "pools": [["DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer TO AppSupport;", "DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer(TaxID) TO AppSupport;", "GRANT EXECUTE ON OBJECT::Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId TO AppSupport;", "GRANT SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer TO AppSupport;", "GRANT SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId TO AppSupport;", "REVOKE SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer(TaxID) FROM AppSupport;"], ["DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer TO AppSupport;", "DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer(TaxID) TO AppSupport;", "GRANT EXECUTE ON OBJECT::Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId TO AppSupport;", "GRANT SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer TO AppSupport;", "GRANT SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId TO AppSupport;", "REVOKE SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer(TaxID) FROM AppSupport;"], ["DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer TO AppSupport;", "DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer(TaxID) TO AppSupport;", "GRANT EXECUTE ON OBJECT::Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId TO AppSupport;", "GRANT SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer TO AppSupport;", "GRANT SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId TO AppSupport;", "REVOKE SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer(TaxID) FROM AppSupport;"]], "correct": [2, 3, 1], "image": 98, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT -\nYou have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named Sales.Customer. Sales.Customer contains\ncolumns named CustomerId, FullName, Email, TaxID, and RegionId.\nYou have a database role named AppSupport that is used by a support application.\nYou need to implement a security solution for AppSupport that meets the following requirements:\n- AppSupport must be prevented from viewing TaxID.\n- AppSupport must be able to query Sales.Customer to troubleshoot issues.\n- AppSupport must be able to run a stored procedure named Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId.\nWhich Transact-SQL statements should you include in the solution? To answer, select the appropriate\noptions in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p154-099.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ65 T02\nCORRECT ANSWER:\n- To run Sales.usp_GetCustomerByCustomerId: GRANT EXECUTE ON OBJECT::Sales.\nusp_GetCustomerByCustomerId TO AppSupport;\n- To query Sales.Customer: GRANT SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer TO AppSupport;\n- To prevent viewing TaxID: DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::Sales.Customer(TaxID) TO AppSupport;\nEXPLANATION:\n- GRANT EXECUTE: Concede el permiso al rol AppSupport para poder ejecutar el procedimiento\nalmacenado.\n- GRANT SELECT: Otorga al rol AppSupport el derecho de consultar los datos de la tabla Sales.Customer.\n- DENY SELECT: Bloquea explícitamente el acceso de lectura a la columna TaxID dentro de la tabla Sales.\nCustomer para el rol AppSupport. En SQL Server y Azure SQL, un permiso DENY explícito a nivel de\ncolumna siempre anula un permiso GRANT concedido a nivel de tabla, permitiendo al usuario consultar el\nresto de la tabla sin exponer datos confidenciales.\nREFERENCES:\nGRANT - Permisos de objeto (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/statements/grant-object-permissions-transact-sql\nDENY - Permisos de objeto (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/statements/deny-object-permissions-transact-sql"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 66, "pages": [155, 157], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p156-100"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "Start IP address and end IP address of the Azure SQL firewall rule: {{0}}\nContainer Apps ingress: {{1}}", "pools": [["0.0.0.0 to 0.0.0.0.", "127.0.0.1 to 127.0.0.1", "10.0.0.0 to 10.255.255.255"], ["Restrict ingress to internal traffic only.", "Set ingress to external and target port 5000.", "Set ingress to internal and target port 5000."]], "correct": [0, 1], "image": 100, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\nYou have an Azure SQL database named ProductsDB.\nYou deploy Data API builder (DAB) to Azure Container Apps.\nYou discover that the container app cannot connect to ProductsDB.\nYour deployment team reports that the container app is unreachable from the internet for integration tests.\nYou need to update Azure SQL Database and Container Apps to meet the following requirements:\n- Ensure that the Azure SQL logical server allows connections from Azure services.\n- Ensure that the Container Apps environment accepts inbound requests from the public internet.\nWhat should you configure? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p156-101.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ66 T02\n1. ANÁLISIS\nConcepto principal: configuración de conectividad de red.\nServicios: Azure SQL Database (firewall del logical server) + Azure Container Apps (ingress).\nEscenario: DAB desplegado en Container Apps no puede conectar a ProductsDB y la app no es accesible\ndesde Internet.\n2. VERIFICACIÓN (MCP)\nDocumentación oficial de Microsoft Learn consultada sobre firewall de Azure SQL y ingress de Container\nApps.\n3. RESOLUCIÓN\nAzure SQL logical server: Habilitar Allow Azure services and resources to access this server.\nContainer Apps environment / app: Configurar Ingress como External (Accepting traffic from anywhere /\npublic internet).\n4. JUSTIFICACIÓN\nAzure SQL – Allow Azure services…\nMicrosoft Learn indica que esta opción crea la regla de firewall 0.0.0.0 – 0.0.0.0\n(AllowAllWindowsAzureIps). Permite que cualquier recurso de Azure (incluido el Container App) se conecte\nal logical server sin necesidad de IPs específicas.\nContainer Apps – Ingress External\nSegún la documentación de Ingress, el tipo External publica la aplicación a través de la IP pública del\nentorno, permitiendo tráfico entrante desde Internet público. El tipo Internal solo permite acceso desde\ndentro del entorno.\nOtras opciones (reglas IP específicas, Private Endpoint, Ingress Internal, etc.) no cumplen simultáneamente\nambos requisitos de forma rápida y directa.\n5. REFERENCIA\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/firewall-configure\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/network-access-controls-overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/container-apps/ingress-overview"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 67, "pages": [157, 158], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p157-102"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a database that contains production data. The schema is stored in a Git repository as an SDK-\nstyle SQL database project and contains the following reference data."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Name | Data type\nRefId | int identity\nCode | nvarchar(10)\nCreateDate | datetime2\nDescription | nvarchar(255)"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "A deployment pipeline can be rerun automatically when a transient failure occurs.\nYou need to deploy the reference data as part of the same CI/CD process. Rerunning the pipeline must\nproduce the same outcome and must NOT create duplicate rows.\nWhat should you do?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Add a post-deployment script that inserts reference rows by using IF NOT EXISTS or MERGE logic."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Restore a backup after each deployment."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Store the reference values in GitHub repository secrets."}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ67 T02\nANSWER: A\nCORRECT ANSWER:\nAdd a post-deployment script that inserts reference rows by using IF NOT EXISTS or MERGE logic.\nEXPLANATION:\nrespuesta correcta:\nLos proyectos SQL de estilo SDK (.sqlproj con Microsoft.Build.Sql) permiten incluir un único script de post-\ndeployment. Este script se ejecuta automáticamente después de aplicar el plan de despliegue del esquema\n(DACPAC).\nLa documentación oficial indica expresamente que los scripts de post-deployment deben ser idempotentes\n(seguros de ejecutar repetidamente) y que uno de los casos de uso principales es la gestión de datos de\nreferencia / estáticos:\nUsar IF NOT EXISTS o, preferiblemente, la sentencia MERGE para insertar/actualizar solo cuando sea\nnecesario.\nDe esta forma, si el pipeline se reejecuta (por fallo transitorio o de forma intencionada), no se generan filas\nduplicadas y el resultado final es siempre el mismo.\nPor qué las demás opciones son incorrectas:\nB. Restore a backup after each deployment\nRestaurar un backup sobrescribe todos los datos de producción (incluyendo datos transaccionales), no es\nparte del proceso de despliegue de esquema/datos de referencia y destruye cualquier cambio realizado\ndespués del backup. No cumple el requisito de idempotencia controlada ni forma parte del flujo CI/CD\nestándar de un SQL project.\nC. Store the reference values in GitHub repository secrets\nLos secrets de GitHub (o Azure DevOps) están diseñados para almacenar valores sensibles (cadenas de\nconexión, tokens, contraseñas). No son el mecanismo adecuado ni soportado para datos de referencia de\nnegocio. Además, no se integran automáticamente en el despliegue del DACPAC.\nREFERENCIA\nFuentes oficiales exactas de Microsoft Learn:\nPre-deployment and post-deployment scripts overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/sql-database-projects/concepts/pre-post-deployment-scripts\nManage static data with a post-deployment script (ejemplo explícito con MERGE)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/database/sql/source-control#manage-static-data-with-a-post-deployment-\nscript"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 68, "pages": [158, 159], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named ProductsDB.\nYou deploy Data API builder (DAB) to Azure Container Apps by using the mcr.microsoft.com/azure-\ndatabases/data-api-builder:latest image.\nThe container app has the following configurations:\n• Secrets: mssql-connection-string, dab-config-base64\n• Environment variables:\n• MSSQL_CONNECTION_STRING=secretref:mssql-connection-string\n• DAB_CONFIG_BASE64=secretref:dab-config-base64\n• Ingress: External on port 5000\nUsers report that the /health endpoint returns a healthy response, but all requests that query an entity\nnamed Products fail and generate a connection error.\nYou confirm that the SQL login in the connection string is correct and the database exists.\nYou need to ensure that the container app can establish connections to the Azure SQL logical server\nwithout changing the container app deployment settings or the DAB configuration file.\nWhat should you do on the Azure SQL logical server?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Create an auto-failover group for ProductsDB."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Run DBCC CHECKDB on ProductsDB."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Create a firewall rule that allows a start and end IP address of 0.0.0.0."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Enable FORCE_LAST_GOOD_PLAN automatic tuning for ProductsDB."}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ68 T02\n2. Explicación Técnica Detallada:\nEl endpoint /health de Data API builder (DAB) responde “healthy” porque solo verifica que el contenedor\nesté en ejecución y el proceso escuche en el puerto 5000. Las peticiones a la entidad Products fallan con\nerror de conexión porque DAB (ejecutado en Azure Container Apps) no puede alcanzar el Azure SQL\nlogical server.\nPor defecto, el firewall de Azure SQL Database bloquea todas las conexiones entrantes. Cuando un\nservicio de Azure (como Container Apps) intenta conectar, el firewall solo permite el tráfico si existe la regla\nespecial de servidor con rango 0.0.0.0 – 0.0.0.0. Esta regla es la que se crea automáticamente al activar la\nopción “Allow Azure services and resources to access this server” (nombre interno\nAllowAllWindowsAzureIps).\nComo el escenario prohíbe modificar la configuración del Container App ni el archivo de configuración de\nDAB, la única acción válida en el logical server es crear esa regla de firewall. Una vez creada, el tráfico\nsaliente de Container Apps (que sale con IPs de Azure) es aceptado y las consultas a la entidad Products\nfuncionan.\n3. Análisis de Opciones Incorrectas:\nA. Create an auto-failover group for ProductsDB.\nLos Auto-Failover Groups proporcionan alta disponibilidad y recuperación ante desastres entre regiones. No\ntienen ninguna relación con el acceso de red ni resuelven errores de conexión por firewall.\nB. Run DBCC CHECKDB on ProductsDB.\nDBCC CHECKDB verifica la integridad lógica y física de la base de datos. El problema reportado es de\nconectividad de red, no de corrupción de datos. Además, el login y la existencia de la base de datos ya\nestán confirmados.\nD. Enable FORCE_LAST_GOOD_PLAN automatic tuning for ProductsDB.\nEsta opción de Automatic Tuning fuerza el uso del último plan de ejecución “bueno” para mejorar el\nrendimiento de consultas. No afecta en absoluto a la conectividad ni a las reglas de firewall.\n4. Referencias Oficiales de Microsoft & SQL Server:\nIP Firewall Rules – Azure SQL Database\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/firewall-configure\nCreate a server-level firewall rule\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/firewall-create-server-level-portal-quickstart\nsp_set_firewall_rule\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-set-firewall-rule-\nazure-sql-database\nSQL Server troubleshooting – Data API builder\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/troubleshooting/mssql\nTutorial: Deploy Data API builder to Azure Container Apps\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/tutorial-deploy-container-app-cli"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 69, "pages": [159, 161], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named SalesDB and an Azure App Service app named sales-api.\nSalesDB contains a table named dbo.Customers. dbo.Customers contains two columns named\nCreditCardNumber and TenantId.\nCurrently, sales-api connects to SalesDB by using SQL authentication with stored username and password.\nYou need to recommend a solution that meets the following requirements:\nProvides a passwordless method for sales-api to access SalesDB.\nEnsures that credit card numbers are NOT stored as plain text.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Azure Key Vault and Always Encrypted"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) and row-level security (RLS)"}, {"id": "C", "text": "managed identities and dynamic data masking"}, {"id": "D", "text": "managed identities and Always Encrypted"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ69 T02\n1. Correct Answer:\nD. managed identities and Always Encrypted\n2. Technical Explanation:\nLa aplicación sales-api (Azure App Service) se conecta actualmente a SalesDB mediante autenticación\nSQL (usuario + contraseña).\nPara cumplir el requisito de acceso passwordless:\nSe debe usar una Managed Identity (system-assigned o user-assigned) en el App Service.\nSe configura autenticación de Microsoft Entra ID en Azure SQL Database y se otorga al managed identity el\nrol adecuado (ej. db_datareader / db_datawriter o un rol personalizado).\nLa cadena de conexión deja de contener credenciales y usa Authentication=Active Directory Managed\nIdentity (o Active Directory Default).\nPara cumplir el requisito de que los números de tarjeta de crédito no se almacenen en texto plano:\nSe implementa Always Encrypted en la columna CreditCardNumber.\nLos datos se cifran en el cliente (driver .NET) antes de enviarse a SQL Server.\nLa clave de cifrado se almacena en Azure Key Vault (o en el certificado del cliente).\nNi siquiera los administradores de la base de datos pueden ver el valor en claro.\nEsta combinación (Managed Identity + Always Encrypted) es la solución oficial recomendada por Microsoft\npara este escenario.\n3. Analysis of Incorrect Options:\nA. Azure Key Vault and Always Encrypted\nAlways Encrypted es correcto para proteger la columna, pero Azure Key Vault por sí solo no proporciona el\nmétodo de autenticación passwordless. Key Vault se usa normalmente para almacenar la clave de Always\nEncrypted, pero no sustituye a Managed Identity para la conexión.\nB. Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) and row-level security (RLS)\nTDE cifra los datos en reposo (archivos, backups), pero cuando se consultan los datos se desencriptan y\nse devuelven en texto plano. No cumple el requisito de “NOT stored as plain text” desde la perspectiva de\nla aplicación.\nRLS controla el acceso por filas (útil con TenantId), pero no cifra datos.\nC. managed identities and dynamic data masking\nManaged Identity es correcto para el acceso passwordless.\nDynamic Data Masking solo enmascara el valor en los resultados de consulta para usuarios no\nprivilegiados. Los datos siguen almacenados en texto plano en la base de datos, por lo que no cumple el\nsegundo requisito.\n4. Official Microsoft References:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/authentication-azure-ad-user-assigned-\nmanaged-identity\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/always-encrypted-database-\nengine\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/always-encrypted-keystore-\nproviders\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/transparent-data-encryption\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/dynamic-data-masking"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 70, "pages": [161, 163], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p161-103"], "type": "drag", "template": "Support agents must see only the rows of their own TenantId: {{0}}\nSupport agents must see only the domain name portion of CustomerEmail: {{1}}", "pools": [["Configure dynamic data masking on selected columns.", "Enable Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) and customer-managed keys.", "Create a row-level security (RLS) predicate that uses the user context.", "Encrypt sensitive columns by using Always Encrypted with Azure Key Vault."], ["Configure dynamic data masking on selected columns.", "Enable Transparent Data Encryption (TDE) and customer-managed keys.", "Create a row-level security (RLS) predicate that uses the user context.", "Encrypt sensitive columns by using Always Encrypted with Azure Key Vault."]], "correct": [2, 0], "image": 103, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named sqldb-ai-prod that stores customer support tickets for a\nmultitenant software as a service (SaaS) application. sqldb-ai-prod contains a table named Tickets. Tickets\ncontains columns named TenantId, TicketId, CustomerEmail, CustomerPhone, and Notes.\nYou plan to harden data access, since a new support team will use ad hoc reporting tools that connect\ndirectly to sqldb-ai-prod.\nYou need to configure security to meet the following requirements:\n- Support agents must see only the rows of their own TenantId column.\n- Support agents must see only the domain name portion of the CustomerEmail column.\nWhat should you do for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct\nrequirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p162-104.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#Q70 T02\nCorrect Answer:\nSupport agents must see only the rows of their own TenantId: Create a row-level security (RLS) predicate\nthat uses the user context.\nSupport agents must see only the domain name portion of CustomerEmail: Configure dynamic data\nmasking on selected columns.\nExplanation:\nLa clave está en que el primer requisito limita filas por tenant, y eso lo resuelve Row-Level Security (RLS)\ncon un filter predicate basado en el contexto del usuario (por ejemplo SESSION_CONTEXT). El segundo\nrequisito limita qué parte del valor de una columna se ve, y eso lo resuelve Dynamic Data Masking (DDM),\nque ofusca datos en el resultado de la consulta según permisos. Para mostrar solo el dominio se usa una\nregla de enmascarado sobre CustomerEmail, como el enmascarado basado en regex con\nREGEXP_REPLACE, que oculta el usuario y conserva el dominio.\nWhy not the others:\n- TDE with customer-managed keys cifra los datos en reposo, pero no restringe lo que ve un usuario\nautorizado que consulta la base de datos.\n- Always Encrypted cifra columnas de extremo a extremo y las herramientas de informes ad hoc no podrían\nmostrar el dominio. Además, no se puede definir una máscara DDM sobre columnas cifradas con Always\nEncrypted.\nReference:\nRow-level security: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/security/row-level-security?view=sql-\nserver-ver17\nRegex-based dynamic data masking in Azure SQL Database (preview): https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/\nazure-sql/database/dynamic-data-masking-regex?view=azuresql\nDynamic data masking (limitaciones, incluye Always Encrypted): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-\ndatabases/security/dynamic-data-masking?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 71, "pages": [163, 165], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p163-105"], "type": "yesno", "template": "The blocking is caused by an uncommitted explicit transaction in the blocking session that is holding locks.\nWhile an UPDATE operation on Sales.Orders is occurring, SELECT statements will be blocked.\nJoining sys.dm_tran_locks to sys.dm_exec_requests will show which session holds locks involved in the blocking chain.", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [0, 1, 1], "image": 105, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that uses the default settings for a newly created database\nand contains a table named Sales.Orders.\nYou have an application that uses two stored procedures to access Sales.Orders.\nWhile monitoring database activity, you discover the following:\nsys.dm_exec_requests shows multiple sessions in a suspended state with wait_type = LCK_M_X. All the\nsessions show the same wait_resource, which maps to Sales.Orders, and the same nonzero\nblocking_session_id.\nsys.dm_exec_input_buffer(blocking_session_id, NULL) returns a last submitted command of BEGIN\nTRANSACTION UPDATE Sales.Orders.\nsys.dm_exec_sessions for the blocking session shows status = sleeping and open_transaction_count =\n1\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p164-106.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ71 T02\nExplanation\nThe blocking is caused by an uncommitted explicit transaction in the blocking session that is holding locks.\n→ Yes\nWhile an UPDATE operation on Sales.Orders is occurring, SELECT statements will be blocked. → No\nPor qué es correcta la primera (Yes):\nLos datos del escenario son claros:\nsys.dm_exec_input_buffer muestra que la última instrucción enviada por la sesión bloqueante fue BEGIN\nTRANSACTION UPDATE Sales.Orders.\nsys.dm_exec_sessions muestra status = sleeping y open_transaction_count = 1.\nEsto es el patrón clásico de un sleeping head blocker: la sesión abrió una transacción explícita, ejecutó el\nUPDATE (adquiriendo locks exclusivos X), y luego se quedó en estado sleeping sin hacer COMMIT ni\nROLLBACK. Mientras la transacción esté abierta, mantiene los locks, provocando que otras sesiones\nesperen con wait_type = LCK_M_X.\nPor qué es falsa la segunda (No):\nSQL database in Microsoft Fabric (basada en Azure SQL Database) tiene\nREAD_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT (RCSI) activado por defecto en las bases de datos nuevas.\nCon RCSI:\nLos lectores (SELECT) usan row versioning y no adquieren Shared (S) locks.\nPor tanto, un UPDATE (que mantiene Exclusive locks) no bloquea a los SELECT.\nLos sesiones suspendidas están esperando LCK_M_X (exclusive lock), lo que indica que son otras\noperaciones de escritura (UPDATE/INSERT/DELETE), no SELECT.\nJustificación de por qué la 3 es falsa (No):\nsys.dm_exec_requests solo muestra solicitudes activas (running, runnable, suspended, etc.).\nEn este escenario, la sesión bloqueante está en estado sleeping (status = sleeping) y tiene\nopen_transaction_count = 1.\nLas sesiones sleeping no aparecen en sys.dm_exec_requests porque no tienen ninguna request en\nejecución.\nPor lo tanto, si haces un JOIN entre sys.dm_tran_locks y sys.dm_exec_requests, no vas a ver la sesión que\nestá sosteniendo los locks (el head blocker), solo verás las sesiones que están esperando (las que están\nen estado suspended con LCK_M_X).\nPara identificar correctamente quién sostiene los locks en este caso, hay que unir sys.dm_tran_locks con\nsys.dm_exec_sessions (o usar blocking_session_id desde sys.dm_exec_requests de las sesiones\nbloqueadas).\nReference\nTroubleshoot Query Blocking in Fabric Data Warehouse (Microsoft Learn) : https://learn.microsoft.com/en-\nus/fabric/data-warehouse/troubleshoot-query-blocking\nUnderstand and Resolve Blocking Problems - Azure SQL Database / SQL database in Microsoft Fabric:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/understand-resolve-blocking?view=azuresql\nDocumentación de sys.dm_exec_sessions, sys.dm_exec_requests y sys.dm_exec_input_buffer (Microsoft\nLearn)"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 72, "pages": [165, 167], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p165-107"], "type": "drag", "template": "To identify long-running queries: {{0}}\nTo verify that the query has multiple persisted plans over time: {{1}}\nTo restore a previous plan without changing the code: {{2}}", "pools": [["Azure Monitor", "Log Analytics", "Query Performance Insight", "The Query Store plan store", "Plan forcing in Query Store", "The Regressed Queries pane in Query Store"], ["Azure Monitor", "Log Analytics", "Query Performance Insight", "The Query Store plan store", "Plan forcing in Query Store", "The Regressed Queries pane in Query Store"], ["Azure Monitor", "Log Analytics", "Query Performance Insight", "The Query Store plan store", "Plan forcing in Query Store", "The Regressed Queries pane in Query Store"]], "correct": [2, 3, 4], "image": 107, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named SalesDB that has Query Store enabled. SalesDB supports an AI-\ndriven product search API.\nUsers report that the latency of the API increased immediately after the API was deployed and remains high.\nYou need to identify whether the latency increase was caused by an execution plan regression and, if so,\ndecide which corrective action will restore a previous plan. The solution must prevent any changes to the\nAPI code.\nWhat should you use? To answer, drag the appropriate tools to the correct step. Each tool may be used\nonce, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view\ncontent.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p166-108.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ72 T02\nCorrect Answer:\nTo identify long-running queries: Query Performance Insight\nTo verify that the query has multiple persisted plans over time: The Query Store plan store\nTo restore a previous plan without changing the code: Plan forcing in Query Store\nExplanation:\nQuery Performance Insight is the right tool for finding long-running queries. In Azure SQL Database it shows\nthe top resource-consuming and long-running queries, ranked by CPU, duration and execution count. It runs\non top of Query Store, which is enabled by default.\nAzure Monitor and Log Analytics are general-purpose telemetry and log tools. They can surface metrics and\ndiagnostics, but they don't give a per-query view of durations and plans. They are also not the tool the\ndocumentation names for this step.\nThe Regressed Queries pane could also list slow queries, but it is meant for queries whose metrics have\nregressed. It is not the tool the scenario intends for this first step.\nThe Query Store plan store is where Query Store persists the execution plans for each query, so you can\nconfirm that a query has more than one plan over time. Multiple plans for the same query are the sign of a\nplan-choice change, and the plan store's limit on plans per query is set by max_plans_per_query.\nThe Regressed Queries pane compares plans and shows how they performed over time, but the step asks\nwhere the multiple plans are persisted. That is the plan store.\nThe runtime stats store and wait stats store hold execution and wait metrics, not the plans themselves.\nAzure Monitor and Log Analytics don't hold Query Store's persisted plan history.\nPlan forcing in Query Store restores the earlier plan. The Query Store keeps several plans per query, so it\ncan direct the optimizer to use a specific one. It works like a query hint but needs no change to the\napplication, which meets the requirement to leave the API code untouched. You can only force plans that\nQuery Store has already recorded for that query, which is why the previous plan can be restored. You do it\nwith sp_query_store_force_plan or the Force Plan button in SSMS.\nAzure Monitor, Log Analytics and Query Performance Insight are read-only monitoring tools, and none of\nthem can change which plan the optimizer uses.\nThe Regressed Queries pane is where you would find the regression and click Force Plan. The action itself\nis plan forcing.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/query-performance-insight-use?view=azuresql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/performance/monitoring-performance-by-using-the-\nquery-store?view=sql-server-ver17#information-in-the-query-store\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/performance/tune-performance-with-the-query-store?\nview=sql-server-ver17#performance-tuning-sample-queries\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/performance/query-store-usage-scenarios?view=sql-\nserver-ver17#pinpoint-and-fix-queries-with-plan-choice-regressions"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 73, "pages": [167, 167], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure container app named app1-contoso-001 that hosts a Data API builder (DAB) container\nin front of an Azure SQL database.\nYou add an entity named Todo that uses a source named dbo.todos.\nWhich URL pattern should clients use to access the Todo REST endpoint?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "https://app1-contoso-001.azurestaticapps.net/data-api/graphq1/Todo"}, {"id": "B", "text": "https://app1-contoso-001.azurestaticapps.net/api/Todo"}, {"id": "C", "text": "https://app1-contoso-001.azurestaticapps.net/data-api/Todo"}, {"id": "D", "text": "https://app1-contoso-001.azurestaticapps.net/data-api/api/Todo"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ73 T02\nCorrect answer: B) https://app1-contoso-001.azurestaticapps.net/api/Todo\nExplanation:\nWhen Data API builder (DAB) is hosted in a container (including Azure Container Apps), the default REST\npath configured in the runtime is /api. Combined with the entity name (Todo), clients reach the endpoint at /\napi/Todo.\nThe GraphQL-style path (/data-api/graphql/Todo) is incorrect because the question asks specifically for the\nREST endpoint, not GraphQL.\nThe path /data-api/Todo is incorrect because it omits the default REST root segment (/api) that DAB\nexpects.\nThe path /data-api/api/Todo applies only to the Static Web Apps Database Connections feature (where a\nfixed /data-api prefix is added in front of the configured REST path). The scenario describes a Container\nApp hosting a DAB container, so the Static Web Apps prefix is not used.\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/tutorial-deploy-container-app-cli\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-api-builder/deployment/azure-container-apps"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 74, "pages": [167, 169], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named Customer. Customer contains the following\ncolumns:\nNationalIDNumber is unique per customer.\nInvestigationNotes contains free-form text.\nYou have a stored procedure that performs point lookups by NationalIDNumber and returns\nInvestigationNotes in the query results without filtering on InvestigationNotes.\nYou need to encrypt both columns by using Always Encrypted and the highest security possible.\nWhich type of Always Encrypted encryption should you use for each column?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "deterministic for NationalIDNumber and randomized for InvestigationNotes"}, {"id": "B", "text": "deterministic for NationalIDNumber and deterministic for InvestigationNotes"}, {"id": "C", "text": "randomized for NationalIDNumber and randomized for InvestigationNotes"}, {"id": "D", "text": "randomized for NationalIDNumber and deterministic for InvestigationNotes"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ74 T02\nRespuesta correcta: A. deterministic for NationalIDNumber and randomized for InvestigationNotes\nPor qué es correcta la A:\nEn Always Encrypted (sin secure enclaves, que es el escenario estándar que asume la pregunta) hay dos\ntipos de cifrado:\nDeterministic: genera siempre el mismo ciphertext para el mismo plaintext. Permite point lookups\n(búsquedas por igualdad, WHERE columna = @valor), equality joins, GROUP BY e indexación. Tiene una\npequeña pérdida de información (se pueden detectar valores iguales por el patrón del ciphertext).\nRandomized: genera un ciphertext diferente cada vez que se cifra el mismo valor. Ofrece mayor seguridad\n(no revela patrones de igualdad), pero no permite ninguna operación sobre la columna cifrada (ni equality\nsearches, ni joins, ni indexes, ni filtros).\nLa pregunta dice explícitamente:\n- Tienes un stored procedure que hace point lookups por NationalIDNumber.\n- Devuelve InvestigationNotes sin filtrar sobre esa columna.\n- Quieres la mayor seguridad posible.\nPor tanto:\nNationalIDNumber → deterministic (necesario para que el point lookup funcione).\nInvestigationNotes → randomized (máxima seguridad, y no se necesita buscar/filtrar sobre ella).\nEsto coincide exactamente con las recomendaciones oficiales de Microsoft.\nPor qué las demás son incorrectas:\nB: (deterministic + deterministic): InvestigationNotes no necesita deterministic. Usar deterministic en una\ncolumna de texto libre reduce la seguridad innecesariamente (permite detectar valores repetidos).\nC (randomized + randomized): NationalIDNumber no podría usarse en point lookups. El stored procedure\ndejaría de funcionar.\nD (randomized + deterministic): Al revés: NationalIDNumber no soportaría los point lookups, e\nInvestigationNotes tendría menos seguridad de la necesaria.\nReferencias oficiales de Microsoft\n- Always Encrypted overview (tipos de cifrado y operaciones soportadas):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/always-encrypted-database-\nengine\n- Recommend when to use Always Encrypted (ejemplo casi idéntico a la pregunta):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/security-azure-sql-database-azure-sql-managed-\ninstance/9-always-encrypted\n- Always Encrypted cryptography (detalle técnico de IV deterministic vs randomized):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/always-encrypted-\ncryptography\n- Tutorial oficial (ejemplo práctico con SSN = deterministic y Salary = randomized):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/security/encryption/always-encrypted-tutorial-\ngetting-started"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 75, "pages": [169, 169], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Your development team uses Microsoft Visual Studio Code with the MSSQL extension and the GitHub\nCopilot Chat extension.\nThe team connects to an Azure SQL database by using individual database logins and uses the @mssql\nchat participant to generate and run Transact-SQL queries from prompts.\nWhat is used to ensure that GitHub Copilot Chat-generated queries run in the context of the developer?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "SQL permissions"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC) permissions"}, {"id": "C", "text": "GitHub organization permissions"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Shared MCP instruction files"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ75 T02\nRespuesta correcta:\nSQL permissions.\nExplicación:\nEl equipo se conecta a Azure SQL Database usando logins individuales de base de datos. Cuando un\ndesarrollador usa el participante @mssql en GitHub Copilot Chat (dentro de la extensión MSSQL de VS\nCode), las consultas T-SQL que se generan y se ejecutan lo hacen bajo la conexión activa del\ndesarrollador.\nEso implica que:\nCopilot / @mssql no tiene una identidad propia ni permisos elevados.\nLas consultas se ejecutan con el login/usuario de base de datos con el que el desarrollador se conectó.\nPor tanto, lo que garantiza que las consultas se ejecuten “en el contexto del desarrollador” (y respeten\nexactamente lo que ese desarrollador puede o no puede hacer) son los SQL permissions de ese login/\nusuario.\nPor qué no las otras opciones\nAzure RBAC: controla el acceso a recursos de Azure (servidor, base de datos a nivel de Azure), no el\ncontexto de ejecución de las consultas T-SQL una vez conectado con un login de base de datos.\nGitHub organization permissions: controlan el acceso a Copilot/GitHub, no el contexto de ejecución en SQL.\nshared MCP instruction files: se usan para dar contexto/instrucciones al modelo (AGENTS.md,\nCONSTITUTION.md, etc.), pero no definen el identity bajo el que se ejecutan las consultas.\nReferencias\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/visual-studio-code-extensions/github-copilot/overview\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/tools/visual-studio-code-extensions/github-copilot/limitations-and-known-\nissues\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/ssms/github-copilot/execution-context\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/ssms/github-copilot/chat"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 76, "pages": [169, 171], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that supports an AI-enabled product catalog API. The database contains\na table named Sales.Orders.\nSales.Orders contains 20 million rows and has a nonclustered index on a column named CreateDate.\nThe API passes a date parameter named @OrderDate.\nYou have a stored procedure named get_latest_day_orders that filters by running the following query:\nWHERE CAST(CreateDate AS date) = @OrderDate.\nA review of the query execution reveals that the query causes an index scan.\nYou need to modify the predicate to ensure that Microsoft SQL Server can use an index seek on\nCreateDate for a single day.\nWhich WHERE clause should you use?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "WHERE CreateDate >= @OrderDate AND CreateDate < DATEADD(day, 1, @OrderDate)"}, {"id": "B", "text": "WHERE CreateDate LIKE CONVERT(char(10), @OrderDate, 121) + '%'"}, {"id": "C", "text": "WHERE LEFT(CONVERT(varchar(30), CreateDate, 121), 10) = CONVERT(char(10), @OrderDate,\n121)"}, {"id": "D", "text": "WHERE CONVERT(char(10), CreateDate, 121) = CONVERT(char(10), @OrderDate, 121)"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ76 T02\nCorrect Answer: WHERE CreateDate >= @OrderDate AND CreateDate < DATEADD(day, 1,\n@OrderDate)\nExplanation:\nPara que SQL Server pueda hacer un index seek, el predicado debe ser SARGable (Search ARGument-\nable). Si se aplica una función o un cálculo sobre la columna indexada, el motor tiene que leer todos los\nvalores, aplicar la función y solo después comparar. Eso produce un scan.\nEl predicado original, CAST(CreateDate AS date) = @OrderDate, envuelve la columna en una función. Por\neso se escanean los 20 millones de filas del índice sobre CreateDate.\nLa opción A deja CreateDate sin tocar y mueve toda la lógica al lado del parámetro. Usa un rango\nsemiabierto, [@OrderDate, @OrderDate + 1 día):\nEl optimizador puede ir directamente al primer valor >= @OrderDate en el índice no clustered y leer hacia\nadelante hasta < @OrderDate + 1 día.\nCubre el día completo, incluidas horas como 23:59:59.9999999. El patrón >= inicio AND < inicio + 1 día es\nel más seguro porque no depende de la precisión del tipo (datetime, datetime2, smalldatetime).\nPor qué las otras son incorrectas:\n(LIKE CONVERT(char(10), @OrderDate, 121) + '%'): LIKE es un operador de cadenas, así que\nCreateDate se convierte implícitamente a texto en cada fila. Eso lo hace no SARGable. El comodín al final\nsolo ayuda cuando la columna ya es de tipo cadena.\n(LEFT(CONVERT(varchar(30), CreateDate, 121), 10) = ...): aplica dos funciones (CONVERT y LEFT)\nsobre la columna, por lo que no es SARGable y produce un scan.\n(CONVERT(char(10), CreateDate, 121) = ...): aplica CONVERT sobre la columna, así que sigue siendo no\nSARGable y produce un scan, con una comparación de cadenas aún más costosa que el CAST original.\nConsejo de examen: cuando la pregunta pide un index seek, la respuesta es la que deja la columna\n\"desnuda\" a la izquierda de la comparación. Cualquier función o aritmética va en el lado del parámetro o la\nconstante. Para filtrar por un día en una columna de fecha y hora, usa >= inicio AND < inicio + 1 día.\nComo alternativa, si no se puede reescribir la consulta, se puede crear una columna calculada indexada\ncon CAST(CreateDate AS date). No es una de las opciones de esta pregunta.\nReferences:\nSARGability: las funciones sobre columnas impiden el seek. Resolve blocking problems caused by lock\nescalation, \"Prevent lock escalation\": https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/sql/database-engine/\nperformance/resolve-blocking-problems-caused-lock-escalation#prevent-lock-escalation\nCONVERT() y CAST() en el WHERE causan scans; la columna calculada indexada como alternativa.\nTroubleshoot high-CPU-usage issues in SQL Server, Step 6: https://learn.microsoft.com/troubleshoot/sql/\ndatabase-engine/performance/troubleshoot-high-cpu-usage-issues#step-6-investigate-and-resolve-\nsargability-issues\nEjemplos de predicados no SARGables (funciones, conversiones implícitas). Post-migration validation and\noptimization guide: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/post-migration-validation-and-\noptimization-guide?view=sql-server-ver17#inability-to-use-predicates-to-filter-data\nDiseño de índices y definición de columnas SARGables. SQL Server and Azure SQL index architecture and\ndesign guide: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/sql-server-index-design-guide?view=sql-\nserver-ver17#query-considerations"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 77, "pages": [171, 175], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p172-109", "p172-110"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You need to generate embeddings to resolve the issues identified by the analysts.\nWhich column should you use?\nCase Study Title (Case Study):\nCase Study\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\nAzure Environment\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupportTickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); FleetId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(256))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(200)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusLastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n  \"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill.\",\n  \"category\": \"Battery\",\n  \"metadata\": {\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\", \"device\": \"Android\", \"language\": \"en-GB\"}\n}"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission.\nProblem Statements\nContoso is deploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the\nfollowing:\nAI workloads\nVector search\nModernized API access\nRetrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines\nSometimes the ingestion pipeline fails due to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads.\nThe engineers at Contoso report that the following dashboard query runs slowly:\nSQL\nSELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealthFROM dbo.\nVehicleHealthSummaryWHERE FleetId = @FleetIdORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;\nYou review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports.\nSecurity Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security requirements:\nRestrict the support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email\naddresses and phone numbers.\nEnforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which they are assigned. The\nanalysts can be assigned to multiple fleets.\nDatabase Performance and Requirements\nContoso identifies the following telemetry requirements:\nTelemetry data must be stored in a partitioned table.\nTelemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention operations.\nlatitude, longitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change.\nAvoid recursive updates.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nContoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following requirements:\nEmbeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables.\nEmbeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\nChunking must preserve semantic coherence.\nHybrid search must combine the following:\nVector similarity\nKeyword filtering or boosting\nDevelopment Requirements\nThe development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot and will\nretrieve live metadata from the databases.\nContoso identifies the following requirements for querying data in the FeedbackJson column of the\nCustomerFeedback table:\nExtract the customer feedback text from the JSON document.\nFilter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword.\nCalculate a fuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description.\nOrder the results by similarity score, with the highest score first."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "VehicleLocation"}, {"id": "B", "text": "IncidentDescription"}, {"id": "C", "text": "IncidentType"}, {"id": "D", "text": "SeverityScore"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ77 - T03\nRESPUESTA CORRECTA: ** IncidentDescription**\nEXPLICACION:\n**IncidentDescription** es el unico campo de **texto libre y de gran longitud (nvarchar(max))** en\nVehicleIncidentReports, por lo que es el campo de lenguaje natural que realmente contiene el detalle\nnarrativo de clima, trafico y ubicacion que los analistas necesitan comparar semanticamente.\nEl requisito del caso de estudio es explicito en dos puntos:\n- \"VehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\" -> el problema es de **busqueda\nsemantica sobre texto descriptivo**, no de filtrado estructurado.\n- \"Embeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\" -> requisito explicito de la seccion AI\nSearch, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing.\nRevisando el esquema de VehicleIncidentReports:\n- IncidentType: nvarchar(50) -> No es texto en lenguaje natural rico, es una categoria corta (taxonomia fija)\n- VehicleLocation: nvarchar(200) -> No es texto en lenguaje natural rico, es un dato estructurado/geografico\ncorto, no narrativo\n- **IncidentDescription: nvarchar(max) -> SI es texto en lenguaje natural rico**, campo de texto libre,\nlongitud ilimitada, redactado en prosa\n- SeverityScore: int -> No es texto, es un valor numerico sin semantica textual\nSolo IncidentDescription permite que el texto narrativo (que, segun el enunciado, suele mencionar clima,\ntrafico y ubicacion) se convierta en un **vector semantico** con un modelo de embeddings (p. ej., text-\nembedding-3-large via **AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS** o un endpoint de Azure OpenAI), para luego\nalmacenarlo en una columna **VECTOR** (como ya existe el patron Embeddings vector(1536) en\nVehicleHealthSummary) y habilitar comparaciones por similitud coseno/producto punto entre incidentes.\nPOR QUE LAS OTRAS OPCIONES SON INCORRECTAS:\nVehicleLocation: Es un campo corto (200 caracteres) con datos de ubicacion estructurados o\nsemiestructurados (coordenadas, direccion), no una descripcion narrativa rica. Generar embeddings de\neste campo captura muy poca semantic contextual y no resuelve el problema de \"encontrar incidentes\nsimilares por clima, trafico y ubicacion\" descrito en prosa.\nIncidentType: Es una categoria corta y acotada (nvarchar(50)), probablemente un valor de una lista fija (p.\nej., \"Collision\", \"Breakdown\"). No aporta el detalle contextual necesario; ademas, para datos categoricos de\nbaja cardinalidad, un **filtro exacto (WHERE IncidentType = ...)** es mas apropiado que una busqueda\nvectorial.\nSeverityScore: Es un int, no texto. Los embeddings se generan a partir de lenguaje natural, no de valores\nnumericos escalares; no existe una representacion semantica significativa para este campo mediante un\nmodelo de embeddings de texto.\nREFERENCIAS:\n- Generate embeddings in SQL Server/Azure SQL Database: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-\ndatabases/vectors/vectors-sql-server\n- AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/ai-\ngenerate-embeddings-transact-sql\n- Vector similarity search in SQL Server: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/vectors/vector-\nsearch\n- Exact nearest neighbor and vector indexes overview: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/\nvectors/vector-indexes"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 78, "pages": [175, 179], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p176-111", "p177-112", "p177-113"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You need to enable similarity search to provide the analysts with the ability to retrieve the most relevant\nhealth summary reports. The solution must minimize latency.\nWhat should you include in the solution?\nCase Study Title (Case Study):\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\nAzure Environment\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupportTickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); FleetId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(256))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(200)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusLastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n  \"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill.\",\n  \"category\": \"Battery\",\n  \"metadata\": {\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\", \"device\": \"Android\", \"language\": \"en-GB\"}\n}"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission.\nProblem Statements\nContoso is deploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the\nfollowing:\nAI workloads\nVector search\nModernized API access\nRetrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines\nSometimes the ingestion pipeline fails due to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads.\nThe engineers at Contoso report that the following dashboard query runs slowly."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "SELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "You review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports.\nSecurity Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security requirements:\nRestrict the support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email\naddresses and phone numbers.\nEnforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which they are assigned. The\nanalysts can be assigned to multiple fleets.\nDatabase Performance and Requirements\nContoso identifies the following telemetry requirements:\nTelemetry data must be stored in a partitioned table.\nTelemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention operations.\nlatitude, longitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change.\nAvoid recursive updates.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nContoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following requirements:\nEmbeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables.\nEmbeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\nChunking must preserve semantic coherence.\nHybrid search must combine the following:\nVector similarity\nKeyword filtering or boosting\nDevelopment Requirements\nThe development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot and will\nretrieve live metadata from the databases.\nContoso identifies the following requirements for querying data in the FeedbackJson column of the\nCustomerFeedback table:\nExtract the customer feedback text from the JSON document.\nFilter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword.\nCalculate a fuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description.\nOrder the results by similarity score, with the highest score first."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "a computed column that manually compares vector values"}, {"id": "B", "text": "a standard nonclustered index on the Embeddings (vector (1536)) column"}, {"id": "C", "text": "a full-text index on the Embeddings (vector (1536)) column"}, {"id": "D", "text": "a vector index on the Embeddings (vector (1536)) column"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ78 T03\nCorrect Answer:\na vector index on the Embeddings (vector (1536)) column\nExplanation:\nEl requisito es buscar por similitud minimizando la latencia. CREATE VECTOR INDEX crea un índice\naproximado (DiskANN) que permite búsqueda ANN con SELECT TOP (N) WITH APPROXIMATE ... FROM\nVECTOR_SEARCH(...). Sin él, la búsqueda es un kNN que recorre toda la tabla. Disponible de forma oficial\nen Azure SQL Database y en SQL database en Fabric.\na computed column that manually compares vector values. Incorrecta. Columna calculada que compara\nvectores: Los vectores no admiten operadores de comparación ni aritméticos, y una columna calculada no\nes una estructura de búsqueda. Sería una comparación fila a fila (kNN exacto), lo contrario de baja latencia.\na standard nonclustered index on the Embeddings (vector (1536)) column. Incorrecta. Índice no clustered\nestándar: No se pueden usar índices B-tree ni columnstore sobre columnas vector. Solo pueden ir como\ncolumna INCLUDE, lo que no ayuda a buscar por similitud, porque un vector no tiene orden ni igualdad.\na full-text index on the Embeddings (vector (1536)) column. Incorrecta. Índice full-text: Indexa texto para\nbúsquedas por palabra clave. Embeddings es un vector(1536), no texto. Serviría para la parte de keyword\nde una búsqueda híbrida, no para la similitud vectorial.\nReferences:\nCREATE VECTOR INDEX: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-vector-index-transact-\nsql?view=sql-server-ver17\nVECTOR_SEARCH: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-search-transact-sql?view=sql-\nserver-ver17\nVector search y vector indexes: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/sql-server/ai/vectors?view=sql-server-ver17\nVector data type, sección Limitations > Indexes: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/data-types/vector-data-\ntype?view=sql-server-ver17#limitations"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 79, "pages": [179, 183], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p176-111"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You need to recommend a solution for the development team to retrieve the live metadata.\nThe solution must meet the development requirements.\nWhat should you include in the recommendation?\nCase Study Title (Case Study):\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\nAzure Environment\nContoso has an Azure subscription in North Europe that contains the corporate infrastructure. The current\ninfrastructure contains a Microsoft SQL Server 2017 database. The database contains the following tables."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Table names | Column names\nCustomerFeedback | FeedbackId (int) (primarykey); FeedbackJson (nvarchar(max))\nFleets | FleetId (int) (primarykey); FleetName (nvarchar(100)); Description (nvarchar(256))\nMaintenanceEvents | MaintenanceId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); LastModifiedUtc (datetime2); Description (nvarchar(256))\nSupportTickets | TicketId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); CreatedUtc (datetime2)\nUserAccounts | UserId (int) (primarykey); UserPrincipalName (nvarchar(256)); JobRole (nvarchar(256)); StartDate (datetime2)\nVehicleIncidentReports | IncidentId (int) (primarykey); VehicleId (int); FleetId (int); IncidentType (nvarchar(50)); VehicleLocation (nvarchar(200)); IncidentDescription (nvarchar(max)); SeverityScore (int)\nVehicles | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); VIN (nvarchar(50)); VehicleDescription (nvarchar(256))\nVehicleHealthSummary | VehicleId (int) (primarykey); FleetId (int); Summary (nvarchar(200)); LastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); EngineStatus (bit); EngineStatusLastUpdatedUtc (datetime2); BatteryHealth (int); Embeddings (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "The FeedbackJson column has a full-text index and stores JSON documents in the following format.\n{\n\"text\": \"The battery drains too fast when driving uphill.\",\n\"category\": \"Battery\",\n\"metadata\": {\n\"appVersion\": \"5.2.1\",\n\"device\": \"Android\",\n\"language\": \"en-GB\"\n}\n}\nThe support staff at Contoso never has the UNMASK permission.\nProblem Statements\nContoso is deploying a new Azure SQL database that will become the authoritative data store for the\nfollowing:\nAI workloads\nVector search\nModernized API access\nRetrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines\nSometimes the ingestion pipeline fails due to malformed JSON and duplicate payloads.\nThe engineers at Contoso report that the following dashboard query runs slowly.\nSELECT VehicleId, LastUpdatedUtc, EngineStatus, BatteryHealth\nFROM dbo.VehicleHealthSummary\nWHERE FleetId = @FleetId\nORDER BY LastUpdatedUtc DESC;\nYou review the execution plan and discover that the plan shows a clustered index scan.\nVehicleIncidentReports often contains details about the weather, traffic conditions, and location. Analysts\nreport that it is difficult to find similar incidents based on these details.\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nContoso wants to modernize Fleet Intelligence Platform to support AI-powered semantic search over\nincident reports.\nSecurity Requirements\nContoso identifies the following security requirements:\nRestrict the support staff from viewing Personally Identifiable Information (PII) data, which is full email\naddresses and phone numbers.\nEnforce row-level filtering so that analysts see only incidents for the fleets to which they are assigned. The\nanalysts can be assigned to multiple fleets.\nDatabase Performance and Requirements\nContoso identifies the following telemetry requirements:\nTelemetry data must be stored in a partitioned table.\nTelemetry data must provide predictable performance for ingestion and retention operations.\nlatitude, longitude, and accuracy JSON properties must be filtered by using an index seek.\nContoso identifies the following maintenance data requirements:\nEnsure that any changes to a row in the MaintenanceEvents table updates the corresponding value in the\nLastModifiedUtc column to the time of the change.\nAvoid recursive updates.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nContoso plans to implement semantic search over incident data to meet the following requirements:\nEmbeddings must be stored in dedicated Azure SQL Database tables.\nEmbeddings must be generated from rich natural language fields.\nChunking must preserve semantic coherence.\nHybrid search must combine the following:\nVector similarity\nKeyword filtering or boosting\nDevelopment Requirements\nThe development team at Contoso will use Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot and will\nretrieve live metadata from the databases.\nContoso identifies the following requirements for querying data in the FeedbackJson column of the\nCustomerFeedback table:\nExtract the customer feedback text from the JSON document.\nFilter rows where the JSON text contains a keyword.\nCalculate a fuzzy similarity score between the feedback text and a known issue description.\nOrder the results by similarity score, with the highest score first."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Export the database schema as a .dacpac file and load the schema into a GitHub Copilot context\nwindow."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Add the schema to a GitHub Copilot instruction file."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Use an MCP server."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Include the database project in the code repository."}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ79 T03\nCorrect Answer\nUse an MCP server.\nExplanation\nLa respuesta correcta es Use an MCP server porque el requisito clave del escenario es que el equipo\nutilizará Visual Studio Code y GitHub Copilot y necesita recuperar metadatos en vivo de las bases de datos.\nMCP (Model Context Protocol) proporciona una arquitectura cliente-servidor mediante la cual aplicaciones\nde IA, como GitHub Copilot en Agent Mode, pueden conectarse a servicios externos y utilizar herramientas\nproporcionadas por un servidor MCP. El servidor puede consultar directamente el sistema de origen y\ndevolver información actualizada en el momento de la petición. Microsoft documenta específicamente la\nintegración de servidores MCP con GitHub Copilot en Visual Studio Code. Microsoft Learn\nPara este escenario resulta especialmente relevante la implementación de SQL MCP Server. Microsoft\nmuestra que un servidor MCP conectado a una base de datos SQL puede exponerse a GitHub Copilot en\nVisual Studio Code y permite realizar peticiones como consultar qué tablas están disponibles. Es decir,\nCopilot obtiene información directamente de la base de datos en lugar de depender de una copia estática\ndel esquema. Microsoft Learn\nAdemás, la integración actual de GitHub Copilot con la extensión MSSQL confirma el mismo principio:\ncuando existe conexión con la base de datos, las herramientas de Agent Mode pueden trabajar con el\nesquema real y devolver información procedente de la base de datos conectada. Microsoft Learn\nRespecto a las demás respuestas:\n- Export the database schema as a .dacpac file and load the schema into a GitHub Copilot context window:\nun archivo DACPAC representa una instantánea del esquema en el momento en que se genera. Si\nposteriormente cambia la base de datos, el DACPAC no se actualiza automáticamente. Por tanto, no\nproporciona live metadata.\n- Add the schema to a GitHub Copilot instruction file: los archivos de instrucciones sirven para proporcionar\nreglas, convenciones y contexto a Copilot. Incluir manualmente el esquema proporcionaría información\nestática, pero no crea una conexión con la base de datos ni recupera los cambios del esquema en tiempo\nreal.\n- Include the database project in the code repository: un database project permite mantener el modelo de la\nbase de datos como código y controlar sus cambios mediante Git, pero representa el estado definido en el\nrepositorio. No garantiza que ese esquema sea exactamente el que está desplegado actualmente en la\nbase de datos y, por tanto, tampoco satisface por sí solo el requisito de recuperar metadatos en vivo.\n- Use an MCP server: permite que GitHub Copilot invoque herramientas que se comunican con la fuente de\ndatos real. Por ello es la única opción de las propuestas específicamente orientada a proporcionar acceso\ndinámico a sistemas externos y recuperar información actualizada.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Learn — What is the Azure MCP Server?\nExplica la arquitectura MCP y cómo Visual Studio Code actúa como host, GitHub Copilot Agent Mode como\ncliente MCP y los servidores MCP proporcionan herramientas y acceso a recursos externos.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/developer/azure-mcp-server/overview\nMicrosoft Learn — Get started with the Azure MCP Server using Visual Studio Code\nDocumenta la conexión entre GitHub Copilot Agent Mode, Visual Studio Code y Azure MCP Server,\npermitiendo que Copilot interactúe con recursos externos mediante herramientas MCP.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/developer/azure-mcp-server/get-started/tools/visual-studio-code\nMicrosoft Learn — Quickstart: Create a Hosted MCP Server in Connector Namespace\nEs la referencia más directa para esta pregunta: muestra un SQL MCP Server conectado a GitHub Copilot\nen Visual Studio Code y utiliza como ejemplo la consulta “What tables are available?”, demostrando la\nrecuperación de información directamente desde SQL.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/connector-namespace/hosted-mcp-quickstart\nMicrosoft Learn — How GitHub Copilot works with the MSSQL Extension\nExplica que las herramientas de Agent Mode tienen conocimiento del esquema mediante llamadas a\nherramientas y que los resultados proceden de la base de datos conectada, a diferencia del contexto\npuramente estático.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/visual-studio-code-extensions/github-copilot/how-it-works?\nview=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 80, "pages": [183, 185], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p184-114"], "type": "drag", "template": "Serialize the order-level JSON: {{0}}\nGenerate a nested lines array: {{1}}\nExtract a single scalar value from the JSON text: {{2}}", "pools": [["FOR JSON PATH", "JSON_MODIFY", "JSON_QUERY", "JSON_VALUE", "OPENJSON"], ["FOR JSON PATH", "JSON_MODIFY", "JSON_QUERY", "JSON_VALUE", "OPENJSON"], ["FOR JSON PATH", "JSON_MODIFY", "JSON_QUERY", "JSON_VALUE", "OPENJSON"]], "correct": [2, 0, 3], "image": 114, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named SalesDB that contains tables named Sales.Order and Sales.\nOrderLines. Both tables contain sales data.\nYou have a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) service that queries SalesDB to retrieve order details\nand passes the results to a large language model (LLM) as JSON text. The following is a simple of the\nJSON.\n[\n{\n\"orderHeaderId\": 102348,\n\"orderNumber\": \"SO-2026-000912\",\n\"orderDateUtc\": \"2026-01-28T14:22:09Z\",\n\"customerId\": 77821,\n\"currencyCode\": \"EUR\",\n\"orderTotal\": 149.97,\n\"lines\": [\n{\n\"lineNumber\": 1,\n\"productId\": 5012,\n\"sku\": \"CBL-USB-C-1M\",\n\"quantity\": 2,\n\"unitPrice\": 19.99,\n\"lineTotal\": 39.98\n},\n{\n\"lineNumber\": 2,\n\"productId\": 8841,\n\"sku\": \"HUB-USB-C-7P\",\n\"quantity\": 1,\n\"unitPrice\": 109.99,\n\"lineTotal\": 109.99\n}\n]\n}\n]\nYou need to return one JSON document per order that includes the order header fields and an array of\nrelated order lines. The LLM must receive a single JSON array of orders, where each order contains a lines\nproperty that is a JSON array of line items.\nWhich Transact-SQL commands should you use to produce the required JSON shape from the relational\ntables? To answer, drag the appropriate commands to the correct operations. Each command may be used\nonce, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view\ncontent.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p184-115.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ80 T03\n2. Correct Answer:\nSerialize the order-level JSON: JSON_QUERY\nGenerate a nested lines array: FOR JSON PATH\nExtract a single scalar value from the JSON text: JSON_VALUE\n3. Explanation:\nPara Serialize the order-level JSON, la respuesta es JSON_QUERY. JSON_QUERY trabaja con\nfragmentos JSON que representan objetos o matrices. Además, cuando se combina con FOR JSON,\npermite que un fragmento que ya contiene JSON válido se incluya como JSON y no como una cadena de\ntexto escapada. Esto es importante para conservar correctamente la estructura JSON que recibirá el LLM.\nMicrosoft Learn\nPara Generate a nested lines array, la respuesta es FOR JSON PATH. Esta cláusula convierte los\nresultados de una consulta SELECT en JSON. Por tanto, una subconsulta sobre Sales.OrderLines con\nFOR JSON PATH permite transformar las filas relacionadas de cada pedido en una matriz JSON de líneas,\nque puede incorporarse como la propiedad lines del pedido. Microsoft Learn\nPara Extract a single scalar value from the JSON text, la respuesta es JSON_VALUE. Esta función está\ndiseñada específicamente para extraer un valor escalar de un documento JSON, como un identificador,\nuna fecha, un código o un importe. Microsoft distingue expresamente JSON_VALUE, que devuelve\nescalares, de JSON_QUERY, que devuelve objetos o matrices. Microsoft Learn\nLas demás opciones no corresponden a estas operaciones. JSON_MODIFY se utiliza para modificar\nvalores o propiedades de un documento JSON, no para extraer un escalar ni para generar una matriz a\npartir de filas relacionales. OPENJSON realiza fundamentalmente la operación inversa: analiza JSON y lo\ntransforma en filas y columnas que pueden procesarse mediante Transact-SQL. Microsoft Learn\n4. References:\nMicrosoft Learn — JSON_QUERY (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/json-query-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nMicrosoft Learn — Work with JSON data in SQL Server:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/json/json-data-sql-server?view=sql-server-ver17\nMicrosoft Learn — Validate, query, and change JSON data with built-in functions:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/json/validate-query-and-change-json-data-with-\nbuilt-in-functions-sql-server?view=sql-server-ver17\nMicrosoft Learn — JSON functions (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/json-functions-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 81, "pages": [185, 187], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p186-116", "p186-117"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "Function: {{0}}\nDistance metric: {{1}}", "pools": [["VECTOR_DISTANCE", "VECTOR_NORM", "VECTOR_SEARCH"], ["cosine distance", "dot product", "Euclidean distance"]], "correct": [2, 0], "image": 117, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains the following tables and columns."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "Tables | Columns\nArticles | ArticleId (int); Title (nvarchar(200)); Notes (nvarchar(max)); Description (nvarchar(max))\nNotesEmbeddings | ArticleId (int); ChunkIndex (int); Embedding (vector(1536))\nDescriptionEmbeddings | ArticleId (int); ChunkIndex (int); Embedding (vector(1536))"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Embeddings in the NotesEmbeddings and DescriptionEmbeddings tables have been generated from values\nin the Description and Notes columns of the Articles table by using different chunk sizes.\nYou need to perform approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) queries across both embedding tables. The\nsolution must minimize the impact of using different chunk sizes.\nWhat should you use? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p187-118.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ81 T03\nRespuesta Correcta:\nFunction: VECTOR_SEARCH\nDistance metric: cosine distance\nExplicación:\nPara realizar consultas de Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) en Azure SQL Database sobre tablas de\nembeddings, se debe usar la función VECTOR_SEARCH. Esta función aprovecha los índices vectoriales\npara devolver resultados aproximados de forma eficiente, a diferencia de VECTOR_DISTANCE (que\nrealiza un cálculo exacto de distancia y no usa índices) o VECTOR_NORM (que solo normaliza un vector).\nCuando los embeddings se generan a partir de campos de texto con diferentes tamaños de chunk (como\nNotes y Description), la métrica cosine distance es la más adecuada. Cosine mide la similitud angular entre\nvectores e ignora diferencias de magnitud que pueden introducirse por el chunking, minimizando así el\nimpacto de usar tamaños de fragmento distintos. Dot product y Euclidean distance son más sensibles a la\nmagnitud y no minimizan ese impacto de la misma manera.\nReferencias:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-search-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/sql-server/ai/vectors\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-distance-transact-sql"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 82, "pages": [187, 191], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p189-119"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "CREATE VECTOR INDEX idx_products_embedding ON dbo.Products ({{0}})\nWITH (METRIC = 'cosine', TYPE = 'DISKANN');\nDECLARE @query_vector VECTOR(1536) = @SuppliedVector\nSELECT t.product_id, t.product_name, s.distance\nFROM {{1}} (\n TABLE = dbo.Products AS t,\n COLUMN = embedding,\n SIMILAR_TO = @query_vector,\n METRIC = 'cosine',\n TOP_N = 10\n) AS s\n{{2}};", "pools": [["distance", "embedding", "product_name"], ["VECTOR_DISTANCE", "VECTOR_NORMALIZE", "VECTOR_SEARCH"], ["GROUP BY s.distance", "ORDER BY s.distance", "PARTITION BY s.distance"]], "correct": [1, 2, 1], "image": 119, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\nYou have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric named SalesD8 that contains a table named dbo.Products.\nYou need to modify SalesDB to meet the following requirements:\nCreate a vector index on the appropriate column.\nUse a supplied natural language query vector. How should you complete the Transact-SQL code? To\nanswer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p190-120.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ82 T02\nCorrect Answer:\nembedding\nVECTOR_SEARCH\nORDER BY s.distance\nExplanation:\nPara crear un índice vectorial sobre la columna adecuada que almacena los embeddings (columna de tipo\nVECTOR), se debe especificar la columna embedding en la instrucción CREATE VECTOR INDEX.\nEsto coincide con la sintaxis documentada CREATE VECTOR INDEX index_name ON object\n(vector_column) y permite realizar búsquedas aproximadas de vecinos más cercanos utilizando el índice\nDiskANN con la métrica cosine.\nLa consulta debe utilizar la función con valores de tabla VECTOR_SEARCH para ejecutar la búsqueda\naproximada de similitud vectorial sobre la columna indexada.\nLa función recibe la tabla, la columna vectorial, el vector de consulta suministrado, la métrica y (en la\nsintaxis utilizada en esta pregunta) el parámetro TOP_N, y devuelve las filas coincidentes junto con una\ncolumna calculada distance.\nPor último, los resultados deben ordenarse por la distancia devuelta por VECTOR_SEARCH. La\ndocumentación indica que la columna distance es la única clave de ordenación válida para los resultados\nde búsqueda vectorial aproximada y que debe ordenarse en orden ascendente.\ndistance es incorrecta para la creación del índice porque es una columna de resultado calculada generada\npor VECTOR_SEARCH, no una columna que exista en la tabla.\nproduct_name es incorrecta para la creación del índice porque es una columna de tipo cadena normal y no\nse puede utilizar para crear un índice vectorial.\nVECTOR_DISTANCE es incorrecta porque es una función escalar que calcula la distancia entre dos\nvectores individuales; no realiza una búsqueda aproximada de vecinos más cercanos sobre una tabla\nindexada.\nVECTOR_NORMALIZE es incorrecta porque es una función escalar que normaliza un único vector; no\ninterviene en la búsqueda sobre un índice.\nGROUP BY s.distance es incorrecta porque agrupar por la columna de distancia no es necesario y\ncolapsaría las filas distintas de vecinos más cercanos.\nPARTITION BY s.distance es incorrecta porque es una cláusula de función de ventana y no ordena el\nconjunto de resultados final.\nReferences:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-vector-index-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-search-transact-sql"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 83, "pages": [191, 192], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named dbo.Products. dbo.Products contains three\ncolumns named Embedding, Category, and Price. The Embedding column is defined as VECTOR(1536).\nYou use AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS and VECTOR_SEARCH to support semantic search and apply\nadditional filters on two columns named Category and Price.\nYou plan to change the embedding model from text-embedding-ada-002 to text-embedding-3-small.\nExisting rows already contain embeddings in the Embedding column.\nYou need to implement the model change. Applications must be able to use VECTOR_SEARCH without\nruntime errors.\nWhat should you do first?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Regenerate embeddings for the existing rows."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Normalize the vector lengths before storing new embeddings."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Convert the Embedding column to nvarchar(max)."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Create a vector index on dbo.Products.Embedding."}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ83 T03\nRespuesta correcta:\nRegenerar los embeddings de las filas existentes.\nExplicación:\nLa columna Embedding es VECTOR(1536), y tanto text-embedding-ada-002 como text-embedding-3-small\ndevuelven vectores de 1.536 dimensiones. Por eso la columna no necesita cambios de tipo ni de tamaño.\nEl problema es otro: los vectores que ya están almacenados los generó ada-002, y los vectores de consulta\nque se generarán con AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS usarán text-embedding-3-small. Cada modelo\nproduce su propio espacio vectorial, así que comparar vectores de modelos distintos no da distancias con\nsentido. Microsoft indica expresamente que no se puede \"actualizar\" entre modelos de embeddings y que,\npara cambiar de ada-002 a otro modelo, hay que generar embeddings nuevos. Por tanto, lo primero es\napuntar el modelo externo al nuevo modelo y volver a calcular la columna Embedding de todas las filas con\nAI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS (por ejemplo, con un UPDATE sobre dbo.Products). Después,\nVECTOR_SEARCH compara vectores de la misma procedencia y los filtros por Category y Price se aplican\nsobre resultados coherentes.\nNormalize the vector lengths before storing new embeddings. es incorrecta porque no hay ninguna\ndiferencia de longitud entre los vectores que corregir: ambos modelos generan 1.536 dimensiones.\nAdemás, normalizar la longitud de un vector no lo traslada al espacio del otro modelo, así que los\nembeddings antiguos seguirían siendo incompatibles con los nuevos.\nConvert the Embedding column to nvarchar(max). es incorrecta porque VECTOR_SEARCH exige que la\ncolumna de búsqueda sea del tipo de datos vector, y que SIMILAR_TO sea una variable o columna de tipo\nvector. Convertir Embedding a nvarchar(max) haría que VECTOR_SEARCH dejara de funcionar sobre esa\ncolumna y perdería las ventajas del tipo vector.\nCreate a vector index on dbo.Products.Embedding. es incorrecta porque un índice vectorial no es un\nrequisito previo: VECTOR_SEARCH también funciona sin índice, haciendo una búsqueda exacta (kNN)\nsobre todas las filas. El índice solo acelera la búsqueda aproximada sobre los vectores almacenados.\nCrearlo ahora indexaría los embeddings generados con el modelo anterior y no resolvería la\nincompatibilidad con el nuevo modelo, así que, en todo caso, sería un paso posterior a regenerar los datos.\nReferencias:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/ai-generate-embeddings-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-search-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/data-types/vector-data-type\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-vector-index-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/alter-external-model-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 84, "pages": [192, 194], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p193-121"], "type": "drag", "template": "Serialize the retrieved rows from knowledge_base: {{0}}\nExtract the answer field from the response: {{1}}\nExtract the embeddings to store in query_cache: {{2}}", "pools": [["AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS", "FOR JSON PATH", "FOR XML PATH", "JSON_QUERY", "JSON_VALUE", "OPENJSON", "VECTOR_DISTANCE"], ["AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS", "FOR JSON PATH", "FOR XML PATH", "JSON_QUERY", "JSON_VALUE", "OPENJSON", "VECTOR_DISTANCE"], ["AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS", "FOR JSON PATH", "FOR XML PATH", "JSON_QUERY", "JSON_VALUE", "OPENJSON", "VECTOR_DISTANCE"]], "correct": [1, 4, 3], "image": 121, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "DRAG DROP\nYou have an Azure SQL database named DB1 that contains two tables named knowledge_base and\nquery_cache. knowledge_base contains support articles and embeddings. query_cache contains chat\nquestions, responses, and embeddings. DB1 supports an AI-enabled chat agent.\nYou need to design a solution that meets the following requirements:\n- Serializes the retrieved rows from knowledge_base\n- Extracts the answer field from the response\n- Extracts the embeddings to store in query_cache\n- You will call the external large language model (LLM) by using the sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint\nstored procedure.\nWhich Transact-SQL commands should you use for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate\ncommands to the correct requirements.\nEach command may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar\nbetween panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p193-122.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n# VCE 84 / Topic 03\nRespuestas Correcta:\n- Serialize the retrieved rows from knowledge_base: FOR JSON PATH\n- Extract the answer field from the response: JSON_VALUE\n- Extract the embeddings to store in query_cache: JSON_QUERY\nExplanation:\nPara construir el payload que se enviará al LLM mediante sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint, hay que\nconvertir el conjunto de filas recuperado de knowledge_base en un único documento JSON. FOR JSON\nPATH es la cláusula Transact-SQL diseñada precisamente para serializar los resultados de una consulta\n(varias filas) como un documento JSON, lo que además evita la sobrecarga de hacer una llamada HTTPS\npor cada fila.\nUna vez que sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint devuelve la respuesta del modelo en el parámetro\n@response, hay que extraer el campo de la respuesta (answer/content), que es un valor escalar (una\ncadena de texto). Para extraer valores escalares de un documento JSON se usa JSON_VALUE.\nPor último, para extraer los embeddings que se van a guardar en query_cache, hay que tener en cuenta\nque un embedding no es un valor escalar, sino un array/objeto JSON (un vector de números). Cuando se\nnecesita extraer un objeto o una matriz JSON en lugar de un valor único, la función adecuada es\nJSON_QUERY, mientras que JSON_VALUE fallaría o devolvería NULL al intentar extraer una estructura\ncompleja.\nLas demás opciones de la lista no encajan en ninguno de los tres requisitos:\n- AI_GENERATE_CHUNKS: se usa para dividir texto en fragmentos (chunking) antes de generar\nembeddings, no para serializar filas ni para parsear respuestas JSON.\n- FOR XML PATH: serializa resultados como XML, no como JSON, por lo que no es compatible con el\nformato que espera el endpoint REST.\n- OPENJSON: convierte un documento JSON en un conjunto de filas (filas/columnas), es decir, hace el\nproceso inverso al requerido para serializar knowledge_base.\n- VECTOR_DISTANCE: calcula la distancia/similitud entre dos vectores, útil para la búsqueda vectorial,\npero no sirve para serializar ni para extraer campos de un JSON.\nReferencias:\nMicrosoft Learn – sys.sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint (Transact-SQL), apartado \"Best practices – Use a\nbatching technique\": \"it's recommended to batch the rows into a single JSON document... This can be done\nusing the FOR JSON statement\"; ejemplo con JSON_QUERY(@response, '$.result.data[0].embedding')\npara extraer un embedding de la respuesta.\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-invoke-external-\nrest-endpoint-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nMicrosoft Learn – Training: Generar y procesar respuestas RAG (módulo \"Design and implement RAG with\nAzure SQL\"): \"Para llegar allí, use JSON_VALUE, que extrae valores escalares de JSON... Si alguna vez\nnecesita extraer un objeto JSON o una matriz en lugar de un solo valor, use JSON_QUERY en su lugar.\"\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/training/modules/design-implement-rag-with-sql/5-generate-process-rag-\nresponses"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 85, "pages": [194, 197], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p195-123"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "{\n \"search\": \"ocean view\",\n \"vectorQueries\": [{\n  \"vector\": [/* embedding array */],\n  \"fields\": \"DescriptionVector\",\n  \"k\": {{0}},\n  \"kind\": \"vector\"\n }],\n \"queryType\": {{1}},\n \"semanticConfiguration\": \"hotels\",\n \"captions\": {{2}},\n \"answers\": {{3}},\n \"top\": 10,\n \"hybridSearch\": {\"maxTextRecallSize\": 50}\n}", "pools": [["-50", "10", "50"], ["\"full\"", "\"semantic\"", "\"simple\""], ["\"extractive\"", "\"generative\"", "\"none\""], ["\"extractive\"", "\"generative\"", "\"none\""]], "correct": [2, 1, 0, 0], "image": 123, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\nYou have an Azure AI Search service and an index named hotels that includes a vector field named\nDescriptionVector.\nYou query hotels by using the Search Documents REST API.\nYou add semantic ranking to the hybrid search query and discover that some queries return fewer results\nthan expected, and captions and answers are missing.\nYou need to complete the hybrid search request to meet the following requirements:\nInclude more documents when ranking.\nAlways include captions and answers.\nHow should you complete the REST request body? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer\narea.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p196-124.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ85 T03\n---\nAnswers:\n1. 50\n2. semantic\n3. extractive\n4. extractive\n---\nExplanation:\n1.\n- \"k\": 50 es correcto. El ranker semántico solo reordena los 50 mejores resultados de la consulta inicial (la\nfusión RRF de la búsqueda de texto y la vectorial). Si k es menor, el ranker recibe menos documentos\ncandidatos y las consultas devuelven menos resultados de los esperados. Con k = 50 se incluyen más\ndocumentos en el ranking, y es coherente con \"maxTextRecallSize\": 50.\n- Por eso 10 no es correcto: limita los candidatos.\n- -50 tampoco lo es: k debe ser un entero positivo y un valor negativo no es válido.\n2.\n- \"queryType\": \"semantic\" es correcto. Para que se aplique el reranking semántico (y se generen captions y\nanswers) la solicitud debe usar queryType \"semantic\" junto con una semanticConfiguration, que aquí es\n\"hotels\".\n- Con \"simple\" o \"full\" solo se usa el analizador de consultas léxico (sintaxis simple o Lucene completa) y no\nse invoca el ranker semántico, por lo que seguirían sin aparecer captions ni answers.\n3.\n- \"captions\": \"extractive\" es correcto. Los captions semánticos se obtienen extrayendo pasajes relevantes\ndel contenido, y para que se devuelvan siempre hay que establecer el parámetro en \"extractive\".\n- \"none\" desactiva los captions, que es justo el problema descrito.\n- \"generative\" no es un valor admitido por este parámetro en la API de búsqueda de documentos: los\nvalores son \"extractive\" y \"none\".\n4.\n- \"answers\": \"extractive\" es correcto. Las respuestas semánticas se extraen de los documentos mejor\nclasificados y solo se devuelven si answers se establece en \"extractive\".\n- \"none\" no devuelve respuestas, y \"generative\" no es un valor admitido.\n---\nReferencias:\nMicrosoft Learn - Configure semantic ranker and return captions in search results (semantic ranking,\nqueryType, captions y answers):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/semantic-how-to-query-request\nMicrosoft Learn - Create a hybrid query in Azure AI Search (k, maxTextRecallSize y RRF):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/search/hybrid-search-how-to-query\nMicrosoft Learn - Documents - Search Post (REST API):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/searchservice/documents/search-post"}], "points": 4}, {"id": 86, "pages": [197, 200], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named dbo.ManualChunks. dbo.ManualChunks\ncontains product manuals.\nA retrieval query already returns the top five matching chunks as nvarchar(max) text.\nYou need to call an Azure OpenAI REST endpoint for chat completions. The request body must include both\nthe user question and the retrieved chunks.\nYou write the following Transact-SQL code.\n01 CREATE DATABASE SCOPED CREDENTIAL AzureOpenAIHeaders\n02 WITH IDENTITY = 'HTTPEndpointHeaders',\n03 SECRET = N'{\"api-key\":\"<YOUR_AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY>\"}';\n04 GO\n05 CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE dbo.AskManuals\n06 . . .\n07 SELECT @chunks =\n08 (\n09 SELECT TOP (5)\n10 mc.ChunkText AS [text]\n11 FROM dbo.ManualChunks AS mc\n12 ORDER BY mc.Score DESC\n13 FOR JSON PATH\n14 );\n15 SET @payload =\n16 (\n17 SELECT\n18 'system' AS [messages[0].role],\n19 'Use only the provided manual chunks.' AS [messages[0].content],\n20 'user' AS [messages[1].role],\n21 CONCAT(@question, CHAR(10), JSON_QUERY(@chunks)) AS [messages[1].content]\n22\n23 );\n24\n25 EXEC @retval =\n26 . . .\n27 END;\n28 GO\nWhat should you insert at line 22?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "FOR XML AUTO, TYPE, XMLSCHEMA"}, {"id": "B", "text": "FOR JSON AUTO, INCLUDE_NULL_VALUES"}, {"id": "C", "text": "FOR XML PATH, INCLUDE_NULL_VALUES"}, {"id": "D", "text": "FOR JSON PATH, WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ86 T03\nRespuesta\nLa respuesta correcta es:\nFOR JSON PATH, WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER\nSolución\nEn la línea 17-23 se está construyendo el @payload que debe enviarse a la API REST de Azure OpenAI\npara chat completions. El cuerpo (body) de una solicitud a esa API tiene este formato:\n{\n\"messages\": [\n{ \"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"...\" },\n{ \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"...\" }\n]\n}\nEs decir, debe ser un único objeto JSON, no un array de objetos. Al usar la sintaxis de alias con notación\nde punto ([messages[0].role], [messages[1].content], etc.) junto con FOR JSON PATH, SQL Server\nconstruye automáticamente la estructura anidada messages: [...] a partir de los nombres de columna.\nEl problema es que, por defecto, FOR JSON PATH envuelve siempre el resultado entre corchetes [ ] (como\nsi fuera un array), incluso cuando solo hay una fila. Como aquí SELECT devuelve una sola fila (es un\nSELECT sin agrupamiento, construyendo directamente el payload), hace falta eliminar ese wrapper de\narray externo para obtener un objeto JSON válido tal como lo espera la API de Azure OpenAI.\nPor eso se necesita la opción WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER, que elimina los corchetes que rodean la\nsalida de FOR JSON por defecto, generando un único objeto JSON como salida en lugar de un array.\nExplicación\nPor qué es correcta\nFOR JSON PATH permite construir JSON anidado usando notación de puntos en los alias de columna (p.\nej. [messages[0].role]), lo cual es justo lo que se necesita para construir la estructura messages: [{role,\ncontent}, {role, content}].\nWITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER quita los corchetes [ ] que FOR JSON añade por defecto, produciendo un\nobjeto JSON único en vez de un array. Esto es necesario porque el body de la API de Azure OpenAI espera\nun objeto JSON ({...}), no un array ([{...}]).\nEjemplo de la documentación oficial: una consulta con FOR JSON PATH, WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER\nsobre una fila produce {\"year\":2015,\"month\":12,\"day\":15} en lugar de [{\"year\":2015,\"month\":12,\"day\":15}].\nPor qué las otras opciones son incorrectas\nFOR XML AUTO, TYPE, XMLSCHEMA: Genera XML, no JSON. La API de Azure OpenAI espera un\ncuerpo en formato JSON, así que esta opción no serviría en absoluto.\nFOR JSON AUTO, INCLUDE_NULL_VALUES: FOR JSON AUTO determina la estructura del JSON\nautomáticamente a partir de la jerarquía de tablas/columnas del SELECT, en lugar de dejar control total\ncomo PATH. Aquí se necesita PATH para poder construir manualmente el array anidado messages[0],\nmessages[1] mediante los alias. Además, INCLUDE_NULL_VALUES solo sirve para incluir propiedades\ncon valor NULL, no resuelve el problema del wrapper de array.\nFOR XML PATH, INCLUDE_NULL_VALUES: De nuevo genera XML en lugar de JSON, por lo que no es\ncompatible con el formato que espera la API REST.\nResultado esperado\nCon FOR JSON PATH, WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER, el @payload resultante sería un objeto JSON\núnico como:\n{\n\"messages\": [\n{ \"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Use only the provided manual chunks.\" },\n{ \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"<pregunta del usuario>\\n<chunks recuperados>\" }\n]\n}\nEsto coincide exactamente con el formato que exige el endpoint de chat completions de Azure OpenAI.\nReferencias\nSELECT - FOR clause (Transact-SQL)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-for-clause-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17#for-json\nDescribe las opciones AUTO, PATH, INCLUDE_NULL_VALUES, ROOT y WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER\nde la cláusula FOR JSON.\nRemove Square Brackets from JSON - WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER Option\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/json/remove-square-brackets-from-json-without-array-\nwrapper-option?view=sql-server-ver17\nEjemplos de uso de WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER para producir un único objeto JSON en lugar de un\narray.\nFormat query results as JSON with FOR JSON\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/json/format-query-results-as-json-with-for-json-sql-\nserver?view=sql-server-ver17\nExplica las opciones adicionales de FOR JSON, incluida WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER, usada \"para\ngenerar un único objeto JSON como salida de un resultado de una sola fila\".\nGet started with JSON features (Azure SQL)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/json-features?view=azuresql#format-relational-data-in-\njson-format\nMuestra ejemplos prácticos de FOR JSON PATH, WITHOUT_ARRAY_WRAPPER para devolver un único\nobjeto JSON."}], "points": 1}, {"id": 87, "pages": [200, 203], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p201-125"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "Retrieve grounding data from: {{0}}\nInference step to perform the retrieval: {{1}}", "pools": [["employee_profiles and benefits_enrollment", "knowledge_base", "PDF exports of the policies", "The LLM training data"], ["Perform keyword searches.", "Call the LLM first, and then store the response.", "Fine-tune the LLM by using the data in knowledge_base.", "Generate query embeddings, and then run a vector similarity search."]], "correct": [1, 3], "image": 125, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\nYou have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named knowledge_base. knowledge_base stores\nhuman resources (HR) policy documents\nand contains columns named title, content, category, and embedding.\nYou have an application named App1. App1 queries two relational tables named employee_profiles and\nbenefits_enrollment that contain HR data.\nApp1 hosts a chatbot that calls a large language model (LLM) directly.\nUsers report that the chatbot answers general HR questions correctly but provides outdated or incorrect\nanswers when policies change. The\nchatbot also fails to answer questions that reference internal policy documents by title or category.\nYou need to recommend a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) solution to resolve the chatbot issues.\nWhat should you recommend? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p201-126.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\n#Q87-T03\nRespuesta\nRetrieve grounding data from: knowledge_base\nInference step to perform the retrieval: Generate query embeddings, and then run a vector similarity\nsearch.\nExplicación\nPor qué es correcta. El chatbot falla porque llama al LLM directamente, sin contexto. Solo conoce lo que\nvio en su entrenamiento, así que se equivoca cuando cambian las políticas y no puede responder sobre\ndocumentos internos. RAG resuelve esto recuperando datos de tu base antes de generar la respuesta,\nde modo que esta se apoya en datos actuales y propios (según Microsoft Learn).\nFuente: knowledge_base ya guarda las políticas de RRHH (title, content, category) y tiene una columna\nembedding. Es la fuente de conocimiento ya preparada para búsqueda semántica. Si se actualiza una\npolítica, se actualiza la fila y el chatbot responde con lo nuevo, sin reentrenar nada.\nRecuperación: el flujo RAG típico es fragmentar, generar embeddings, almacenarlos y, en la consulta,\nvectorizar la pregunta con el mismo modelo y buscar los fragmentos más similares. Después se añade ese\ncontexto al prompt y se genera la respuesta. Azure SQL admite el tipo vector y la función de distancia\nvectorial para esto. Que la pregunta use palabras distintas a las del documento no importa, porque se\ncompara el significado.\nPor qué las demás no son correctas.\nemployee_profiles y benefits_enrollment: son datos transaccionales de empleados, no documentos de\npolíticas. Sirven para enriquecer el contexto con un join, pero no para responder preguntas sobre políticas\nni sobre documentos por título o categoría.\nPDF exportados de las políticas: duplican el contenido que ya está en knowledge_base. Habría que extraer,\nfragmentar y vectorizar de nuevo, y se desincronizarían de la base.\nDatos de entrenamiento del LLM: son justo la causa del problema, porque están desactualizados y no\ncontienen documentos internos.\nBúsqueda por palabras clave: es una recuperación válida, pero no aprovecha la columna embedding y falla\ncon sinónimos o preguntas formuladas distinto. Los embeddings son lo que RAG usa aquí.\nLlamar primero al LLM y guardar la respuesta: invierte el patrón. La recuperación debe ir antes de la\ngeneración, no después.\nAjustar (fine-tune) el LLM con knowledge_base: es costoso y deja el conocimiento fijado en el modelo, así\nque se queda obsoleto en cuanto cambia una política. Además no es una etapa de recuperación.\nReferencias\nMicrosoft Learn:\nIntelligent applications and AI (Azure SQL Database): https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/\nai-artificial-intelligence-intelligent-applications?view=azuresql\nUse SQL database in AI applications (patrón RAG, Fabric): https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/database/sql/\nuse-case-ai-application\nRetrieval-augmented generation (RAG) in Azure AI Search: https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/\nretrieval-augmented-generation-overview\nSQL Server:\nVector search and vector indexes in the SQL Database Engine: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/sql-server/ai/\nvectors?view=sql-server-ver17\n🇬🇧 English version\nAnswer\nRetrieve grounding data from: knowledge_base\nInference step to perform the retrieval: Generate query embeddings, and then run a vector similarity search.\nExplanation\nWhy it's correct. The chatbot fails because it calls the LLM directly with no context. It only knows what it saw\nduring training, so it gives wrong answers when policies change and can't answer questions about internal\ndocuments. RAG fixes this by retrieving data from your own database before generating the answer, so the\nresponse rests on current, domain-specific facts (per Microsoft Learn).\nSource: knowledge_base already stores the HR policies (title, content, category) and has an embedding\ncolumn. It is the knowledge source already prepared for semantic search. When a policy changes, you\nupdate the row and the chatbot answers with the new content, with no retraining.\nRetrieval: the typical RAG flow is chunk, embed, store, then at query time embed the question with the\nsame model and find the most similar chunks. That context is added to the prompt and the answer is\ngenerated. Azure SQL supports the vector data type and a vector distance function for this. It doesn't matter\nif the question uses different words than the document, because meaning is compared.\nWhy the others are wrong.\nemployee_profiles and benefits_enrollment: these are transactional employee data, not policy documents.\nThey can enrich the context through a join, but they can't answer questions about policies or documents by\ntitle or category.\nPDF exports of the policies: they duplicate what's already in knowledge_base. You would have to extract,\nchunk and vectorize again, and they would drift out of sync with the database.\nThe LLM training data: this is the cause of the problem, since it's outdated and contains no internal\ndocuments.\nKeyword searches: a valid retrieval method, but it ignores the embedding column and misses synonyms and\ndifferently phrased questions. Embeddings are what RAG uses here.\nCall the LLM first, then store the response: this reverses the pattern. Retrieval must come before\ngeneration, not after.\nFine-tune the LLM with knowledge_base: it's costly and bakes knowledge into the model, so it goes stale as\nsoon as a policy changes. It is also not a retrieval step.\nReferences\nMicrosoft Learn:\nIntelligent applications and AI (Azure SQL Database): https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/\nai-artificial-intelligence-intelligent-applications?view=azuresql\nUse SQL database in AI applications (RAG pattern, Fabric): https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/database/sql/\nuse-case-ai-application\nRetrieval-augmented generation (RAG) in Azure AI Search: https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/\nretrieval-augmented-generation-overview\nSQL Server:\nVector search and vector indexes in the SQL Database Engine: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/sql-server/ai/\nvectors?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 88, "pages": [203, 205], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p204-127"], "type": "yesno", "template": "Generating an embedding by concatenating product_name, category, and description will support the customer requirements.\nIncluding price in the text used to generate embeddings is required.\nThe underlying base type of the embeddings will be float(32).", "pools": [["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"], ["Yes", "No"]], "correct": [0, 1, 0], "image": 127, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\nYour company has an ecommerce catalog in a Microsoft SQL Server 2025 database named SalesDB.\nSalesDB contains a table named products.\nproducts contains the following columns:\nproduct_id (int)\nproduct_name (nvarchar(200))\ndescription (nvarchar(max))\ncategory (nvarchar(50))\nbrand (nvarchar(50))\nprice (decimal)\nsku (nvarchar(40))\nThe description fields are updated daily by a content pipeline, and price can change multiple times per day.\nYou want customers to be able to submit natural language queries and apply structured filters for brand and\nprice.\nYou plan to store embeddings in a new VECTOR(1536) column and use VECTOR_SEARCH(...\nMETRIC='cosine' ...).\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p204-128.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ88 T03\nCorrect Answer\nGenerar el embedding concatenando product_name, category y description cubre los requisitos --> Yes\nIncluir price en el texto usado para generar los embeddings es obligatorio --> No\nEl tipo base de los embeddings será float(32) --> Yes\nExplanation\nStatement 1: Yes. La búsqueda en lenguaje natural necesita que el vector capture el significado del\nproducto, y ese significado está en las columnas de texto descriptivo. La documentación de vectores de\nSQL contempla dos opciones cuando hay varias columnas: un embedding por columna, o concatenarlas en\nuna sola cadena y generar un único embedding. brand no hace falta en el texto porque el requisito lo pide\ncomo filtro estructurado, no como criterio semántico.\nStatement 2: No. El precio se resuelve con un predicado relacional en el WHERE, que es exacto y puede\nusar un índice B-tree. Meterlo en el embedding es contraproducente por dos motivos:\nUn embedding no representa bien comparaciones numéricas ni rangos como \"menos de 50\".\nEl precio cambia varias veces al día, así que habría que regenerar el vector en cada cambio, con el\ncoste de llamadas al modelo que eso supone.\nLa documentación describe justo este patrón: embeddings y datos relacionales viven en la misma base de\ndatos y se combinan similitud vectorial y filtros WHERE en la misma consulta.\nStatement 3: Yes. VECTOR(1536) no especifica tipo base, y la documentación indica que por defecto es\nfloat32 (precisión simple, 4 bytes por elemento). float16 solo se usa si se declara explícitamente como\nVECTOR(1536, float16), y en SQL Server 2025 requiere además PREVIEW_FEATURES. Las 1.536\ndimensiones caben en el máximo de 1.998 de float32.\n-- Columna de embeddings: base type float32 por defecto\nALTER TABLE dbo.products ADD embedding VECTOR(1536) NULL;\n-- Embedding solo con el texto semántico (sin price ni brand)\nUPDATE dbo.products\nSET embedding = AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS(\nCONCAT_WS(N' | ', product_name, category, description)\nUSE MODEL MyEmbeddingModel);\n-- Consulta: similitud semántica + filtros estructurados\nDECLARE @qv VECTOR(1536) =\nAI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS(N'zapatillas ligeras para correr por montaña' USE MODEL\nMyEmbeddingModel);\nSELECT TOP (10) p.product_id, p.product_name, p.brand, p.price, r.distance\nFROM VECTOR_SEARCH(\nTABLE = dbo.products AS p,\nCOLUMN = embedding,\nSIMILAR_TO = @qv,\nMETRIC = 'cosine',\nTOP_N = 100 -- sobremuestreo: el filtro se aplica después\n) AS r\nWHERE p.brand = N'Contoso' AND p.price <= 120\nORDER BY r.distance;\nNotas críticas\nRedacción de float(32). La pregunta se refiere al tipo base float32 del vector. Tomado literalmente\ncomo tipo escalar de T-SQL, float(32) sería un valor de 8 bytes (doble precisión, porque n está entre 25\ny 53). Es un defecto de redacción; la respuesta defendible sigue siendo Yes.\nPost-filtering en SQL Server 2025. Según la documentación actual, la versión más reciente del índice\nvectorial (filtrado iterativo, DML completo, sintaxis TOP (N) WITH APPROXIMATE) está en Azure SQL\nDatabase, SQL database in Fabric y Managed Instance con Always-up-to-date. En SQL Server 2025 el\níndice y VECTOR_SEARCH siguen en preview, y con la versión anterior el WHERE se aplica después\nde recuperar los TOP_N vecinos. Por eso el ejemplo sobremuestrea.\nTabla de solo lectura. Con esa versión anterior, una tabla con índice vectorial queda en solo lectura\nsalvo que se active ALLOW_STALE_VECTOR_INDEX. Como price cambia varias veces al día, en\nproducción conviene separar los embeddings en una tabla propia (product_embeddings) y unirla por\nproduct_id.\nRegeneración diaria. Cuando el pipeline actualiza description, hay que regenerar el embedding de\nesas filas; un vector desactualizado devuelve resultados semánticos incorrectos sin dar error.\nReferences\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/data-types/vector-data-type?view=sql-server-ver17&tabs=csharp\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/data-types/vector-data-type-half-precision-float?view=sql-server-\nver17\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-search-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/statements/create-vector-index-transact-sql?view=sql-server-\nver17\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/sql-server/ai/vectors-frequently-asked-questions?view=sql-server-\nver17"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 89, "pages": [205, 206], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You need to design a generative Al solution that uses a Microsoft SQL Server 2025 database named DB1\nas a data source. The solution must generate responses that meet the following requirements:\nAre grounded in the latest transactional and reference data stored in DB1\nDo NOT require retraining or fine-tuning the language model when the data changes\nCan include citations or references to the source data used in the response\nWhich scenario is the best use case for implementing a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pattern?\nMore than one answer choice may achieve the goal. Select the BEST answer."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "summarizing free-form user input text"}, {"id": "B", "text": "training a custom language model on historical database data"}, {"id": "C", "text": "answering user questions based on company-specific knowledge"}, {"id": "D", "text": "generating marketing slogans based on user sentiment analysis"}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ89 / T03\nCORRECT ANSWER:\nAnswering user questions based on company-specific knowledge\nEXPLANATION:\n• answering user questions based on company-specific knowledge es correcta: RAG recupera información\nrelevante de DB1 y la proporciona al\nmodelo como contexto para responder preguntas sobre datos específicos de la\nempresa. Permite utilizar datos actualizados sin reentrenar ni ajustar el modelo y\npuede incluir referencias a las fuentes utilizadas.\n• summarizing free-form user input text es incorrecta: Resumir texto proporcionado por el usuario no\nrequiere recuperar\nconocimiento empresarial desde DB1.\n• training a custom language model on historical database data es incorrecta: Entrenar un modelo con datos\nhistóricos no cumple el requisito de\nutilizar información actualizada sin reentrenamiento.\n• generating marketing slogans based on user sentiment analysis es incorrecta: Generar eslóganes a partir\ndel sentimiento del usuario no es el\ncaso que mejor encaja con los requisitos de conocimiento empresarial actualizado y\nreferencias.\nREFERENCES:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/dotnet/ai/conceptual/rag\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/azure/databricks/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 90, "pages": [206, 209], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p207-129"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "Semantic query operator/function: {{0}}\nKeyword retrieval operator/function: {{1}}\nFinal ranking expression: {{2}}", "pools": [["VECTOR_DISTANCE and order by distance ascending", "VECTOR_SEARCH with METRIC cosine and TOP_N", "VECTORPROPERTY to calculate the similarity between two vectors"], ["CONTAINSTABLE on description and return ranked matches", "FREETEXTTABLE on description for keyword scoring", "JSON_VALUE extraction from description for keyword scoring"], ["order by (distance * 0.6) + ((1.0 - RANK/1000.0) * 0.4)", "order by (distance * 0.6) + (RANK * 0.4)", "order by (distance + RANK), and then apply TOP 20"]], "correct": [0, 1, 0], "image": 129, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "HOTSPOT\nYou have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named stores. stores contains a column named\ndescription and a vector column named embedding.\nYou need to implement a hybrid search query that meets the following requirements:\n- Uses full-text search on description for the keyword portion\n- Returns the top 20 results based on a combined score that uses a weighted formula of 60% vector\ndistance and 40% full-text rank\nHow should you configure the query components?\nTo answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p208-130.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ90 / T03\nCorrect Answer:\nSemantic query operator/function:\nVECTOR_DISTANCE and order by distance ascending\nKeyword retrieval operator/function:\nFREETEXTTABLE on description for keyword scoring\nFinal ranking expression:\norder by (distance * 0.6) + ((1.0 - RANK/1000.0) * 0.4)\n**Explanation:**\nSemantic query operator/function: En Azure SQL Database, la función nativa `VECTOR_DISTANCE` se\nutiliza para calcular la distancia geométrica entre dos vectores. Debido a que una distancia menor indica\nuna mayor similitud, la consulta debe ordenar los resultados de forma ascendente para priorizar las\nmejores coincidencias.\nKeyword retrieval operator/function: `FREETEXTTABLE` se utiliza para búsquedas de texto completo\n(Full-Text Search). A diferencia de otras funciones que buscan coincidencias exactas, `FREETEXTTABLE`\nbusca palabras con el mismo significado y devuelve una columna `RANK` que puntúa la relevancia de cada\ncoincidencia, lo cual es esencial para calcular el peso de la palabra clave en una búsqueda híbrida.\nFinal ranking expression: Para combinar correctamente ambas métricas en una búsqueda híbrida, deben\ncompartir la misma escala direccional. Mientras que en `VECTOR_DISTANCE` un valor cercano a `0` es\nmejor, el `RANK` de texto completo funciona a la inversa, otorgando valores más altos (hasta 1000) a las\nmejores coincidencias. Para unificarlos, la puntuación de texto completo se normaliza y se invierte\nmediante `(1.0 - RANK/1000.0)`. Después, se aplican los porcentajes requeridos multiplicando la distancia\nvectorial por `0.6` (60%) y el rango de texto normalizado por `0.4` (40%).\n**References:**\nInformación general sobre vectores: https://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/sql-server/ai/vectors\nDocumentación técnica de VECTOR_DISTANCE: https://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/t-sql/functions/\nvector-distance-transact-sql\nDocumentación técnica de FREETEXTTABLE: https://learn.microsoft.com/es-es/sql/relational-databases/\nsystem-functions/freetexttable-transact-sql"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 91, "pages": [209, 213], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p211-131", "p212-132"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You need to use the UsefulPrompts table as defined in the AI requirements.\nWhich stored procedure should you use?\nCase Study Title (Case Study):\nCase Study\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\nAzure Environment\nFabrikam has a single Azure subscription in the East US 2 Azure region. The subscription contains an\nAzure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains the following tables:\nPatients\nEmployees\nProcedures\nTransactions\nUsefulPrompts\nProcedureDocuments\nYou store a column master key as a secret in Azure Key Vault.\nYou have an on-premises application named TransactionProcessing that uses a hard-coded username and\npassword in a connection string to access DB1.\nProblem Statements\nUsers report that after executing a long-running stored procedure named sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient,\nupdates to the underlying data are sometimes inconsistent.\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nFabrikam plans to manage all changes to Azure SQL Database objects by using source control in GitHub.\nEvery pull request submitted to production will be validated before it can be merged. Deployments must use\nthe Release configuration.\nSecurity Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the following security requirements:\nThe TransactionProcessing application must use a passwordless connection to DB1.\nThe Employees table contains two columns named TaxID and Salary that must be encrypted at rest.\nAuditors must have a tamper-evident history of transactions with cryptographic proof of changes to the\nemployee data.\nDatabase Performance Requirements\nRecords accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must NOT be changed by other transactions\nwhile the stored procedure runs.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nFabrikam identifies the following AI-related requirements:\nQueries to the ProcedureDocuments table must use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\nUsers must be able to query the data in DB1 by using prompts in Copilot in Microsoft Fabric.\nThe UsefulPrompts table will store prompts that doctors can use to help diagnose patient illness by\nconnecting to an Azure OpenAI endpoint.\nDevelopment Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the following development requirements:\nProvide the functionality to retrieve all the transactions of a given patient between two dates, showing a\nrunning total.\nExpose a Data API builder (DAB) configuration file to enable Azure services to perform the following\noperations over a REST API:\no Read data from the procedures table without authentication.\no Read and insert data into the Transactions table once authenticated.\no Execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure.\nProvide the functionality to retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical procedures during\nthe last 30 days.\nInformation for each medical procedure will be stored in a table. The table will be used with a large language\nmodel (LLM) for user querying and will have the following structure."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE dbo.ProcedureDocuments\n(\n DocumentId INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n SourceId NVARCHAR(200) NULL,\n Content NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,\n CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\n)"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "You create a DAB configuration file that meets the development requirements for DB1 and includes the\nfollowing entities."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n \"entities\": {\n  \"Procedures\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Procedures\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"]}]\n  },\n  \"Transactions\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Transactions\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"read\", \"create\"]}]\n  },\n  \"UpdateProcedurePatient\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\",\n   \"rest\": {\"enabled\": true, \"method\": \"post\", \"path\": \"/procedurepatient\"},\n   \"graphql\": false,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"execute\"]}]\n  }\n }\n}"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "sp_addendpoint"}, {"id": "B", "text": "xp_cmdshell"}, {"id": "C", "text": "sp_OACreate"}, {"id": "D", "text": "sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ91 T03\nCORRECTA\nsp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint\nANÁLISIS\nConcepto principal: invocación de un endpoint REST externo (Azure OpenAI) desde Azure SQL Database\npara cumplir el requisito de AI relacionado con la tabla UsefulPrompts.\nServicio: Azure SQL Database + Azure OpenAI.\nEscenario: la tabla UsefulPrompts debe almacenar prompts que se conectan a un endpoint de Azure\nOpenAI para ayudar en el diagnóstico.\nVERIFICACIÓN (MCP)\nDocumentación oficial de Microsoft Learn consultada sobre sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint y su uso con\nAzure OpenAI.\nJUSTIFICACIÓN\nMicrosoft Learn documenta que sp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint permite invocar endpoints HTTPS\nREST directamente desde T-SQL en Azure SQL Database. Azure OpenAI está explícitamente en la lista de\nendpoints permitidos (*.openai.azure.com). Este procedimiento es el mecanismo oficial para conectar la\nlógica de UsefulPrompts con el endpoint de Azure OpenAI.\nIncorrectas\nsp_addendpoint → Se usa para crear endpoints de Service Broker, no para llamadas REST externas.\nxp_cmdshell → Ejecuta comandos del sistema operativo; no está diseñado ni permitido para invocar\nservicios REST de forma segura.\nsp_OACreate → Forma parte de OLE Automation (obsoleto y restringido); no es el método soportado para\nintegraciones modernas con Azure OpenAI.\nREFERENCIA\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sp-invoke-external-rest-\nendpoint-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/azure-sql/database/ai-artificial-intelligence-intelligent-applications"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 92, "pages": [213, 217], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p215-133", "p216-134"], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You implement ProcedureDocuments to support the planned changes.\nWhen users consume data through the Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pattern, they experience\ndata retrieval delays.\nYou need to improve the data retrieval performance and reduce the number of tokens per retrieval.\nWhat should you implement?\nCase Study Title (Case Study):\nCase Study\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would\nlike to complete each case. However, there may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You\nmust manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions included on this exam in the\ntime provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in\nthe case study. Case studies might contain exhibits and other resources that provide more information\nabout the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is independent of the other questions\nin this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers\nand to make changes before you move to the next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you\ncannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to\nexplore the content of the case study before you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays\ninformation such as business requirements, existing environment and problem statements. If the case study\nhas an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the\nquestion.\nExisting Environment\nAzure Environment\nFabrikam has a single Azure subscription in the East US 2 Azure region. The subscription contains an\nAzure SQL database named DB1. DB1 contains the following tables:\n- Patients\n- Employees\n- Procedures\n- Transactions\n- UsefulPrompts\n- ProcedureDocuments\nYou store a column master key as a secret in Azure Key Vault.\nYou have an on-premises application named TransactionProcessing that uses a hard-coded username and\npassword in a connection string to access DB1.\nProblem Statements\nUsers report that after executing a long-running stored procedure named sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient,\nupdates to the underlying data are sometimes inconsistent.\nRequirements\nPlanned Changes\nFabrikam plans to manage all changes to Azure SQL Database objects by using source control in GitHub.\nEvery pull request submitted to production will be validated before it can be merged. Deployments must use\nthe Release configuration.\nSecurity Requirements\nFabrikam identifies the following security requirements:\n- The TransactionProcessing application must use a passwordless connection to DB1.\n- The Employees table contains two columns named TaxID and Salary that must be encrypted at rest.\n- Auditors must have a tamper-evident history of transactions with cryptographic proof of changes to the\nemployee data.\nDatabase Performance Requirements\nRecords accessed by using sp_UpdateProcedureForPatient must NOT be changed by other transactions\nwhile the stored procedure runs.\nAI Search, Embeddings, and Vector Indexing\nFabrikam identifies the following AI-related requirements:\n- Queries to the ProcedureDocuments table must use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n- Users must be able to query the data in DB1 by using prompts in Copilot in Microsoft Fabric.\n- The UsefulPrompts table will store prompts that doctors can use to help diagnose patient illness by\nconnecting to an Azure OpenAI endpoint.\nDevelopment Requirements\n- Fabrikam identifies the following development requirements:\n- Provide the functionality to retrieve all the transactions of a given patient between two dates, showing a\nrunning total.\n- Expose a Data API builder (DAB) configuration file to enable Azure services to perform the following\noperations over a REST API:\n-- Read data from the procedures table without authentication.\n-- Read and insert data into the Transactions table once authenticated.\n- Execute the sp_UpdateProcedurePatient stored procedure.\n- Provide the functionality to retrieve a list of the names of patients who underwent medical procedures\nduring the last 30 days.\n- Information for each medical procedure will be stored in a table. The table will be used with a large\nlanguage model (LLM) for user querying and will have the following structure."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "CREATE TABLE dbo.ProcedureDocuments\n(\n DocumentId INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n SourceId NVARCHAR(200) NULL,\n Content NVARCHAR(MAX) NOT NULL,\n Embedding VECTOR(1536) NOT NULL,\n CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME()\n);"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "DAB\nYou create a DAB configuration file that meets the development requirements for DB1 and includes the\nfollowing entities."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "{\n \"entities\": {\n  \"Procedures\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Procedures\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"]}]\n  },\n  \"Transactions\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.Transactions\", \"rest\": true, \"graphql\": true,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"read\", \"create\"]}]\n  },\n  \"UpdateProcedurePatient\": {\n   \"source\": \"dbo.sp_UpdateProcedurePatient\",\n   \"rest\": {\"enabled\": true, \"method\": \"post\", \"path\": \"/procedurepatient\"},\n   \"graphql\": false,\n   \"permissions\": [{\"role\": \"authenticated\", \"actions\": [\"execute\"]}]\n  }\n }\n}"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "chunking"}, {"id": "B", "text": "embeddings"}, {"id": "C", "text": "a small language model (SLM)"}, {"id": "D", "text": "JSON content"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ92 T03\nCORRECT ANSWER:\nchunking\nEXPLANATION:\nLa documentación oficial de Microsoft establece claramente que el chunking (particionar documentos\ngrandes en trozos más pequeños y semánticamente coherentes) es la técnica fundamental para:\nMantener el texto por debajo de los límites de tokens de los modelos de embedding y de chat completion\n(ej. text-embedding-3-small tiene un límite de 8.191 tokens).\nReducir el número de tokens enviados al LLM en cada recuperación RAG.\nMejorar la precisión y el rendimiento de la búsqueda vectorial, porque se recuperan fragmentos más\nrelevantes y pequeños en lugar de documentos completos o demasiado grandes.\nEvitar truncamiento y pérdida de información.\nEn el caso de estudio, la tabla ProcedureDocuments ya tiene la columna Embedding VECTOR(1536). Por\ntanto, los embeddings ya están implementados. El problema descrito (retrasos + alto consumo de tokens)\nse resuelve aplicando chunking al contenido (Content) antes de generar/almacenar los embeddings.\nPor qué las demás opciones son incorrectas:\nembeddings\nYa están presentes en el esquema (Embedding VECTOR(1536)). Añadir o mejorar embeddings no\nresuelve por sí solo el problema de tokens excesivos ni de retrasos cuando se recuperan documentos\ngrandes.\na small language model (SLM)\nUn modelo de lenguaje más pequeño puede reducir coste/latencia de generación, pero no aborda el\nproblema raíz de recuperación (cantidad de tokens enviados al modelo ni la granularidad de los resultados\ndel vector search).\nJSON content\nNo es una técnica de optimización de RAG ni de reducción de tokens. No tiene relación con el problema\ndescrito.\nREFERENCIA\nFuentes oficiales exactas de Microsoft Learn:\nChunk large documents for RAG and vector search in Azure AI Search\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/vector-search-how-to-chunk-documents\n(Explica explícitamente que el chunking reduce tokens, evita límites de modelos y mejora el rendimiento de\nRAG).\nRetrieval-augmented generation (RAG) in Azure AI Search – Content preparation for RAG\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/retrieval-augmented-generation-overview\nGuía de arquitectura RAG (fase de chunking)\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/rag/rag-chunking-phase"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 93, "pages": [217, 218], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "Your team is developing an Azure SQL database solution from a locally cloned GitHub repository by using\nMicrosoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot Chat.\nYou need to ensure that GitHub Copilot Chat uses the team’s coding standards when generating Transact-\nSQL code in Visual Studio Code."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": ".github/copilot-instructions.md"}, {"id": "B", "text": ".vscode/settings.json"}, {"id": "C", "text": "%APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\settings.json"}, {"id": "D", "text": "%APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\copilot-instructions.md"}], "correct": ["A"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "A\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ93 T03\n1. Respuesta Correcta:\ngithub/copilot-instructions.md\n2. Explicación Técnica Detallada:\nEl archivo .github/copilot-instructions.md (ubicado en la raíz del repositorio, dentro de la carpeta .github) es\nel mecanismo oficial de custom instructions a nivel de repositorio para GitHub Copilot Chat.\nCuando el equipo trabaja en un repositorio clonado localmente con Visual Studio Code + GitHub Copilot\nChat, este archivo se incluye automáticamente en el contexto de todas las solicitudes de chat (incluyendo\ngeneración de código Transact-SQL).\nEsto permite definir de forma centralizada y versionada (en Git) los estándares de codificación del equipo:\nconvenciones de nomenclatura T-SQL, uso de tipos de datos, patrones de procedimientos almacenados,\nformato de scripts, comentarios obligatorios, compatibilidad Azure SQL / SQL Server, etc.\nComo el archivo forma parte del repositorio, todos los miembros del equipo reciben las mismas\ninstrucciones de forma automática, sin necesidad de configurar nada a nivel de usuario o máquina\nindividual. Es la solución diseñada precisamente para escenarios de desarrollo colaborativo de soluciones\nAzure SQL Database.\n3. Análisis de Opciones Incorrectas:\n.vscode/settings.json\nArchivo de configuración de workspace de VS Code. Se puede usar para apuntar a archivos de\ninstrucciones adicionales mediante la setting github.copilot.chat.codeGeneration.instructions, pero no es el\nmecanismo principal ni el recomendado para definir los estándares del equipo de forma automática y\ncompartida. No sustituye a .github/copilot-instructions.md.\n%APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\settings.json\nSettings de usuario de VS Code (ámbito máquina/usuario). Cualquier instrucción definida aquí es personal\ny no se comparte con el equipo a través del repositorio. No cumple el requisito de aplicar los estándares del\nequipo de forma consistente.\n%APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\copilot-instructions.md\nRuta de usuario (no existe como convención oficial de repositorio). Las instrucciones personales se\ngestionan de otra forma y no se propagan al resto del equipo ni se versionan en el repositorio GitHub\nclonado.\n4. Referencias Oficiales de Microsoft & SQL Server:\nCustomize GitHub Copilot Responses using Instruction Files (Microsoft Learn – módulo oficial):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/configure-customize-github-copilot-visual-studio-code/3-\ncustomize-copilot-responses-instruction-files\nCustomize GitHub Copilot Chat Responses (Microsoft Learn):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/introduction-vibe-coding/6-customize-github-copilot-\nresponses\nQuickstart: Custom Instructions for GitHub Copilot and T-SQL (MSSQL Extension for Visual Studio Code):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/tools/visual-studio-code-extensions/github-copilot/custom-instructions?\nview=sql-server-ver17\nAdding repository custom instructions for GitHub Copilot (GitHub Docs – documentación oficial referenciada\npor Microsoft):\nhttps://docs.github.com/en/copilot/customizing-copilot/adding-custom-instructions-for-github-copilot\nUse custom instructions in VS Code (documentación VS Code):\nhttps://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/copilot-customization"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 94, "pages": [218, 220], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a Microsoft SQL Server 2025 database that contains a table named products. products contains\ntwo columns named description and embedding. embedding is generated from description.\nYou have an application named App1. App1 has a feature that uses the following query.\nVECTOR_DISTANCE('cosine', @query_vector, embedding)\nUsers report that the feature is slow during peak usage times.\nYou discover that during peak usage times, the semantic search latency increases and CPU utilization\nspikes.\nYou need to reduce latency and CPU utilization related to the App1 feature.\nWhat should you do?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Create a vector index on products.embedding and change the query to use VECTOR_NORMALIZE and\nNORM_TYPE = 'norm1'."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Use CONTAINSTABLE against description only and remove the vector search."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Create a vector index on products.embedding and change the query to use VECTOR_SEARCH and\nMETRIC = 'cosine'."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Add a nonclustered index on products.embedding."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Change embedding to VARBINARY(8000)."}], "correct": ["C"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "C\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ94 T03\n1. Correct Answer:\nCreate a vector index on products.embedding and change the query to use VECTOR_SEARCH and\nMETRIC = 'cosine'.\n2. Technical Explanation:\nLa consulta actual usa VECTOR_DISTANCE('cosine', @query_vector, embedding), que realiza una\nbúsqueda exacta de vecinos más cercanos (exact kNN). Esto implica escanear todas las filas de la tabla,\ncalcular la distancia coseno para cada vector y ordenar los resultados. En tablas grandes y durante picos\nde uso, genera alta latencia y picos de CPU.\nEn SQL Server 2025 la solución recomendada es:\nCrear un vector index (algoritmo DiskANN) sobre la columna embedding especificando la misma métrica\n(METRIC = 'cosine').\nCambiar la consulta para usar la función VECTOR_SEARCH con METRIC = 'cosine'.\nVECTOR_SEARCH realiza una búsqueda aproximada de vecinos más cercanos (ANN). El índice vectorial\npermite encontrar los vectores más similares de forma muy eficiente, reduciendo drásticamente el uso de\nCPU y la latencia, manteniendo una alta precisión (recall normalmente > 0.95).\n3. Analysis of Incorrect Options:\nCreate a vector index on products.embedding and change the query to use VECTOR_NORMALIZE and\nNORM_TYPE = 'norm1'.\nVECTOR_NORMALIZE normaliza vectores, pero no es la función de búsqueda. Además, no se combina de\nesa forma con el índice vectorial. La función correcta para aprovechar el índice es VECTOR_SEARCH.\nUse CONTAINSTABLE against description only and remove the vector search.\nCONTAINSTABLE es full-text search tradicional (basado en texto). Elimina la capacidad de búsqueda\nsemántica por embeddings, que es el requisito de la aplicación.\nAdd a nonclustered index on products.embedding.\nNo se pueden crear índices B-tree (nonclustered tradicionales) sobre columnas de tipo vector. Solo se\nadmiten vector indexes específicos (CREATE VECTOR INDEX).\nChange embedding to VARBINARY(8000).\nCambiar el tipo de dato a VARBINARY elimina el soporte nativo de vectores y las funciones\nVECTOR_DISTANCE / VECTOR_SEARCH. No soluciona el problema de rendimiento.\n4. Official Microsoft References:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-search-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-vector-index-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/sql-server/ai/vectors\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/build-ai-solutions-sql-server/3-implement-vector"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 95, "pages": [220, 225], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p221-135"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE dbo.SearchProducts\n @query_text NVARCHAR(4000),\n @keywords NVARCHAR(4000)\nAS\nBEGIN\n SET NOCOUNT ON;\n DECLARE @qv VECTOR(1536) =\n {{0}} (@query_text USE MODEL Ada2Embeddings);\n ;WITH ann AS\n (\n  SELECT t.product_id, t.product_name, s.distance\n  FROM {{1}} (\n   TABLE = dbo.Products AS t,\n   COLUMN = embedding,\n   SIMILAR_TO = @qv,\n   TOP_N = 20,\n   METRIC = 'cosine'\n  ) AS s\n )\n SELECT TOP (20) t.product_id, t.product_name, ann.distance, fts.RANK AS text_rank\n FROM ann\n JOIN dbo.Products AS t ON t.product_id = ann.product_id\n JOIN {{2}} (dbo.Products, description, @keywords) AS fts\n ON t.product_id = fts.[KEY]\n ORDER BY (ann.distance * 0.6) + ((1.0 - fts.RANK/1000.0) * 0.4);\nEND;", "pools": [["AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS", "SEMANTICKEYPHRASETABLE", "VECTOR_NORMALIZE", "VECTOR_SEARCH"], ["CONTAINSTABLE", "SEMANTICSIMILARITYTABLE", "VECTOR_DISTANCE", "VECTOR_SEARCH"], ["CONTAINSTABLE", "FREETEXTTABLE", "SEMANTICKEYPHRASETABLE", "SEMANTICSIMILARITYDETAILSTABLE"]], "correct": [0, 3, 0], "image": 135, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a SQL database in Microsoft Fabric that contains a table named dbo.Products. dbo.Products\ncontains product catalog data.\nYou need to create a stored procedure that performs hybrid search. The solution must meet the following\nrequirements:\nUse approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) to retrieve the top 20 candidate products.\nRe-rank only the candidates that also match a full-text query.\nGenerate the query embedding.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer\narea.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p223-136.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ95 T03\nCorrect Answer:\nAI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS\nFROM VECTOR_SEARCH (\nJOIN CONTAINSTABLE (dbo.Products, description, @keywords) AS fts\nExplanation:\nAI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS: la clave es \"Generate the query embedding\" y la sintaxis (@query_text\nUSE MODEL Ada2Embeddings). Esa es la forma de la función, y devuelve un VECTOR. Microsoft Learn\nusa este mismo patrón: DECLARE @qv VECTOR(1536) = AI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS(... USE\nMODEL Ada2Embeddings).\nVECTOR_SEARCH: la clave es \"approximate nearest neighbor (ANN)\" y los parámetros TABLE, COLUMN,\nSIMILAR_TO, METRIC y TOP_N. VECTOR_SEARCH es la función de ANN, y TOP_N = 20 da los 20\ncandidatos.\nCONTAINSTABLE: la clave es la tabla full-text con RANK y [KEY] para hacer el JOIN con los candidatos de\nANN, y el parámetro se llama @keywords. CONTAINSTABLE hace coincidencia de palabras y frases\n(keyword matching) y devuelve KEY y RANK. Con el JOIN (inner), solo sobreviven los candidatos que\ntambién cumplen el full-text.\nWhy not the others:\nAI_GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS (1er desplegable):\nSEMANTICKEYPHRASETABLE extrae frases clave de documentos.\nVECTOR_NORMALIZE normaliza un vector existente, no genera embeddings. VECTOR_SEARCH busca,\nno genera.\nVECTOR_SEARCH (2º desplegable):\nCONTAINSTABLE es full-text.\nSEMANTICSIMILARITYTABLE es semantic search sobre documentos.\nVECTOR_DISTANCE calcula la distancia entre dos vectores, no es una función de tabla con TABLE/\nCOLUMN/SIMILAR_TO.\nCONTAINSTABLE (3er desplegable):\nSEMANTICKEYPHRASETABLE y SEMANTICSIMILARITYDETAILSTABLE son de Semantic Search (otra\ncaracterística), no de búsqueda por palabras clave.\nFREETEXTTABLE es el único distractor sintácticamente válido: devuelve también KEY y RANK, pero hace\ncoincidencia por significado (stemming, sinónimos), no por palabras exactas. La documentación no\ndistingue más allá de eso, así que la elección de CONTAINSTABLE se apoya en el nombre @keywords y\nen que la parte semántica ya la cubre el vector search. Es también la respuesta que suele aparecer en los\nbancos de práctica.\nNota Fabric/versiones: TOP_N en VECTOR_SEARCH es la sintaxis de índices vectoriales de versión\nanterior, ahora deprecated. La versión más reciente usa SELECT TOP (N) WITH APPROXIMATE sin\nTOP_N. La pregunta usa TOP_N, así que responde según el código mostrado.\nReference:\nVECTOR_SEARCH (Transact-SQL): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/vector-search-transact-\nsql?view=sql-server-ver17\nVector search and vector indexes in the SQL Database Engine: https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/sql-server/ai/\nvectors?view=sql-server-ver17\nQuery with Full-Text Search (CONTAINSTABLE vs FREETEXTTABLE): https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/\nrelational-databases/search/query-with-full-text-search?view=sql-server-ver17"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 96, "pages": [225, 225], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have a GitHub Enterprise subscription.\nYour team is developing an Azure SQL dataset solution from a locally cloned GitHub repository by using\nMicrosoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot Chat.\nA mix of GitHub Copilot instructions is configured at different levels, including organization-wide, repository-\nwide, agent-specific, and personal.\nWhich instructions will take precedence over the others?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "repository-wide"}, {"id": "B", "text": "personal"}, {"id": "C", "text": "organization-wide"}, {"id": "D", "text": "agent-specific"}], "correct": ["B"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "B\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ96 / T03\nLa respuesta correcta es \"personal\".\nSegún la documentación oficial de GitHub, el orden de precedencia de las instrucciones personalizadas de\nGitHub Copilot (de mayor a menor prioridad) es el siguiente:\nPersonal instructions (las de mayor prioridad)\nRepository custom instructions:\nPath-specific (archivos .github/instructions/**/*.instructions.md)\nRepository-wide (archivo .github/copilot-instructions.md)\nAgent instructions (por ejemplo, AGENTS.md)\nOrganization custom instructions (las de menor prioridad)\nPor tanto, las instrucciones personales tienen precedencia sobre todas las demás (repository-wide,\norganization-wide y agent-specific). Todas las instrucciones relevantes se envían a Copilot, pero en caso de\nconflicto, las personales prevalecen.\nPor qué las otras opciones son incorrectas:\nrepository-wide: Tienen prioridad media. Están por debajo de las personales y de las path-specific, pero por\nencima de las agent-specific y de las organization-wide.\norganization-wide: Son las de menor prioridad. Se aplican a todos los miembros de la organización, pero se\nsobrescriben fácilmente por las de repositorio o personales.\nagent-specific: Forman parte del grupo de repository custom instructions y están en el nivel más bajo de\nese grupo (después de path-specific y repository-wide). No tienen la máxima prioridad.\nREFERENCIAS:\nsys.dm_exec_requests: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-dynamic-\nmanagement-objects/sys-dm-exec-requests-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nsys.dm_exec_sessions: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-dynamic-\nmanagement-objects/sys-dm-exec-sessions-transact-sql?view=sql-server-ver17\nTroubleshoot Query Blocking in Fabric Data Warehouse: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-\nwarehouse/troubleshoot-query-blocking"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 97, "pages": [225, 228], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p226-137"], "type": "drag", "template": "{\n \"search\": \"ocean view\",\n \"queryType\": {{0}},\n \"semanticConfiguration\": {{1}},\n \"captions\": {{2}},\n \"top\": 10,\n \"hybridSearch\": {\"maxTextRecallSize\": 50},\n \"vectorQueries\": [{\n  \"kind\": \"vector\",\n  \"vector\": [/* embedding array */],\n  \"fields\": \"DescriptionVector\",\n  \"k\": 50\n }]\n}", "pools": [["\"default\"", "\"extractive\"", "\"full\"", "\"generative\"", "\"hotels\"", "\"hybrid\"", "\"none\"", "\"semantic\"", "\"simple\""], ["\"default\"", "\"extractive\"", "\"full\"", "\"generative\"", "\"hotels\"", "\"hybrid\"", "\"none\"", "\"semantic\"", "\"simple\""], ["\"default\"", "\"extractive\"", "\"full\"", "\"generative\"", "\"hotels\"", "\"hybrid\"", "\"none\"", "\"semantic\"", "\"simple\""]], "correct": [7, 0, 1], "image": 137, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure AI Search service and an index named hotels that includes a vector field named\nDescriptionVector.\nYou query hotels by using the Search Documents REST API.\nYou need to implement a hybrid search query that uses DescriptionVector and includes captions.\nHow should you complete the REST request body? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct\ntargets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar\nbetween panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p227-138.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ97 T03\nCorrect Answers:\n\"queryType\": \"semantic\"\n\"semanticConfiguration\": \"default\"\n\"captions\": \"extractive\"\nExplanation:\n\"queryType\": \"semantic\"\nThe request has both search (\"ocean view\") and vectorQueries, so it is already a hybrid query. The text and\nvector results are merged with RRF.\nCaptions are a semantic ranker feature, so the semantic ranker has to run over the merged results.\nqueryType must be semantic for that.\n\"simple\" is the default BM25 text-query mode and cannot return captions. \"full\" is Lucene syntax, which\nsemantic ranking does not support through queryType.\n\"hybrid\", \"extractive\", \"generative\", \"none\" and \"default\" are not valid queryType values. A query is hybrid\nbecause it combines search with vectorQueries, not because of this property.\n\"semanticConfiguration\": \"default\"\nWhen queryType is semantic, semanticConfiguration is required. It must name a semantic configuration\ndefined in the index.\n\"default\" is the only value in the list that can be a configuration name.\n\"hotels\" is the name of the index, not of a semantic configuration. It is a distractor.\nThe other values are keywords for other parameters, not configuration names.\n\"captions\": \"extractive\"\nThe valid values for captions are extractive and none. The default is none, so you must set extractive to get\ncaptions.\nThe semantic ranker extracts verbatim passages from the top-ranked documents, and they appear in\n@search.captions.\n\"none\" disables captions. \"generative\" does not exist in this API, because captions and answers are\nextracted and never composed by an LLM.\n\"semantic\", \"hybrid\", \"simple\", \"full\" and \"default\" are not valid caption values.\nThe rest of the body already follows best practice. k: 50 gives the semantic ranker its maximum of 50 inputs,\nand maxTextRecallSize controls how many text results go into the merge.\nResources:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/semantic-how-to-query-request#set-up-the-query\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/hybrid-search-how-to-query#examples-of-hybrid-queries\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/azure/search/search-get-started-semantic#understand-the-code\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/rest/api/searchservice/documents/search-post"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 98, "pages": [228, 229], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains tables named dbo.Tickets and dbo.TicketNotes. dbo.\nTickets cotains support tickets and dbo.TicketNotes contains ticket notes.\nA retrieval query returns the top five relevant ticket notes for a user question.\nYou plan to implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pattern that meets the following\nrequirements:\nFormats the retrieved relational data for large language model (LLM) processing\nSends the user question and retrieved context to an Azure OpenAI REST endpoint for chat completions\nExtracts the response text from the LLM response\nWhich Transact-SQL function should you use to extract the response text?"}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "JSON_MODIFY(@response, '$.result')"}, {"id": "B", "text": "OPENJSON(@response)"}, {"id": "C", "text": "JSON_QUERY(@response, '$.choices')"}, {"id": "D", "text": "JSON_VALUE(@response, '$.choices[0].message.content')"}], "correct": ["D"], "type": "single", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "D\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ98 T03\nCorrect answer: JSON_VALUE(@response, '$.choices[0].message.content')\nExplanation:\nLa función correcta para extraer el texto de la respuesta del LLM es JSON_VALUE(@response, '$.choices\n[0].message.content').\nCuando se llama al endpoint de Azure OpenAI (chat completions) mediante\nsp_invoke_external_rest_endpoint, la respuesta llega en formato JSON. El texto generado por el modelo (el\ncontenido del mensaje del assistant) se encuentra en la ruta choices[0].message.content. JSON_VALUE\nestá diseñada precisamente para extraer un valor escalar (string) de un documento JSON a partir de una\nexpresión de ruta, que es exactamente lo que se necesita para obtener el texto de la respuesta del LLM.\nLas demás opciones no sirven para este propósito:\nJSON_MODIFY(@response, '$.result') no extrae nada: esta función se usa para modificar (actualizar,\ninsertar o eliminar) valores dentro de un JSON y devolver el JSON modificado, no para leer un valor.\nOPENJSON(@response) convierte un documento JSON en un conjunto de filas (tabla relacional). Es útil\npara descomponer arrays u objetos, pero no extrae directamente el texto escalar de la respuesta del\nmodelo.\nJSON_QUERY(@response, '$.choices') extrae un objeto o un array completo (en este caso devolvería el\narray choices). No devuelve el valor escalar del contenido del mensaje. Además, la ruta es incompleta: no\nllega hasta .message.content.\nPor tanto, solo JSON_VALUE con la ruta correcta al contenido del mensaje cumple el requisito de “extraer\nel texto de la respuesta del LLM”.\nReference:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/design-implement-rag-with-sql/5-generate-process-rag-\nresponses\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/json-value-transact-sql\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/functions/json-query-transact-sql"}], "points": 1}, {"id": 99, "pages": [229, 231], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p229-139"], "type": "dropdown", "template": "Table type: {{0}}\nIndex type: {{1}}", "pools": [["Rowstore disk-based", "SCHEMA_AND_DATA in-memory", "SCHEMA_ONLY in-memory"], ["HASH", "Nonclustered", "Nonclustered columnstore"]], "correct": [2, 0], "image": 139, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL managed instance that supports a gaming leaderboard API and contains a table\nnamed dbo.Leaderboard.\nYou plan to reduce write latency during peak events of dbo.Leaderboard.\nYou need to ensure that dbo.Leaderboard supports point lookups. The leaderboard information does NOT\nneed to persist after a restart.\nWhich type of table and index should you configure? To answer, select the appropriate options in the\nanswer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p230-140.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ99 / T03\n1. Respuesta correcta.\nTable type: SCHEMA_ONLY in-memory\nIndex type: HASH\n2. Explicación de por qué esa es la respuesta correcta y por qué las otras son\nincorrectas.\nEl escenario requiere:\nReducir la latencia de escritura durante picos de eventos (alto throughput de inserts/updates en un\nleaderboard de gaming).\nSoporte de point lookups (búsquedas por igualdad, típicas en un ranking: “dame la puntuación del jugador\nX”).\nQue la información no necesite persistir después de un reinicio.\nLas tablas memory-optimized (In-Memory OLTP) de Azure SQL Managed Instance están diseñadas\nexactamente para este tipo de cargas: eliminan latches y bloqueos tradicionales, mantienen los datos en\nmemoria y ofrecen latencias de escritura mucho más bajas.\nDentro de las tablas memory-optimized existen dos opciones de durabilidad:\nSCHEMA_AND_DATA: persiste tanto el esquema como los datos (sobreviven a reinicios).\nSCHEMA_ONLY: solo persiste el esquema; los datos se pierden en un reinicio o failover.\nComo el enunciado indica explícitamente que “the leaderboard information does NOT need to persist after a\nrestart”, la opción correcta es SCHEMA_ONLY in-memory.\nPara el índice:\nLos HASH indexes en tablas memory-optimized están optimizados para point lookups (predicado de\nigualdad en todas las columnas de la clave). Proporcionan un acceso de tiempo constante y son la elección\nrecomendada cuando solo se necesitan búsquedas por igualdad.\nUn índice Nonclustered (B-tree) es mejor para rangos y ordenaciones, no el caso principal aquí.\nUn Nonclustered columnstore está pensado para analítica, no para OLTP de alta concurrencia con point\nlookups.\nPor tanto, la combinación correcta es SCHEMA_ONLY in-memory + HASH.\nPor qué las demás opciones son incorrectas:\nRowstore disk-based: tablas tradicionales en disco. No aprovechan In-Memory OLTP, por lo que no\nreducen la latencia de escritura de forma significativa en picos.\nSCHEMA_AND_DATA in-memory: persistiría los datos, lo cual viola el requisito de no necesitar\npersistencia tras reinicio.\nNonclustered: útil para rangos, no óptimo para point lookups puros.\nNonclustered columnstore: orientado a cargas analíticas, no a un leaderboard OLTP de baja latencia.\n3. Referencias:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/in-memory-oltp/defining-durability-for-memory-\noptimized-objects\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/in-memory-oltp/indexes-for-memory-optimized-\ntables\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/managed-instance/in-memory-oltp-overview"}], "points": 2}, {"id": 100, "pages": [231, 233], "notes": [], "sourceImages": ["p232-141"], "type": "drag", "template": "USE [SalesDB];\nGO\n{{0}}\nGO\nEXEC sys.sp_cdc_enable_table\n @source_schema = N'dbo',\n @source_name = N'Articles',\n @role_name = NULL,\n @captured_column_list = {{1}},\n @supports_net_changes = {{2}};\nGO", "pools": [["0", "1", "EXEC sys.sp_cdc_disable_db;", "EXEC sys.sp_cdc_enable_db;", "N'ArticleId, Title, Body'", "N'Title, Body, LastModifiedUtc, EmbeddingVector'"], ["0", "1", "EXEC sys.sp_cdc_disable_db;", "EXEC sys.sp_cdc_enable_db;", "N'ArticleId, Title, Body'", "N'Title, Body, LastModifiedUtc, EmbeddingVector'"], ["0", "1", "EXEC sys.sp_cdc_disable_db;", "EXEC sys.sp_cdc_enable_db;", "N'ArticleId, Title, Body'", "N'Title, Body, LastModifiedUtc, EmbeddingVector'"]], "correct": [3, 4, 1], "image": 141, "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database named SalesDB. SalesDB contains a table named dbo.Articles. dbo.\nArticles contains the following columns:\n• ArticleId\n• Title\n• Body\n• LastModifiedUtc\n• EmbeddingVector\nYou have an application that generates embeddings from the concatenation of Title and Body and stores\nthe results in EmbeddingVector.\nYou plan to implement an incremental embedding maintenance method that will use change data capture\n(CDC) to update embeddings only for rows that change, without scanning the entire table.\nYou need to ensure that only the columns required to generate the embeddings are captured. The solution\nmust support querying net changes.\nHow should you complete the Transact-SQL script? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct\ntargets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar\nbetween panes or scroll to view content."}, {"kind": "transcription", "text": "[[INTERACTION]]"}], "solution": [{"kind": "image", "src": "solutions/p232-142.png", "alt": "Solución o justificación original del PDF"}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Section: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ100 T03\nLos tres huecos del drag and drop se rellenan así:\nPrimer hueco (después del primer GO):\nEXEC sys.sp_cdc_enable_db;\nSegundo hueco (@captured_column_list):\nN'ArticleId, Title, Body'\nTercer hueco (@supports_net_changes):\n1\nPor qué estos tres valores son los correctos:\nEXEC sys.sp_cdc_enable_db es el único valor que habilita CDC a nivel de base de datos, paso previo\nobligatorio antes de poder habilitarlo en una tabla.\nN'ArticleId, Title, Body' captura únicamente las columnas necesarias para generar el embedding (Title y\nBody) e incluye ArticleId porque es la clave primaria, requisito indispensable cuando se activa el soporte de\nnet changes.\nEl valor 1 activa el soporte de consultas de net changes, tal como exige el enunciado.\nPor qué las demás opciones no se usan:\n0 desactiva el soporte de net changes, lo que va en contra del requisito.\nEXEC sys.sp_cdc_disable_db deshabilita CDC en toda la base de datos, que es lo contrario de lo que se\nnecesita.\nN'Title, Body, LastModifiedUtc, EmbeddingVector' incluye columnas que no intervienen en la generación del\nembedding y, además, omite ArticleId, que es obligatorio para poder usar net changes.\nReferencias oficiales de Microsoft Learn:\nDocumentación de sys.sp_cdc_enable_table:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/system-stored-procedures/sys-sp-cdc-enable-\ntable-transact-sql\nCómo habilitar y deshabilitar Change Data Capture:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/track-changes/enable-and-disable-change-data-\ncapture-sql-server\nChange Data Capture en Azure SQL Database:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/change-data-capture-overview"}], "points": 3}, {"id": 101, "pages": [233, 234], "notes": [], "sourceImages": [], "body": [{"kind": "text", "text": "You have an Azure SQL database that contains a table named Tickets_Embeddings. Tickets_Embeddings\ncontains a VECTOR(1536) column.\nEmbeddings are generated by using text-embedding-ada-002 and the /openai/v1/embeddings endpoint.\nAfter a review of the current environment, the following changes are requested:\n- The AI architect has recommended switching to text-embedding-3-small.\n- The security team requires authentication by using Microsoft Entra managed identities only. Storing API\nkeys in an application is prohibited.\nWhich two actions should you include in the solution? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point."}], "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Alter the database tables."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Regenerate all the embeddings."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Change the endpoint."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Change the primary keys for the tickets."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Add a firewall rule."}], "correct": ["B", "C"], "type": "multiple", "solution": [{"kind": "text", "text": "BC\nSection: (none)\nExplanation/Reference:\nQ101 T03\n---\nRespuesta Correcta:\nRegenerate all the embeddings.\nChange the endpoint.\n---\nExplanation:\nRegenerate all the embeddings. Los vectores generados por modelos de embeddings distintos pertenecen\na espacios vectoriales diferentes y no son comparables entre sí. Si se cambia de text-embedding-ada-002\na text-embedding-3-small, hay que regenerar todos los embeddings ya almacenados. De lo contrario, la\nbúsqueda de similitud (por ejemplo con VECTOR_DISTANCE) mezclaría vectores de dos modelos distintos\ny devolvería resultados incoherentes.\nChange the endpoint. Al cambiar de modelo hay que apuntar a la nueva implementación (deployment) de\ntext-embedding-3-small en Azure OpenAI, y por tanto cambiar el endpoint de embeddings. Además, el\nrequisito de seguridad obliga a autenticarse solo con una identidad administrada de Microsoft Entra (sin\nclaves de API), por lo que el modelo externo se define con la URL del nuevo endpoint y una credencial de\námbito de base de datos de tipo Managed Identity.\nIncorrectas\nAlter the database tables. text-embedding-3-small devuelve por defecto vectores de 1536 dimensiones, las\nmismas que text-embedding-ada-002, por lo que la columna VECTOR(1536) sigue siendo válida y no hace\nfalta modificar las tablas.\nChange the primary keys for the tickets. Las claves primarias de los tickets no tienen relación con el modelo\nde embeddings ni con el método de autenticación, así que cambiarlas no aporta nada a la solución.\nAdd a firewall rule. Una regla de firewall controla el acceso de red al servidor, pero no cambia el modelo de\nembeddings ni el método de autenticación. No cumple ninguno de los dos requisitos.\nReferencias:\nMicrosoft Learn - Azure OpenAI embeddings (modelos text-embedding-3-small y ada-002, dimensiones):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/openai/concepts/understand-embeddings\nMicrosoft Learn - CREATE EXTERNAL MODEL (Transact-SQL), con endpoint y credencial Managed\nIdentity:\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/create-external-model-transact-sql\nMicrosoft Learn - Vector data type (Transact-SQL):\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/data-types/vector-data-type"}], "points": 1}]};